losed : closedBall z₀ r ⊆ Ω` already implies it, while the conclusion is entirely substantive.\n\n**An unused hypothesis makes a theorem *stronger*, not vacuous.** I had generalised from a symptom to a test. That's the third time tonight I've over-generalised from one case — but the first time I caught it myself, in the same turn, rather than having the scribe catch it.\n\nThe corrected rule is now in the RESUME doc: **test the conclusion, not the hypotheses.** Find the definitions the conclusion mentions, unfold them, ask whether any is a bare constant or a `True`-typed field. a95J03 fails at that step (`windingNumber := 0`); a93J07 passes (every subject is a genuine analytic object). Unused parameters are a hint to look, never the finding.\n\nWorth being plain about the cost of the wrong version: it would have false-positived on every redundantly-stated theorem in the corpus *and* still missed a vacuous one whose hypotheses happened to be used. A check that fires on the symptom rather than the cause is worse than no check, because it manufactures work while leaving the hole open.\n\n**State: 43 resolved, 22 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a93A04 attempt 2, a94A03). Usage 16%. Scribe 34 will be owed for a93J07, a94A01, and whatever the two in-flight runs return.Chain healthy — both runners busy, both jobs running, 2 real parks. But **heartbeat count is zero**, so arming one per the rule. Then using the quiet wake to bring the summary current — it still says 24 closed:Heartbeat fired on a **healthy** chain — second quiet wake. Both runners busy, both jobs running, heartbeat count zero so I armed exactly one. Four firings now: three caught a stopped chain, one reported health correctly. Worth noting because a dead-man switch that only ever fires on failure tells you nothing about its false-positive rate; this one has now been observed in both states.\n\nI used the wake to **bring the overnight summary current** — it still said twenty-four closed when the count is thirty.\n\nThree substantive edits:\n\n**I removed the pinned corpus figure entirely**, replacing it with \"take it when nothing is in flight — see trap 1.\" A static clean/sorries number in a live document is exactly the working-tree trap I documented and then fell into once already tonight. Better to make the reader take the measurement than to hand them a stale one.\n\n**Rewrote the taxonomy to scribe 33's final structure** — which is neither shape I proposed. One umbrella (*\"missing semantic-domain guards admit degenerate witnesses\"*) with three mechanisms kept **separate because the repairs differ**, laid out as a table so the repair sits next to the mechanism. Constant placeholder and pre-formal elaboration failure sit *outside* the umbrella entirely.\n\n**Gave the statement preflight its own section as the most transferable thing tonight produced** — three checks, none needing a proof attempt, each of which caught a real defect. And I put the check I got **wrong** right next to it, because that half is more instructive: \"unused parameter ⇒ vacuity\" was a symptom mistaken for a test, and a93J07 disproved it within the hour. A reader gets the rule and its known false form together.\n\n**Decisions waiting for you went from three to nine rows**, each carrying `:decision-owner \"joe\"`, the mechanism, the proposed repair, and whether the defect is proved or argued — plus a95J03 flagged `:counts-toward-clean :disputed`. Several offer two or three materially different repairs.\n\nThat's the honest shape of the night: **the loop closed thirty problems and surfaced nine statement-level decisions it deliberately did not make.**a94A03 gives two notable results: it **used the memory I've amended twice tonight** (in its original forward direction), and it **corrected the same stale \"Lp translation continuity\" note that a03J03 corrected** — the second instance of the pessimistic-scaffold class. Dispatching first:A third file carries it. Checking whether it's the same stale claim:a95A02's note is a **different** claim — about `AbsolutelyContinuousOnInterval` and the integral representation being \"only partially available.\" I have no evidence it's wrong, and assuming so would be the over-generalisation I've made three times tonight. But the class has been wrong twice, so the calibrated action is to flag it for verification, not to assert it's stale:**a94A03: second instance of the same false pessimistic scaffold note — that's propagation, not coincidence.**\n\nThe file claimed *\"Mathlib does not have Young's convolution inequality or Lp translation [continuity]\"*; the runner replaced it with the accurate statement that `Continuous.compMeasurePreservingLp` provides translation continuity. **a03J03 carried the identical false claim earlier tonight, and correcting it there later unblocked the close.** This defect class costs invisibly: a runner that believes the note simply avoids a viable route, and no gate ever fires.\n\n**I checked how widespread it is, and then deliberately didn't over-claim.** a95A02 carries a third partial-support note — but about `AbsolutelyContinuousOnInterval`, a *different* claim that may well be accurate. I did **not** mark it stale. I added a `:scaffold-note-caution` asking the next runner to verify before accepting, stating explicitly it isn't an assertion the note is wrong. Two instances of a class is enough to warrant checking the third; it isn't enough to convict it.\n\n**And the memory I amended twice tonight was used in its original forward direction within hours.** `derive-integrable-from-nonzero-bochner-integral` derived kernel integrability *from* the nonzero integral, while my amendments extended it to the reverse — using non-integrability to refute statements. The memory is now load-bearing in both directions, which is a better outcome than either the original or a split would have produced.\n\nIt also reported a **precise boundary** on a second memory rather than a bare verdict: `reduce-probability-kernel-L2-contraction-to-young` correctly identifies the missing Young estimate but is scoped to p = 2, while this target needs general finite p — as is the repository's own `YoungL2.lean`. A memory reported as *accurate-but-too-narrow* is more useful than a hit or a miss, because it names the extension that would make it apply.\n\nThe remaining obstruction is a genuine Mathlib gap: the integral Minkowski/Young step for general finite p, with negative search results carried forward and revision-scoped.\n\n**State: 43 resolved, 22 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a93A04 attempt 2, a94A04). Usage 16%.**a93A04 closed — and the runner did the opposite of what I instructed.** I told it that if the named converse was confirmed absent, mark the row as a Mathlib frontier. Instead it **proved the converse itself** — `sum_edist_le_eVariationOn_of_mem_disjWithin`, added as a reusable lemma. That's thirty-one. Dispatching first:**a93A04 closed axiom-clean — thirty-one problems — and the runner got there by contradicting my instruction.**\n\nAttempt 1 named the missing Mathlib converse precisely. I recorded it as a frontier and told the next runner, in the park payload, to mark the row `:blocked-mathlib-frontier` if the converse was confirmed absent — citing rouche as the cautionary case against re-dispatching. **It built the lemma instead, in one run**: filter degenerate intervals, sort the active subtype by left endpoint via `Tuple.sort`/`Tuple.monotone_sort` through an arbitrary `Fin` equivalence, then use pairwise disjointness to embed the increments in a single monotone partition. All six declarations axiom-clean.\n\n**The lesson is mine and it's a real one: a named Mathlib gap is not automatically a blocker.** I was one instruction away from marking a *constructible* lemma as a permanent frontier. And my own cited precedent undercut me — `rouche-root-count-transfer` had **three** independent attempts before it was marked; a93A04 had one. Naming a gap precisely is what makes it **attemptable**, not what makes it permanent. That rule is now in the RESUME doc.\n\nTwo consequences worth noting. The new lemma is **reusable** beyond this problem — ordering a disjoint interval family into a variation partition is generic. And **scribe 33 had already drafted a frontier memory for exactly this gap**, which is now resolved by *construction* rather than discovery; that memory needs amending to `:resolved` with the lemma named, the same shape as the two a01A07 frontier memories earlier tonight. Owed to scribe 34.\n\nAlso: third time tonight that carried-forward negative results saved a search arm.\n\n**State: 44 resolved, 21 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a94A04, a94A03 attempt 2). Usage 16%.a94A04: statements false for arbitrary μ, via a **sharper structure than plain vacuity**. I checked the arithmetic: with μ = ∑δ_{xₘ}, xₘ = 1−1/(m+2), f(xₘ) = 1/(m+1) — then ∑1/(m+1)² converges so f ∈ L², but xₘⁿf(xₘ) ≥ 2⁻ⁿ/(m+1) is harmonic-divergent, so every monomial integral is junk-zero. **And p(X)=1−X gives p(xₘ)f(xₘ) = 1/((m+1)(m+2)), which is genuinely integrable with telescoping integral 1 ≠ 0** — that's a real counterexample, not just a vacuous hypothesis. Dispatching first:Confirmed by reading both: they quantify over **arbitrary `μ : Measure ℝ` with no finiteness constraint**, and neither assumes integrability of the monomial products. The statement preflight would have caught this — hypotheses mention integrals with nothing guaranteeing they're defined.**a94A04: both targets false, and the counterexample is sharper than vacuity.**\n\nBoth statements quantify over an **arbitrary `μ : Measure ℝ` with no finiteness constraint** and assume no integrability — I read them to confirm rather than take the report. The counterexample uses an atomic measure: μ = ∑δ at xₘ = 1−1/(m+2), f(xₘ) = 1/(m+1). Then f ∈ L² since ∑1/(m+1)² converges, but xₘⁿf(xₘ) ≥ 2⁻ⁿ/(m+1) is harmonic-divergent, so every monomial integral is junk-zero while f is nonzero at every atom.\n\n**The second half is a genuinely stronger form of evidence, and I've folded it into the preflight.** For `orthogonal_to_polynomials`, taking p(X) = 1−X gives p(xₘ)f(xₘ) = 1/((m+1)(m+2)) — **genuinely integrable, telescoping to 1, not 0**. So the conclusion is refuted on an instance where the integral *is* defined. The hypothesis is vacuous, but the falsity is real rather than merely unconstrained.\n\nThat's now step 3 of the statement preflight: **when a hypothesis is found vacuous, ask whether some instance of the conclusion is computable and false — that upgrades \"vacuous\" to \"refuted.\"** It's a meaningful distinction for the morning's decisions: a vacuous statement might be salvaged by adding a hypothesis, but a refuted one is wrong on its own terms.\n\nOne more thing the row records carefully: **the repairs differ per target.** The standalone polynomial lemma needs integrability folded into the moment hypothesis; the main theorem needs finite measure on [0,1], after which L² ⊆ L¹ and the intended Stone–Weierstrass proof works. One mechanism, two repairs — exactly why scribe 33 insisted the mechanisms stay separate under the umbrella.\n\n**State: 44 resolved, 20 untouched, 16 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a94A03 attempt 2, a94A06). Usage 16%. Scribe 34 owed over five runs.a94A03's second attempt found something actionable: **`a95J08` contains the desired general Young architecture but still has four sorries** — a cross-problem dependency. And it used a memory to *prevent* a misuse rather than to build. Dispatching first:Verified — **a95J08 does contain `young_convolution_inequality` and `convolution_memLp`**, exactly the architecture a94A03 is blocked on, with 4 sorries. The cross-problem dependency is real, so dispatching out of priority order was justified.**First queue reorder driven by a runner-reported cross-problem dependency — and I verified it before acting.**\n\na94A03's runner reported that a95J08 holds the general Young architecture it's blocked on. I checked: a95J08 declares `young_convolution_inequality` and `convolution_memLp`, with 4 sorries. Real. So I dispatched a95J08 **deliberately out of priority order** and recorded the link on both rows — `:depends-on-problem \"a95J08\"` on one, `:unblocks-problem \"a94A03\"` on the other — so it survives whoever picks them up. Closing a95J08 is worth more than its own sorry count suggests, and nothing in the priority sort could have known that.\n\n**a94A03 attempt 2 is another headline-zero run with three declarations newly usable** — including the fixed-kernel normalisation `gaussConv_eq_integral_unitKernel`. Six of eight declarations in that file are now axiom-clean; only the two blocked targets carry `sorryAx`.\n\n**And a memory was used to prevent a mistake rather than to build something.** `separate-compact-bump-convergence-from-full-support-poisson-kernels` stopped the runner applying compactly-supported bump convergence to the full-support Gaussian. Nothing was proved with it; something wrong was not done. Counting that as a \"use\" is right, but a memory that averts an error and one that carries a proof are different goods — and `:memory-use/use-mode` is currently the only place that distinction lives. If metric 3 is meant to measure whether the corpus helps, it should probably separate those.\n\nIt also **considered then declined** a memory drafted tonight from a03J03, on the precise ground that it's a related density technique that doesn't supply Young's inequality. The corpus is surfacing its own fresh memories and judging them on what they actually provide rather than topical similarity.\n\n**State: 44 resolved, 20 untouched, 16 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a94A06, a95J08). Usage 16%.**a94A06 closed — both declarations axiom-clean, sorries 2 → 0. Thirty-two.** Dispatching a proving row and **scribe 34**, which is at six runs and shouldn't grow further:The selector picked a94A03 — but that's the row **blocked on a95J08, which is running right now**. Dispatching it would waste a slot on a known blocker. Using the dependency I just recorded and skipping to the next:**a94A06 closed axiom-clean — thirty-two problems since yesterday morning.** All ten declarations in the file re-elaborate clean with zero signature changes; the largest fully-clean file of the night.\n\n**One observation from it is worth more than the close itself.** The proof used `integral_Ioi_of_hasDerivAt_of_nonneg'`, which **produces integrability as an output** — deriving it from a monotone antiderivative with a finite limit rather than demanding it as a hypothesis.\n\nThat's the structural opposite of the junk-value family that broke six problems tonight. a01A10, a01J01, a02J01, a02J03, a02J07 and a94A04 all *assumed* an integral was well-behaved and were satisfied vacuously when it wasn't. A scaffold written around FTC-style lemmas that **establish** integrability wouldn't have been vulnerable to that family at all. That's a design observation about how these problems should be stated, not just a fact about one proof — and it's the closest thing tonight has produced to a *preventive* answer rather than a diagnostic one.\n\n**The dependency I recorded an hour ago immediately earned its keep.** The selector's next pick was a94A03 — blocked on a95J08, currently in flight. Dispatching it would have burned a slot re-attempting a known blocker. I added a skip rule: never dispatch a row whose `:depends-on-problem` is running. It fired on its first use, which is the point of recording a link the priority sort can't see.\n\n**Scribe 34 is dispatched** over six runs, asking it to judge the frontier memory it drafted in pass 33 that was **resolved by construction** hours later — and whether my \"named gap = blocker\" instruction is capturable or *just operator error