hat's now two use-modes the benchmark scores identically with \"a memory carried the proof.\" A memory that stops a runner from a wrong turn and one that supplies the proof are both wins, but they're different wins, and metric 3 currently flattens them.\n\n**State: 45 resolved, 19 untouched, 16 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Usage 17%.Scribe 34 answered all three and confirmed each. Notably on (1): my \"named gap = blocker\" error **is** capturable — as a frontier-lifecycle rule: *\"library absence identifies a construction target, not automatically a durable blocker; record attempt history before making that transition.\"* And it caught the a94A06 receipt I'd omitted from the brief — second time it has repaired its own inputs.\n\nBoth runners are busy, so processing the scribe:**Zero real parks — the chain has stopped again.** Caught it by checking at turn end rather than waiting for the heartbeat. Checking what happened to the two in-flight jobs:Both completed while I was processing the scribe — their results are pending delivery and the chain has no park. **Dispatching two immediately**, both runners idle:Scribe 34 landed — but the more important thing is what I found checking park state at the end of the turn.\n\n**Pass 34 confirmed all three questions.** On the frontier one it declined the exit I offered (\"or is it just operator error\"): my mistake **is** capturable, as a **frontier-lifecycle rule** — *library absence identifies a construction target, not automatically a durable blocker; record attempt history before making that transition*. That's more useful than either \"claude-9 erred\" or a vague caution, because it names the missing datum: rouche had three failed attempts before marking, a93A04 had one.\n\nIt also accepted **both** new use-modes — `:prevented-misapplication` and `:scope-boundary-confirmed` — and I applied four more amendments, **twenty tonight**, all append-only with proposer ≠ applier. Three drafts promoted and reviewed into existing patterns. It again repaired its own brief, catching the a94A06 receipt I'd omitted.\n\n**Then: the chain had stopped a fourth time, and the cause was new.** a94J04 and a94J07 both completed *while I was processing the scribe*. Their parks were consumed by completion, the long promote-and-amend block occupied the turn, and I ended with zero real parks.\n\n**Dispatch-first protects against ending a turn without dispatching. It does not protect against a turn long enough for the in-flight work to finish inside it.** The rule that follows: after any long processing block, re-check whether the chain still has a real park before finishing.\n\nI caught this myself rather than being rescued by the heartbeat — the first time. Dispatched two immediately and parked.\n\n**And two results are unprocessed.** a94J04 and a94J07 completed but were never verified or receipted; their bellbacks haven't arrived as turns. I've named both job ids in the new park payload with an instruction to collect them via the jobs endpoint first. A completed job whose result was never processed is the quietest way for work to vanish here — nothing else in the loop would have surfaced it.\n\n**State: 45 resolved, 17 untouched, 16 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight or pending.** Usage 17%.a94J04 surfaced something operationally important: **`YoungL2.lean` contains an axiom-clean `convolution_L2_contraction_of_probability_kernel`, but it sits outside all configured Lake libraries and cannot be imported.** Proved work that's unreachable by build configuration. And it's the *third* file tonight with a stale pessimistic note. Verifying both:Confirmed: **`./YoungL2.lean` at the repo root, `convolution_L2_contraction_of_probability_kernel` at line 200, zero sorries — fully proved and unreachable.** And the lakefile comment mentions a `lean_lib` added on 2026-07-30. Looking properly:**This has explicit precedent in the lakefile itself.** The comment documents that `ConstructionTargets` had exactly this defect until 2026-07-30 — \"the proved lemmas were therefore unreachable from the problems they were built for\" — and a `lean_lib` was added to fix it. **`YoungL2.lean` has the same problem and was missed.** Four declarations, zero sorries, unreachable. Fixing it with a backup and a build test:**The fix works.** `lake build YoungL2` succeeds and `import YoungL2` now resolves — the L² contraction is importable with exactly the signature a94J04 needs. Checking I haven't broken anything else:**a94J04's most valuable output isn't a proof — it's a build defect that made proved work unreachable, and I've fixed it.**\n\nThe runner reported that `YoungL2.lean` holds an axiom-clean `convolution_L2_contraction_of_probability_kernel` but sits \"outside all configured Lake libraries.\" I verified: it's at the repo root, **4 declarations, zero sorries, no `[[lean_lib]]` entry** — so `import YoungL2` failed with \"unknown module prefix.\"\n\n**The lakefile itself documents this exact defect class.** A comment records that `ConstructionTargets` had no `lean_lib` until 2026-07-30, so *\"the proved lemmas were therefore unreachable from the problems they were built for.\"* YoungL2 had the identical defect and was missed by that fix.\n\nFixed and committed (`7958c53`), with verification rather than assumption: `lake build YoungL2` succeeds; `import YoungL2` resolves and `#check` returns the expected signature; a93J06 still elaborates with zero errors; `ConstructionTargets` still builds. Lakefile backed up first.\n\n**This plausibly unblocks three rows** that have all been circling Young-type estimates tonight — a94J04 needs exactly this L² contraction, a95J08 is proving the general finite-p version and can now reference the proved p=2 case, and a94A03's own note says YoungL2 covers only p=2, which is now an *importable* p=2 rather than an inaccessible one.\n\nIt's worth naming the shape: **a proved, axiom-clean lemma was invisible to every problem that needed it, and no gate we run would ever have reported it.** Sorry counts, axiom censuses and statement diffs all pass on unreachable code. The only thing that surfaced it was a runner trying to import it — the same way the pessimistic scaffold notes only surfaced when someone tried the discouraged route.\n\nAlso: a94J04 closed four declarations axiom-clean (6 → 3 sorries), used two memories directly, and corrected a **third** stale note claiming Mathlib lacks something it has.\n\n**State: 45 resolved, 17 untouched, 16 blocked-and-diagnosed.** a94J07's result is still unprocessed — I'll take that next.**a94J07 closed — all four declarations axiom-clean, sorries 4 → 0. Thirty-three.** And it did something notable: the declarations are *named* `rouche_*`, but it **replaced the stale Rouché route with a direct Lipschitz perturbation argument** — `cexp` is exp(1)-Lipschitz, z³ is 3-Lipschitz, and exp(1)+3 < 8 so the linear term forces injectivity.**a94J07 closed axiom-clean — thirty-three problems since yesterday morning.** All five declarations clean, zero signature changes.\n\n**The interesting part is how it closed.** The runner abandoned the scaffold's Rouché route entirely for a direct Lipschitz perturbation argument: on the unit disk `cexp` is exp(1)-Lipschitz and z↦z³ is 3-Lipschitz, and since exp(1)+3 < 8 the linear term dominates and forces injectivity.\n\n**The scaffold's *naming* had baked in the route assumption** — `rouche_deriv_bound` and `rouche_f_bound` are now proved without Rouché, and the names still say otherwise. That's a subtler form of the stale-guidance class than the three prose notes tonight: a comment can be corrected, but a **name** can't be without changing the statement. The runner was right to leave them alone.\n\n**And this sharpens the Rouché frontier finding without overturning it.** `rouche-root-count-transfer` remains a genuine Mathlib frontier on three independent confirmations — that stands. But a problem *named* for Rouché just closed without it, so **the frontier blocks the route, not necessarily every scaffold that assumed that route.**\n\nWhether other Rouché-blocked rows have non-Rouché routes available is worth checking — but I've explicitly recorded that as a hypothesis for the morning, **not** a finding. I've over-generalised three times tonight and been corrected each time; one counterexample to \"Rouché-named ⇒ Rouché-needed\" doesn't license re-opening the frontier row.\n\nBoth unprocessed results are now cleared. **State: 46 resolved, 17 untouched, 16 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 dispatched.** Chain healthy — one real park awaiting two jobs, heartbeat behind it. Usage 17%.**Fourth instance confirmed — the class is systemic.** The runner found the file's stated obstruction was stale: current Mathlib has `AbsolutelyContinuousOnInterval.integral_deriv_eq_sub`, `.intervalIntegrable_deriv`, and `Integrable.tendsto_setIntegral_nhds_zero`. My caution flagged it for verification without asserting it was wrong — and it was wrong. Dispatching first:Decisive — the diff shows **multiple** stale claims removed from the same file: *\"a deep result that is only partially [available]\"* → *\"Current Mathlib gives both the ε-δ definition and the integral representation\"*, plus *\"This bridge is not fully available in Mathlib\"*. Three new declarations, zero changed, zero removed.**Fourth confirmed instance — the stale-pessimistic-note class is systemic.** a95A02's header claimed the AC-to-integral-representation bridge was *\"a deep result only partially available in Mathlib\"* and *\"not fully available.\"* Current Mathlib has all three needed lemmas. Multiple stale claims in one file, all removed.\n\nWith a03J03, a94A03 and a94J04: **four for four — every partial-support note examined tonight was false.**\n\n**The calibration matters more than the finding.** Two hours ago I added a `:scaffold-note-caution` to this row explicitly stating it was *not* a claim the note was wrong — only that the class had been wrong twice and deserved verification. It turned out wrong. Had I marked it stale on suspicion I'd have been right by luck and wrong as a method. Two instances justified asking; four make it a standing check. That's the distinction I've been getting wrong all night in the other direction — over-generalising from one or two cases — and this is the first time I set the threshold correctly in advance.\n\n**The runner also pre-empted the consolidation concern unprompted**, reporting that \"none were relocated through another sorry-bearing declaration.\" That's exactly the metric-integrity failure I found on a01A07 attempt 1 — headline movement with zero genuine discharge — now appearing spontaneously in a runner's self-report rather than being caught by my review.\n\nThree new axiom-clean declarations, headline delta zero, statements unaltered. Both non-component arms genuinely empty: Zulip returned only unrelated Lusin–Souslin material, and an arXiv manifest query for \"Lusin condition N\" returned no rows — recorded so the next attempt doesn't repeat them.\n\n**State: 46 resolved, 16 untouched, 17 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 18%. Scribe 35 is owed over six runs and I'll dispatch it next turn.**a94J08 closed — sorries 2 → 0, all four declarations axiom-clean. Thirty-four.** The construction is elegant: `quadraticQuotient f z = if z = 0 then 0 else z²/f z`, whose lower bound gives ‖·‖ ≤ ‖z‖^(1/2); it extends holomorphically at zero, and Cauchy's estimate at radius R forces every derivative to vanish as R → ∞.\n\nDispatching a row and **scribe 35**, which is at six runs:The park missed scribe 35 — its id came back in the same block. Adding a park for it, then verifying a94J08:**a94J08 closed axiom-clean — thirty-four problems since yesterday morning.** All nine declarations in the file clean, 5 added / 0 changed / 0 removed.\n\nThe construction is worth keeping: `quadraticQuotient f z = if z = 0 then 0 else z²/f z`. The assumed lower bound gives ‖·‖ ≤ ‖z‖^(1/2); it extends holomorphically across zero by removable singularity; and Cauchy's estimate at radius R forces every derivative to vanish as R → ∞. That's a **sublinear-growth form of Liouville** assembled from removable singularity plus Cauchy estimate — the standard bounded-entire statement doesn't apply to a function only known to grow like ‖z‖^(1/2).\n\nTwo smaller notes. Arms (b) and (c) were correctly **skipped** because arm (a) supplied a complete route — I recorded that as a case where *not* searching was right, since I've been counting empty arms all night and \"searched and found nothing\" differs from \"correctly didn't search\" for the payoff ledger.\n\nAnd I made the batching error again: composed the park in the same command block as the scribe dispatch, so scribe 35's job id wasn't in the `awaiting` list. Caught it on read-back and added a second park. **Third occurrence tonight** — the rule is written in the RESUME doc and I keep violating it when dispatch-and-park *feel* like one action. They aren't, and knowing the rule clearly isn't sufficient; I should be composing the park from a variable I read back rather than typing it alongside.\n\n**Scribe 35 is dispatched over seven runs**, with the build-config defect as the explicit priority: whether \"check whether the lemma already exists in-repo but off the module path\" is capturable, whether the 4/4 stale-note class yields a *check* or a *calibration* or both, and whether \"scaffold naming can encode a stale route\" is separable from the prose-note class.\n\n**State: 47 resolved, 15 untouched, 17 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 18%.Scribe 35 sharpened all three. Most usefully on (1): YoungL2 reachability isn't a new memory but a **second, stronger instance of an existing construction-delivery memory** — amend it with explicit delivery states *through consumer import and field use*. That's better than my framing, which treated it as novel.\n\nBoth runners busy, so processing the scribe:Two targets stand out: