holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:402:   :review "THE ANSWER IS NOT SAFE TO RUN, which is the answer I asked for and the right one. Verified the load-bearing claim myself rather than taking it: war_machine.clj:4844-4854 POSTs /api/alpha/portfolio/step from inside judge, UNCONDITIONALLY -- wrapped in try/catch, which swallows the error but does not prevent the call, and carrying {\"emit-evidence\":false}, which limits what it emits but still runs an AIF step on the futon3c portfolio engine. A validation run of 20 ticks would step that engine 20 times. ONE DETAIL THE REPORT DID NOT DRAW, and it makes the fix easy: a read-only path already exists in the same file. scan-portfolio (war_machine.clj:2202-2212) takes a step? parameter DEFAULTING TO FALSE and documents GET /api/alpha/portfolio/state as the non-stepping call. The judge code does not use it; it POSTs directly. So the capability is there and unused at this site. THE 14/4 SPLIT IS HONEST AND THE SHAPE MATTERS: most of the effects without a disabling mechanism are full-loop only -- agency bells that let agents edit and commit, status mutation, phase and run receipt files, cancellation POSTs -- and the diagnostic entrypoint does not enter the full loop. Confirmed: scripts/futon2/run_tick_once.clj contains no reference to full_loop_runner. So the diagnostic path has exactly ONE unsafe actuator, and it is the portfolio POST. R2 STAYS BLOCKED. Opened R5 to suppress the POST on the diagnostic path; RUN2 unblocks when that lands and I have read it, not before. codex-21 answering NOT SAFE rather than producing a confident command is the outcome the packet was written to make possible, and it is worth saying that a discovery which stops the campaign is a successful discovery." :assigned "2026-09-01, dispatched to codex-21. Discovery only, no run."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:408:   :review "THE SUPPRESSION IS STRUCTURAL, not a flag someone checks: portfolio-step-for-judge is (if-not step? {absence} (try POST ...)), so with step? false the POST is in an unreachable branch. Option :step-portfolio?, default TRUE, so production is unchanged; run_tick_once.clj passes false. THE INJECTION TEST IS REAL and does what the packet demanded: with-redefs on http/post to a fn that THROWS, then assert the result equals {:status :absent :reason :portfolio-step-suppressed}. If the POST were reached the throw would be swallowed by the historical catch-all into nil, and nil is not the absence map, so the test fails. That is a proof of no-call rather than an inspection of a branch. THE ABSENCE IS HANDLED, NOT JUST PRODUCED -- I checked the consumers rather than the producer. mission-priorities reads it through get-in with [] defaults (war_machine.clj:4284-4285), so it degrades exactly as the old nil did; and the :portfolio-recommendation cond at :4924-4931 tests (= :absent (:status portfolio-step)) FIRST and propagates the absence, so a suppressed tick records why the recommendation is missing instead of shaping {:action nil :recommendation nil} that reads like a present-but-empty answer. Suppressed and failed are also distinguishable downstream: absence map versus nil. ONE REVIEW FINDING, fixed: THE CROSS-VERSION CONTROL DECAYED. It began as HEAD~, which the shared checkout broke when an unrelated commit landed between; codex-20 fixed that by anchoring to the parent of the LATEST commit touching war_machine.clj. That survives concurrency but rots -- after the next edit to this file that parent already contains R5 change, so the control compares R5 default path with itself, and the claim it backs quietly turns from the default preserves pre-suppression behaviour into the last commit changed nothing. Both are checks; only one is the claim R5 makes. Pinned to e7a9bb6^ with the reasoning in the def. A control for a fixed claim gets a fixed anchor. R2 IS NOW UNBLOCKED -- on my having READ the suppression, which is what the row required, not on a report saying it is fixed." :assigned "2026-09-01, dispatched to codex-20, after R1 found the one unsafe actuator on the diagnostic path."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:414:   :review "I RAN THE ENUMERATION MYSELF THIS TIME rather than checking the pointers I was handed, which is what went wrong at R1. grep over war_machine.clj, full_loop_runner.clj and enact.clj: FOUR http/post in war_machine (:2179 mission-detail, now suppressed; :2222 scan-portfolio, not reached because generate-war-machine calls the 0-arity which passes false; :2264 judge, suppressed by RUN5; :4099 the /eval fallback, now suppressed), FOUR in full_loop_runner (:107, :413, :586, :601 -- not reachable from run_tick_once), NONE in enact.clj. codex-21 list matches mine and it found no fifth POST. I ALSO WIDENED THE AXIS, because HTTP was the axis I was GIVEN and effects are the axis that matters. git: every call is `log`, read-only, no commit/push/add anywhere. Subprocess: `bb build-invariant-state-projection.bb --wm-status` spawns for real; its only writes go to fs/create-temp-dir, so it is harmless. THE DELIVERED WHOLE-TICK TEST IS NOT WHOLE-TICK. It redefines wm/generate-war-machine with a stub that calls the two producers of interest, so the real generator -- about twenty scans, the real judge, the real invariant inventory -- never runs. It therefore cannot catch a site nobody thought of, which is the only kind that matters. Kept, because it pins the two options cheaply, but it does not carry the acceptance. SO I WROTE THE REAL ONE and ran it: r6_zero_post_preflight.clj intercepts http/post and spit, leaves http/get alone so the tick sees real data, and runs tick/run-tick-once end to end. RESULT: a real diagnostic tick completes in 34 seconds with ZERO POSTS ATTEMPTED. That is the acceptance met on the actual path rather than on a stub. AND IT FOUND A WRITE NOBODY ENUMERATED: a tick also writes data/wm-trace/.lane-futility-index.edn -- persistent state in our own repo, carried forward between ticks, absent from RUN1 fourteen-effect audit and from my own review of it. It is a file in our tree rather than a call to another service, so it is far lower risk, but a validation run mutates it and RUN2 must say so rather than discover it afterwards. TWO NUMBERS R2 NEEDS AND DID NOT HAVE: 34 seconds per tick, so about 11 minutes for 20 ticks; and three files written per tick -- the trace record, the run receipt, and the lane futility index."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:422:   :evidence "futon2 (this commit): r6_zero_post_preflight.clj. Three pre-flight runs to date: 34 s, 81 s (claude-1), 28.5 s -- per-tick wall time varies enough that R2 must record it rather than assume it."}
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:433:   :review "Records read myself with edn/read in a loop. VERIFIED: 20 records; :controller-score in 20/20; :prediction-mean in 20/20; :f-pi-by-candidate-id in 20/20, one :absent envelope at tick 1 (cold start, correct) and 19 :present. THE RANK-PROXY LIMIT IS LIFTED. Every rank measurement in this campaign -- the 82-of-110 reshuffle, the argmax claims, the V6 arms -- was over a G-total proxy, because the July fields carried :G-total but not the score rank-actions sorted on. These records carry it. Those measurements are RE-RUNNABLE AT THE MACHINE OWN GRAIN and should be re-run before any of them is quoted again. ONE DEFECT THAT BLOCKS R3: :wm/route IS ABSENT FROM ALL 20 TRACE FORMS. The tick computes the route -- the receipt carries :route and :route-verdict -- but trace-record does not persist it, and the receipt is one file per DATE overwritten each tick, so nineteen routes were lost and only tick 20 survives. Conformance cannot be checked against a run whose routes were not kept. Opened RUN10. AND THE FINDING, from the one route we have. Tick 20: R20->R12, R12->R2, R2->R7, R7->R3, R3->R8, R8->R5, R5->R6, R6->R14, R14->TRACE. Checked against control-map-edges.edn -- against the registry :decisions :retires set, NOT the list in the campaign brief, which I nearly used and which does NOT match the registry. TWO HOPS TRAVERSE RETIRED EDGES: R2->R7, retired by :r2-r7-superseded-by-eps on the grounds that R7 imports the prediction errors and not the observation; and R5->R6. CORRECTED SAME DAY after claude-1 pushed on the grain question: my first pass checked :edges ONLY and missed :route-measured-drawn, so I reported five hops as unmapped. NONE ARE. All nine are accounted for -- five route-measured (R20->R12, R12->R2, R3->R8, R6->R14, R14->TRACE), two drawn (R7->R3, R8->R5), two retired and discussed below. ALSO ESTABLISHED so nobody re-derives it: seven of the eight retired pairs are still :status :drawn in :edges, and that is BY DESIGN -- gen_live_topology.bb:133 says a retired edge is drawn in a distinct class, not removed. I was one step from reporting the registry as self-contradictory. :edges is the canvas; :decisions are applied on top. SO NEITHER TRAVERSAL IS WHAT I FIRST THOUGHT. R2->R7 is :code-retired by :r2-r7-superseded-by-eps, but what that retired is a DEPENDENCY claim, while the same pair sits in :route-measured-drawn from WM-RUN2, decomposed through R2->R3a and R3a->R7 with the note that no direct R2-to-R7 call exists. A route can only refute a route-grain claim, so this refutes nothing; it is the C9 two-relations distinction as a live case. R5->R6 is :ruling-retired by :r6-r14-order -- Joe ordering ruling, recorded as intent with no code changed to match -- so a route still running R5 before R6 is evidence that THE RULING IS NOT YET REALISED, not evidence against the drawing. Opened as I4. 19 of 21 drawn edges never fired. WHAT IT MEANS, stated carefully. The campaign rule says a retired edge on the route means the RETIREMENT was wrong and the FIGURE changes. That rule assumes the route tag names the same relation the figure draws. Before any retirement is reversed RUN3 must establish that: the route mixes scan-phase hops (R20->R12, R12->R2) with judge-phase ones, and a tagging convention that differs from the drawing would produce exactly this pattern. Either two retirements are refuted on record, or the route instrumentation is not measuring the drawn relation. Both are findings, they are not the same finding, and RUN3 decides which."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:489:   :review "Signed, no defect. futon2 6917ba0 (codex-21). Route persisted present-only and UNCONDITIONALLY, justified by measurement rather than assertion: 1,093 bytes for nine hops on a 1,042,451-byte record, 0.105%. 40 tests / 124 assertions, clj-kondo clean, check-parens OK, pre-flight PASS on 6917ba0. Receipt now carries the run id -- confirmed in the pre-flight output, tick-run-record-2026-09-01-5c5d6b26-....edn -- so same-day runs no longer overwrite. THE ROUND-TRIP TEST CHECKS HOP ORDER EXPLICITLY, asserting [:R20 :R12 :R2] rather than presence, which is what RUN3 needs since a route is a SEQUENCE. And the fixture matches the real shape: route-tag (war_machine.clj:4340-4345) emits {:node :via :at}, so a hop is a CONSECUTIVE PAIR and the receipt :fromNode/:toNode form is a derived rendering. Worth stating because RUN3 must pair consecutive nodes, not look for from/to keys. ONE NOTE, not a defect: the I3 golden test still passes because its fixture carries no route, while a real default record HAS legitimately changed by 1,093 bytes. The golden was not regenerated, which is right; its claim is simply narrower than its name suggests. WHAT THE REVIEW DID NOT COVER, and I opened rows for both: the trace record still carries no run id although the receipt now does (RUN11), and nothing prevents two agents starting ticks at once (RUN12) -- which happened while this review was in progress."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:495:   :result "CONFORMANT. 20 records, 20 routes, 180 hops, 9 distinct, exit 0. ZERO refutations and ZERO unmapped hops. All twenty ticks took the SAME route, which is worth stating: the run exercises one path twenty times, not twenty paths. By the by-grounds rule: R2->R7 EXCLUDED as dependency-grain (retired on a dependency claim while the pair is drawn as a measured route; a route is a tag sequence, not the dependency DAG, so it cannot refute it) and reported as excluded rather than passed over; R5->R6 RULING-UNREALISED (retired by Joe ordering ruling with no code changed to match) which opens I4; two drawn (R7->R3, R8->R5); five route-measured. 19 OF 22 DRAWN EDGES NEVER FIRED. Not a conformance failure -- the figure draws the apparatus, the run exercises one tick path through it -- but it BOUNDS what a shadow tick can validate: learning (R1->R4, R3->R1), actuation (R9->R16, R15->R16, R16->R2) and the retired-but-still-drawn pairs are all untouched. Also confirmed, since S1b did not check it: :f-pi-by-candidate-id present 20/20, :status :present 20/20 -- but only because the aborted run left a predecessor to score against, so this is NOT evidence a cold start is handled. The earlier S1 showed that separately (:absent at tick 1, correct)."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:496:   :controls "THREE RUN, all refused, exit 1: a :code-retired ROUTE-GRAIN edge (R7->R14) gives 1 refutation; an unknown pair (R4->R20) gives unmapped; a drawn edge traversed in REVERSE (R3->R7 where only R7->R3 is drawn) gives unmapped, so direction is checked and not just adjacency. A FOURTH WAS SPECIFIED AND IS NOT IMPLEMENTED, and the reason is the finding: a legitimate edge traversed OUT OF ORDER cannot be tested, because the drawing carries no total order over edges -- there is nothing to be out of order against. \"Every hop is an edge\" and \"the hops form the route we drew\" are different claims and only the first is currently checkable. Recorded rather than quietly dropped."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:498:   :decision-rule "BY GROUNDS, not pass/fail (claude-1, verified against the registry by claude-20). Read the :retires set from control-map-edges.edn AT RUN TIME and carry no list, including a corrected one: the list in the campaign brief was wrong in three places -- it named R8->R5, which is not retired, and omitted R13->R14 and R2->R3. Eight retired pairs, not six. A pair may be retired by MORE THAN ONE decision on different grounds (R6->R13 is retired on both :ruling and :code), so classify by the SET of grounds, not the first match. THREE OUTCOMES. (1) :code-retired ROUTE-GRAIN edge traversed -> REFUTATION: the retirement was wrong and the figure changes. (2) :code-retired DEPENDENCY-GRAIN edge traversed -> NOT TESTED BY ROUTES: excluded from the verdict and REPORTED as excluded, since a route is a sequence of tag stamps and not the dependency DAG (D6). R2->R7 is the only one of this kind. (3) :ruling-retired edge traversed -> THE RULING IS NOT REALISED IN CODE: opens a build row, not a conformance failure. The figure legend draws all seven retirements alike; the conformance report must not."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:590:   :progress "(a) DISCOVERY: futon2 1fe0704, holes/labs/wm-contract/C474-cascade-order-discovery.md. The acceptance splits this row in its own words -- \"DISCOVERY FIRST, no implementation in the same slice\" -- so :loop-mode was added to make the ledger say what the acceptance already required; it describes the row, it is not a scheduling preference (same handling as I5). SLICE (b), THE DISPOSITION, IS UNTAKEN. Do not take (a) again. WHAT (b) INHERITS, so none of it is re-derived. (1) THE CASCADE IS CONSTRUCTED BEFORE SELECTION, NOT AFTER: futon2.report.cascade-lane/cascade-lane (cascade_lane.clj:404-454, shelling out to a Python constructor at cascade_lane.clj:20-22) is called at war_machine.clj:5047-5051 over wm-ranked -- R5's ordering -- while policy/select-action is not reached until war_machine.clj:5101-5110. (2) IT ALREADY TRIES TO BE TARGET-FIRST AND AIMS ONE STEP TOO EARLY: *gate-decision-target?* (cascade_lane.clj:381-389) carries an operator ruling of 2026-07-06 with the SAME content as Joe's 2026-08-30 one -- \"accept the decision so that the machine can act on its decision\" -- and decision-entry implements it as (first ranked-actions) (cascade_lane.clj:391-402), the rank-1 of the EFE sort. (3) R6 CANNOT BE MOVED BEFORE R5 BY REORDERING, which is the finding that decides what (b) can even ask for: on this tick's :strategic-recommendation boundary the choice is positional -- (or (first controller-entries) (first ranked-actions)) at policy.clj:365 -- and the scores are read at policy.clj:525 into tau and the softmax (policy.clj:358-359). select-action is a function of R5's output; literal inversion is a NEW SELECTION LAW, Joe's to rule on. Under the catalogue reading of R6 (control-stages.edn:15 labels it \"Candidate action space\") the inversion is ALREADY TRUE and untagged: the candidate space is built at war_machine.clj:4933-4942 and enriched at :4954-4960, both before rank-actions at :5002, which is what control-map-edges.edn :derived-undrawn already records as R6->R5. One box, two roles, one route tag -- WHICH ROLE THE RULING RETIRES IS (b)'S FIRST QUESTION. (4) THE TARGET IS SETTLED THREE TIMES AND THE CASCADE AIMS AT THE FIRST: R5 rank-1 (war_machine.clj:5002), R6 controller decision (policy.clj:365), then R14 strategic selection (war_machine.clj:5126-5138) whose action OVERWRITES the R6 one at war_machine.clj:5155-5157 and is what the record persists (war_machine.clj:5277). MEASURED, not argued: over 20 S1b and 4 S5 records, R5 rank-1 equals R6's input rank-1 in 20/20, and R6's choice differs from the recorded decision in 24/24 -- rank-1 was M-expressions-of-interest on all 24 while the decision was M-aif-policy-conditioned-eig or M-wm-aif-policy-grain-compliance. (5) NO RECORDED RUN BUILT A CASCADE AT ALL: run_tick_once.clj:211 and full_loop_runner.clj:2501 both pass :include-advisory-lanes? false, so cascade-policies is [] on every tick in runs/2026-09-01*. The R5->R6 hop RUN3 classified is the ranking-to-selection hop; the cascade is not on it, and any run (b) proposes must first turn the Python subprocess on. (6) THE ONE COUPLING THAT MAKES THE SMALL MOVE NOT SMALL: f-pi-dark-readback and beta-dark-carry (war_machine.clj:5079-5086) run over wm-ranked+cascades and, under FUTON_WM_TAU_MODE=variational-beta-gamma, beta feeds select-action's temperature at war_machine.clj:5109-5110. Making the cascade depend on the decision would make beta depend on the cascade and the decision on beta -- a cycle where there is none today; the comment at war_machine.clj:5069-5078 says the ordering was chosen for exactly this reason. (7) AND CONSTRUCTING FOR THE DECIDED TARGET UPSTREAM DOES NOT MAKE IT THE ENACTED ONE: enact/act-gates-with-shown (enact.clj:162-171) rebuilds the lane from the served :ranked-actions and close-loop! enacts the FIRST PASSING gate (enact.clj:307), which is a second change on the R16 side. BOUND ON EVERY NUMBER ABOVE, stated rather than implied: the recorded ticks ran the STUB selector (:selectorSeam \"stub:first-ranked-authorized-mission\"; run_tick_once.clj:72-73) because futon3c.peripheral.live-wm-selection/validated-selection (run_tick_once.clj:18-19) did not resolve, so the SIZE of the R6-vs-R14 gap under the live selector is not measured. ALSO: [:R6 :R13] and [:R11 :R16], the other two pairs :r6-r14-order retires, are untouched by (a); [:R6 :R13] is independently code-retired by :r6-r13-depth-precedes-selection (control-map-edges.edn:235-239), so only [:R11 :R16] has no second ground. REPORTED NOT REPAIRED: C465's cascade-subprocess row cites war_machine.clj:4820-4824 for this call site, which has drifted to :5047-5051. GATES: no src/, no registry, no run -- nothing in (a) needed clj-kondo, check-parens, tests or the run lock; negative_controls.sh and pointer_check.bb run after this row was written, per C472 s7. || (b) THE DISPOSITION: futon2 7970890, holes/labs/wm-contract/C475-cascade-order-disposition.md. BOTH SLICES ARE NOW DONE and the row is :blocked, not :done -- see :blocker. WHAT (b) SETTLED, and it is settled from a source rather than referred to Joe as (a) expected. (a) s first question -- which of R6 s two roles the ruling retires -- is answered by the record of the exchange the registry entry quotes, futon2/holes/problems/P-validated-R5.md s3d-prime (:412-441): :431 says the candidate space R6-C is a space of TARGETS, :435-437 says the drawn R5-rank-R6-R13-R14 is mis-ordered FOR THE CATALOGUE S R6 with derived path R6-C -> R5 -> R14 -> R13 -> R16, which is character-for-character the entry s :derived-control-path (control-map-edges.edn:261). So the retired [:R5 :R6] is a claim about the candidate-space node, and under that reading it is ALREADY TRUE IN CODE (candidates war_machine.clj:4933-4942 and :4954-4960, rank-actions :5002) and already recorded at control-map-edges.edn:68 by both derivations. CONSEQUENCE FOR RUN3: its :ruling-unrealised verdict on the R5->R6 hop measures a different relation from the one retired, the same two-relations distinction it applies to [:R2 :R7] and states in its own header (run3_conformance.bb:28-42). Its grain proxy could not have caught this: classify tests (measured hop) only on :code retirements (run3_conformance.bb:117-124), and [:R5 :R6] is not in :route-measured-drawn anyway; the R6-C split lives in :derived-undrawn, which topology does not read (run3_conformance.bb:52-60). Nothing RUN3 measured is wrong; the inference from traversed to unrealised is. THE UNREALISED HALF has no route signature at all: 48 records across s1b/s2/s4/s5 carry ZERO :apply-cascade entries, so the corpus cannot speak to it either way. TWO CORRECTIONS TO (a), carried forward rather than edited away. (a) named the second [:R6 :R13] ground :r6-r13-depth-precedes-selection -- NO SUCH KEY EXISTS; it is :r6-r13-supplied-independently at control-map-edges.edn:234-239. And (a) said only [:R11 :R16] has no second ground -- it HAS one, :drawn-not-derivable control-map-edges.edn:142; what it lacks is a CODE ground. NEW on R11, reported not resolved: two carriers with one label. futon2.aif.hierarchical-budget (hierarchical_budget.clj:2) has no non-test caller -- policy/select-budgeted-actions (policy.clj:22-30) only from hierarchical_budget_test.clj:85, and hierarchical_budget_adapter.clj:68 only from its own test -- so neither the retired [:R11 :R16] nor the ruling s replacement [:R11 :R14] is realisable under it; while close_loop.clj:54-58 says binding *semilattice-fold?* false disarms R11 and THAT fold is on the actuation path (close_loop.clj:76-107 -> enact.clj:175 -> first passing gate enact.clj:307), which is an R11->R16 relation. Unexercised in every recorded run by the same zero-cascade measurement. GATES: no src/, no registry, no run -- nothing in (b) needed clj-kondo, check-parens, tests or the run lock; negative_controls.sh and pointer_check.bb run after this row was written."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:594:   :review "Accepted with one small evidence-checker fix, p4ng c320107: pointer_check.bb lacked the scripts/ source root needed to resolve the valid scripts/wm_scheduled_run.clj:113 pointer. Read 183749a in full. Reproduced 86 tests / 324 assertions, 0 failures and 0 errors; clj-kondo 0 errors / 0 warnings; check-parens exit 0. RUN3 controls passed, including by-run-id selection of 2 of 3 shared-file records; S1b fallback reproduced 20 records, 180 hops, 0 refutations, 0 unmapped, 1 ruling-unrealised R5->R6, 1 excluded R2->R7, 19/22 unfired, CONFORMANT. After the fix pointer_check resolved 214/214 and negative_controls passed 16 negative / 10 positive. No registry or generator changed."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:597:   :evidence "futon2 183749a. FIELD: :run/id on the trace record -- the SAME key and value the receipt carries (scripts/futon2/run_tick_once.clj:179), so receipt and record join by equality, not by clock. PATH, one id per TICK: minted at run_tick_once.clj:263-266, into the judge opts at run_tick_once.clj:203-217 and :231, onto the judgement at scripts/futon2/report/war_machine.clj:5000-5006, onto the persisted record at src/futon2/aif/trace.clj:521-536. COST MEASURED, not assumed, over the twenty S1b records (runs/2026-09-01-s1b/wm-trace-s1b.edn): +48 bytes on a 1,045,286-byte record, 0.0046%, IDENTICAL on every record because a UUID string has fixed width -- so unconditional, and there is no flag-off byte shape to preserve. PRESENT-ONLY, and the two producers that mint no id were not changed: scripts/wm_scheduled_run.clj:113 and src/futon2/aif/full_loop_runner.clj:2639; absence is tested as absence rather than nil (test/futon2/aif/trace_test.clj run-id-is-absent-not-nil-when-the-producer-has-none-test). ROUND TRIP: trace_test.clj run-id-roundtrips-through-the-shared-trace-file-test writes three records into ONE per-date file -- two runs plus a producer with no id -- through the real write-trace!, reads back with read-trace, and asserts exactly one record belongs to the first run; that is the 2026-09-01 collision in miniature. WIRING ON THE REAL PATH: test/run_tick_once_test.clj full-diagnostic-tick-issues-no-http-post-test now asserts the opts handed to generate-war-machine carry :run-id and that it equals the receipt's :run/id. RUN3 SELECTION: run3_conformance.bb takes a run DIRECTORY, reads the ids from its receipts' :run/id field (not from the filename, so a rename cannot widen the selection) and selects the shared per-date file's records by membership; the pre-extracted timestamp-range trace is the FALLBACK, entered only when by-id finds nothing, and which path ran is printed and written to conformance.edn as :selection / :selection-fallback-file. S1b re-checked through the directory entry point: by-timestamp-range (20 receipts, 20 run ids), SAME verdict -- 20 records, 180 hops, CONFORMANT, 0 refutations, 0 unmapped, 1 ruling-unrealised R5->R6, 1 excluded R2->R7, 19/22 unfired. SELECTION CONTROL, because by-id would otherwise be unexercised code claiming to be the method (no record in existence carries an id): run3_conformance_controls.bb control 4 plants one per-date file holding two records of this run (real S1b route, conformant) and one of another run (planted R4->R20, not conformant) with a receipt naming only this run, and asserts selection by-run-id, 2 records, exit 0. Sensitive: adding a receipt for the other run widens it to 3 records and the check reports NOT CONFORMANT with 2 unmapped hops. FUTON_WM_TRACE_DIR exists for that control alone. GATES: clj-kondo 0/0 and check-parens OK on the five Clojure files; clojure -X:test :nses [futon2.report.war-machine-test futon2.aif.trace-test run-tick-once-test wm-run-lock-test] 86 tests / 322 assertions, 0 failures 0 errors; bb run3_conformance_controls.bb PASS; negative_controls.sh PASS (16 negative, 10 positive); pointer_check 207/0. NOT DONE, stated rather than implied: NO war-machine tick was run (the row asks for a round-trip test, not a run), so the first real record carrying :run/id will come from the next run; S1b's own records are not retro-fitted and its conformance is still selected by the timestamp range COLLISION-NOTE.md fixes; the scheduled and full-loop runners still write no run id. Findings: holes/labs/wm-contract/C467-trace-run-identity.md. Prior evidence: runs/2026-09-01/ and runs/2026-09-01-s1b/COLLISION-NOTE.md; RUN10 6917ba0 fixed the receipt and not the trace."}
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:689:   :evidence "futon2 6110d5f. Replay only over the three 2026-09-02 records (0a18c4f7, 4abad68c, 801976e7; data/wm-trace/wm-trace-2026-09-02.edn, 3 records, no newer on disk) -- no live tick, no run lock, nothing written under data/. Script holes/labs/wm-contract/u12_c_mis_falsifier.clj; artifacts runs/U12-c-mis-falsifier/ (U12-FALSIFIER.txt, measurements.edn, planted-criteria-declared-observable.edn, node-fixtures/ with README.md). Every number comes from the SHIPPED mission-c-readback (war_machine.clj:1557-1613) or mission-c's public fns; the only thing this row constructs is the plant. EXTRACTOR FIRST, as the :statement asks: 40 fixtures, one per (run-id, node), 30 present and 9 typed absence over 13 nodes x 3 ticks plus the README. The node->field map is not invented -- every :via column is copied off the record's OWN :route entry, so the mapping is the tick's claim about itself. R7 IS LOCATED, answering the gap zaif S7 carried as ':precision has no top-level key on the record': it is :precision-state on all three records, and the real gap is that it covers 8 of the 14 channels R2 observes (missing :consulting-pct :depositing-signal :loop-health :mathematics-pct :portfolio-pct :stack-pct), absent rather than at precision 0. (a) DETERMINISM PASSES AS STATED WITH A QUALIFICATION THAT MATTERS FOR EVERY LATER REPLAY: two passes of mission-c-readback over the same records, called with (:active-mission, :ranked-actions, :observation) and nothing else, are equal on all fields, and the artifacts are byte-identical across two PROCESS runs. But the readback is not a function of record fields alone -- the criteria arrive from a FILE named by war_machine.clj:1532-1541 or by the candidate's :action :mission-path, and the record carries the PATH (:criteria-source) with no content hash, so a replay after that file is edited returns different numbers with no way to tell. The two sources this run read are sha256'd into measurements.edn (S4-identify-ingest.edn b85cb1da..., M-wm-aif-policy-grain-compliance.md 51f6de53...); the repair is one field, :criteria-source-sha256, on the readback map. (b) DISCRIMINATION FAILS ON EVERY FIELD, FOR FOUR INDEPENDENT REASONS. On the corpus as it stands the question is unanswerable and typed as such: all three records are :absent (0a18c4f7 :no-active-clock; the other two :no-measurable-criteria), 0 of 3 criteria measurable on the zaif ingest and 0 criteria at all on the compliance mission's doc -- U11's finding reproduced from the same files. So the row PLANTED the repair U11 named, one declared :observable per criterion (:u-rows-green -> :support-coverage, :reporting-gate-holds -> :mission-health, :honest-gap-list-published -> :annotation-health), and asked again where a number exists. Then: (1) non-mission actions carry NO risk_mis field at all -- war_machine.clj:1584-1592 maps only mission-action? entries -- so the comparison section 7 names is between a number and an absent field, 133 mission actions against 13 non-mission on both clocked ticks; (2) among mission actions the value is CONSTANT, 1 distinct value [4.539889921682063E-5] over all 133, because v0's forward model is the status quo for every pi (mission_c.clj:337-342); (3) it does not even separate the CLOCKED mission's action from the other missions' -- 1 clocked action and 132 other-mission actions carry the SAME single value, :scope records the difference and the number does not; (4) THE SECOND FINDING OF THE ROW, INDEPENDENT OF THE FORWARD MODEL: risk_mis does not vary with the WORLD either. A single-criterion sweep over all 14 channels R2 observes returns 1 distinct value. THE CAUSE IS A TYPE COMPARISON, NOT A MODELLING CHOICE, and it is a defect this row REPORTS AND DOES NOT REPAIR: log-preference's Bernoulli branch (preferences.clj:242-244) selects the non-target outcome with `(= 0 x)`, which is Clojure value equality and therefore FALSE across number classes -- true for the long 0, false for the double 0.0. R2's observation vector is doubles throughout (:consulting-pct 0.0, :sorry-count-norm 0.0, :ticks-firing-ratio 0.0 on 801976e7), so no observed value can select it; measured on the shipped fn, 0 (Long) gives -10.000045398900186 while 0.0, 0.5, -3.0 and nil all give -4.539889921682063E-5. `nil` reading as a MET criterion is the C130 discipline inverted. Every undeclared criterion takes {:becomes 1} (mission_c.clj:57-65) and no criterion in the corpus declares a :spec, so this is the live path. SCOPE, grepped across every .clj under ~/code: log-preference has exactly two production callers, mission_c.clj:333 and mission_c.clj:370 -- both U11's new code. C_int and the c-vector lane score Bernoulli outcomes through pref/kl with an explicit {:kind :bernoulli :p q} (c_vector.clj:681-684) and never take this path, so nothing older is exposed. mission_c_test.clj:197-205 pins the branch with the LONG 0 and passes while the live vocabulary cannot reach that value -- the test and the corpus disagree about what an outcome is. (c) THE SATISFIED-CRITERIA ZERO FAILS, and by more than the floor. Satisfied field: 4.539889921682063E-5, not 0 -- a temperature floor, -ln c* with c* = 0.9999546021312976 at T = 0.1 (preferences.clj:216-221), 220270.66x below the long-0 unsatisfied 10.000045398900184, which on its own would be a nit. What defeats the clause is that at the DOUBLE 0.0 the unsatisfied field scores 4.539889921682063E-5, identical to the satisfied one: the unsatisfied pole is unreachable from the machine's own observables, so 'goes to zero as criteria are met' is not measurable on the live path at all and the completion gate design section 3 argues for does not exist yet. A RANGE :spec is a different problem, reachable and unexercised (no corpus criterion declares one): inside the band the term is ln Z, which is NEGATIVE -- [0.5 1.0] at 0.75 gives -0.5119492459595545, [0.4 0.6] at 0.5 gives -0.9254907419272016 -- so summing risk_mis with C_int's KL (>= 0, preferences.clj:277-303) in one G would let a satisfied range criterion pay a BONUS. VERDICT: design section 7's revert condition IS MET by (b) ('Failure of (b) on all fields = the status-quo forward model is too weak, and the row that flips anything on reverts'). Nothing is flipped on -- FUTON_WM_MISSION_C defaults off (war_machine.clj:1615-1621) -- so the revert is a no-op today and what it binds is U13: the :choices :c-grain entry must record the v0 binding as MEASURED NON-DISCRIMINATING, not as an untested limitation. NO RULING WRITTEN; registries untouched, :covers-key :none. GATES: clj-kondo 0 errors / 0 warnings on the one new file; futon4/dev/check-parens.sh OK; negative_controls.sh PASS (16 negative, 10 positive; shared registries untouched); pointer_check.bb 810 pointers in 3 files, 0 unresolved. No src/ change, so no suite was owed. Figure not regenerated (TN section 9a)."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:690:   :reviewer-should-check "(1) THE DEFECT CLAIM, because three of this row's four (b) reasons and all of (c) rest on it. Read preferences.clj:242-244 and decide for yourself whether `(= 0 x)` can be true for any value R2 puts on the observation vector -- (= 0 0.0) is false in Clojure and the vector is doubles. If you think a criterion should never be bound to a continuous channel in the first place, say so, because that is a different repair from changing `=` to `==` and U13 has to record one of them. (2) THAT I DID NOT REPAIR IT. U12 is a measurement row and preferences.clj is C_int's file; a one-character edit there changes what every Bernoulli criterion scores. The repair is a decision about what an outcome IS -- `==`, a threshold, or a refusal to score a continuous observable against a Bernoulli C -- and it wants a row, not a patch inside a falsifier. Push back if you think the falsifier should have shipped the fix. (3) THE PLANT, which is the one thing here that is not the shipped path. runs/U12-c-mis-falsifier/planted-criteria-declared-observable.edn is the S4 ingest plus one :observable per criterion and nothing else; the channel assignment is ARBITRARY and is stated as such. Check that no conclusion depends on which channels were picked -- the 14-channel sweep exists to make that checkable rather than assertable, and it returns 1 distinct value for every channel. (4) THAT BOTH CLOCKED RECORDS WERE POINTED AT THE SAME PLANTED FILE, including M-wm-aif-policy-grain-compliance, whose own mission doc has no criteria section. That holds the criteria fixed on purpose so only the tick varies; a reviewer who reads it as attributing zaif's criteria to another mission should say so, since it would change how row 4abad68c is read. (5) CLAUSE (a)'s VERDICT WORD. I called it PASS-with-qualification rather than FAIL. The shipped fn is pure and reproduces exactly; what is impure is the criteria file behind the path. A reviewer who reads 'reproduces from record fields alone' strictly should mark it FAIL and require :criteria-source-sha256 before U13 -- the artifact carries both readings and the sha256s, so either verdict is supportable from it. (6) THE EXTRACTOR'S NODE MAP (u12_c_mis_falsifier.clj node-map). Every :via is copied from the record's own :route; R1, R9 and S4 are the three rows NOT justified by a route hop and say so in their :basis. If you think R3 belongs to :mu-pre rather than :mu-post, that is a real disagreement and the fixtures carry both. (7) THAT NOTHING LEAKED: git show --stat 6110d5f should touch no registry .edn, no p4ng file, no src/ and nothing under data/."}
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:701:   :evidence "futon2 0f22895 (registry) and p4ng 3cf872c (tally row, figure, one pointer_check root). (a) aif-equations.edn gains :choices :c-grain, :status :observed-not-decided -- an observation, not a decision, per TN section 1 rule 3. Nine fields: :evidence (the two C's with their pointers -- C_int at preferences.clj:193 with risk a KL at preferences.clj:277-303, C_mis at mission_c.clj:246-268/270-315/317-336/338-376 with the readback at war_machine.clj:1557-1613, carry at :1615-1621, binding after selection is final at :5788-5799, persistence at trace.clj:604-610); :statement; :answers (the S1 refusal at P-validated-R5.md:58, restated futon3 LA1c-restatement.md:543, and Q3 at P-validated-R5.md:308); :v0-forward-model; :measured; :scalar-bridge; :lean-note; :flip-is-j-gated; :not-claimed. THE ONE PLACE THE ROW'S :statement WAS OVERTAKEN BY EVIDENCE, and it is what a reviewer should weigh first: :statement asked for the v0 limitation 'stated plainly', and U12 (futon2 6110d5f) had by then MEASURED it, so the entry records the v0 binding as MEASURED NON-DISCRIMINATING rather than as an untested limitation -- one risk_mis value 4.539889921682063E-5 across all 133 mission actions on each clocked tick, non-mission actions carrying no field at all, the clocked mission indistinguishable from the other 132, and 1 distinct value over all 14 channels R2 observes. That last one is not the forward model: preferences.clj:242-244 selects the Bernoulli non-target with (= 0 x), false for the double 0.0 R2 emits. Design section 7's revert condition is recorded as MET, and as a no-op today because FUTON_WM_MISSION_C is off. (b) THE TALLY ROW MOVED TO :partial, NOT :repaired, WHICH IS THE SECOND THING TO CHECK -- the row's :statement said the row 'closes on the section-6 bridge' and I did not close it. The counting rule (defect-repair-tally.edn:3-8) makes :repaired need a named commit/record/artefact and :partial a named subset, and resolves ambiguity toward open. What landed: the bridge is STATED (DESIGN-c-vector.md:125-136) and INSTANTIATED ONCE, at mission grain (mission_c.clj:317-336 composes ln C; the scalar risk_mis at :338-376 is derived from it, not authored beside it). What did not: the type tag :scalar-awaiting-density occurs in NO source file -- grep over ~/code for *.clj *.edn *.md *.lean returns DESIGN-c-vector.md:131 and worklist.edn:693 only -- and every scalar section 6 names is still bare: futon3c zaif_controller.clj:11-20 (Z2 arm constants, ask payoff at :87-96) and :expected-roi-gbp at war_machine.clj:1184-1197, summed into a sustainability payoff at wm_regulator_sweep.clj:93-96. So class 7 reads 3/4 +1 partial and the ledger 59 repaired / 2 partial / 0 open over the unchanged 61-instance population. Figure regenerated (defect-repair-tally.svg/.pdf, stamp 'as of 2026-09-02 source SHA-256 bcba46638f3f'); --check and --negative-control both PASS. (c) THE LEAN NOTE IS ONE SENTENCE IN THE REGISTRY ENTRY and Holes.lean is untouched: preferenceStackLiveRecorded (Holes.lean:6574-6575) has its close-by-record path NOTED and not taken, because no committed trace record carries a :mission-c key -- grep over futon2 data/wm-trace/*.edn on 2026-09-02 returns zero files (the only 'mission-c' hits in the 09-02 trace are 21 :mission-coherence-patterns), U11 and U12 both replayed, and the flag is off. ONE TOOL FIX, the same defect class a FIFTH time and the first crossing a repository: pointer_check.bb resolves a BARE FILENAME against a hand-maintained directory allowlist, so the futon3c pointers this entry needs -- the scalars where the bridge is NOT applied -- reported 'file not found'. Appended /code/futon3c/src/futon3c/agents/, not inserted, so no existing first-match resolution changes (p4ng 3cf872c); 824 -> 850 pointers, 0 unresolved. GATES: worklist_check 99 items OK; negative_controls PASS (16 negative, 10 positive; shared registries untouched); pointer_check 850 pointers in 3 files, 0 unresolved. No src/ change, so no suite was owed and no clj-kondo/check-parens run was owed. gen_aif_dag.bb NOT run -- adding a :choices key changes the generated conformance counts, and regeneration is gated on review (TN section 9a)."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:729:   :statement "Full-AIF-loop unit test on ZAIF -- a practical mission driven through the system, per component (Joe 2026-09-02, superseding the Ants target; ChipWits provenance at P-validated-R5.md:704-716). The mission exists: futon2/holes/M-zaif-harness.md; the artifact is zaif v0 (futon3c/src/futon3c/agents/zaif_controller.clj -- pure per-decision arm choice over retrieve/act/ask with fixed documented constants, plus zaif_inputs.clj). Drive ONE recorded zaif decision through the WM's R nodes with planted expectations at each boundary: mission/tool observations at R2, belief at R1/R3, per-arm predicted outcomes at R4 (Q(o|pi) from the arm's declared yield/cost model), G = risk + ambiguity at R5 over the three arms, posterior with the flagged score at R6/R8/R14 (ln E from arm history once live; candidates OPAQUE at the seam per U5, so the same test survives arms -> cascades), action at R16, and the constants-vs-learning boundary at R17 (v0 constants are fixed BY DESIGN -- the test pins that they do not drift, and names R17 as stubbed). A regression names the node that broke. The stated end: these zaif agents are the WM's crew -- the test is the first fitting."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:762:   :statement "BRANCHES BUILT AND RUN for the Bernoulli outcome semantics (U12's defect, verified by claude-1 at preferences.clj:242; Joe's 2026-09-01 rule: a choice the theory does not settle gets branches built and run, not an advance ruling): log-preference's Bernoulli branch selects the non-target outcome with (= 0 x), false for double 0.0, so the unsatisfied pole is unreachable from R2's all-doubles observation vector, and nil scores as SATISFIED. Scope: only U11's mission_c.clj calls this path; C_int unaffected. Build the three candidate semantics behind a declared per-call selector (no default change): (1) == numeric equality; (2) declared binarization -- a Bernoulli spec on a continuous observable must carry a threshold, else refuse; (3) typed refusal on spec/observable mismatch, == for genuinely binary observables. Under ALL arms nil is a typed absence, never satisfied (C130-entailed, not a branch). Run all three over the U12 corpus and the 14-channel sweep; report where they agree, where they differ, and what each does to (b)-discrimination and (c)-satisfied-zero. mission_c_test.clj:197-205 (pins the long-0 vocabulary the live path cannot speak) gains a double-0.0 and nil case per arm."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:769:         :b "The arms were RUN and they are distinguished behaviourally -- over the 14 channels R2 observes, :numeric-equality scores 14/14 with 2 distinct risk values, :declared-binarization 4/14, :typed-binary-only 0/14 (U16 artifact section 2). What no experiment on a recorded field can do is RANK them, because ranking needs to know whether a criterion like 'U-rows green' is a binary observable, and no field records that. Every field the machine writes is downstream of the reading, so measuring harder produces more consequences of each arm and no evidence about which is correct."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:771:   :statement "WHAT IS A BERNOULLI OUTCOME when the observable is continuous? U12 found log-preference's Bernoulli branch selects the non-target pole with (= 0 x), false for the double 0.0, so R2's observation vector cannot reach the unsatisfied pole and nil reads as met (preferences.clj:242). U16 built and ran the three candidate readings; all three fix the defect and none is preferred by any number. THE ARMS AND WHAT EACH COSTS, measured over the 14-channel sweep and the U12 corpus: (1) :numeric-equality -- == rather than =, needs no declaration, scores every channel (14/14, 2 distinct risk values), and calls 0.75 a MET criterion, so a channel two thirds of the way to its target is scored as satisfied. (2) :declared-binarization -- a Bernoulli spec on a continuous observable must carry a threshold; scores 4/14 (the channels reading exactly 0), refuses the rest :no-declared-threshold, and with a threshold declared scores 14/14 and reads 0.75 as unmet or met depending on where the threshold is put. (3) :typed-binary-only -- refuse on spec/observable mismatch, == for genuinely binary observables; scores 0/14 because no criterion declares an observable kind, and 4/14 once one does. Under all three, nil is a typed absence rather than a satisfied criterion, which is C130 discipline and not one of the branches. Arms 2 and 3 are indistinguishable at the composition on this corpus and part company only where a declaration exists. THE QUESTION: should a criterion whose observable carries no declaration be SCORED on the type fix alone, or REFUSED until someone writes the declaration down? Scoring gives the mission-grain C numbers on every tick at the price of calling most readings satisfied; refusing gives it a typed absence on nearly every tick until the mission documents are amended, which is work on nine criteria across two missions and everything written later. NOT PART OF THIS QUESTION, because U16 measured them settled: clause (b) is unchanged by every arm (risk_mis is one number copied to every mission action, so an outcome reading cannot make it vary -- U13's forward model is what would), and the clause (c) zero is unreachable under every arm because 4.5399e-5 is the T=0.1 temperature floor."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:792:   :evidence "futon2 5af75e6 (code, tests) and this commit (registry + ledger). THE RULING IS RECORDED, AND THE CHANNEL WITH IT: aif-equations.edn :choices :c-grain gains :outcome-semantics-ruled (aif-equations.edn:323), additively -- no existing text changed, no new :choices key, the entry's own :status stays :observed-not-decided because the two-C split it covers is still not decided. It quotes the channel exactly as J6 recorded it rather than smoothing it: claude-1 recommended arm 2 and Joe answered add the criteria to the doc, so the assent was to a linked framing and not to the arm's name, and the entry says so. It also records the PRICE the ruling buys (a typed absence on nearly every tick until the mission documents are amended) and states that J6's 0.75-consequence note is not applicable, because arm 2 refuses an undeclared continuous observable instead of scoring it. (b) THE DECLARATION'S HOME MOVED, which is J6's acceptance: pref/c-distribution now carries :observable-kind and :threshold from a Bernoulli SPEC onto the built distribution, present-only (preferences.clj:193 observable-kinds, :200 bernoulli-declaration-fields, :235-283 c-distribution), and log-preference-under merges the spec's declaration OVER the caller's selector (preferences.clj:432; declaration-of at :228) -- a per-call selector may fill a declaration the mission omits and may not overrule one it wrote down, which is the whole reason for moving the home. A declared :binary observable needs no threshold and refuses a non-binary value under :non-binary-value-on-binary-observable rather than :no-declared-threshold (preferences.clj:392-397), because the declaration is present and the value contradicts it. mission-c/default-outcome-semantics (mission_c.clj:86) is the ruled arm and is what log-c-mis and risk-mis take when :outcome-semantics is OMITTED. AN EXPLICIT nil STILL NAMES THE PRE-RULING RAW PATH, and that is deliberate rather than a destructuring accident: U16's comparison script names its baseline column :selector nil (u16_outcome_semantics.clj:101), so its committed measurements still reproduce after the ruling; the two are pinned apart by mission_c_test/the-ruled-arm-is-the-default-and-an-explicit-nil-is-the-old-path (mission_c_test.clj:327). (c) THE LIVE-VOCABULARY PINS: the fixture that pinned U12's defect as the baseline is replaced by that test, which reads the DOUBLE 0.0 R2 actually emits (now the unsatisfied pole, risk 10.000045398900186), nil as :unread-outcome with no :risk key, and the explicit-nil column still returning U12's 4.539889921682063E-5. The long-0 pins in risk-mis-numbers-are-reproducible-from-the-record and a-met-criterion-stops-pulling are doubles now, and so is the observation at the war-machine seam (war_machine_test.clj:1688). (d) GAUGES, DECLARED NOT PLANTED, which is the distinction U12's stated plant makes necessary: war-machine/mission-c-declared-gauges (war_machine.clj:1543 the three bindings, :1566 the table) binds M-zaif-harness-v1's three criteria -- criterion-1 to :worklist-acceptance-state, criterion-2 to :reporting-gate-test-result, criterion-3 to :registry-gap-list-present -- each with :spec {:becomes 1 :observable-kind :binary}, ONE ENTRY PER CRITERION LISTED UNDER BOTH IDS IT HAS, because the ingest names its criteria and the doc reader numbers them and futon2 161ac09 ported the one into the other in order. mission-c/apply-gauge (mission_c.clj:171-197) merges a gauge into a criterion entry with the DOCUMENT WINNING on any key it declared itself, and criterion-row records :observable-source :declared-gauge, :spec-source :declared-gauge, :gauge and :gauge-source (mission_c.clj:206-222), so a supplied binding can never read as the mission's own declaration; a-gauge-cannot-overwrite-what-the-document-declared (mission_c_test.clj:604) pins the precedence. read-criteria takes :gauges and the readback supplies them, recording :declared-gauges, :outcome-semantics and :refused-outcomes (war_machine.clj:1669-1680) -- the last because after the ruling a risk can be absent for a reason the ARM gives, and a refusal recording only that there is no number would be a worse record than the :missing-observables sibling beside it. THE ACCEPTANCE'S 3/3: war_machine_test/mission-c-reads-the-zaif-criteria-3-of-3-with-the-gauges-supplied-test (war_machine_test.clj:1789) reads the doc through the readback with the gauge observables supplied and gets 3 criteria, 3 measurable, :status :measured, outcomes [0 0 1] -- the first mission-grain C reading on this machine that is not a typed absence -- and the second half of that test pins a 0.75 on a declared-binary observable refusing with the criterion named. TWO FIXTURES futon2 161ac09 BROKE ARE REPAIRED, and they were failing at HEAD before this row: the mission doc now parses as :markdown-numbered-list, not the inline paragraph, so mission_c_test/reads-both-markdown-shapes-and-prefers-the-section (mission_c_test.clj:63) and war_machine_test/mission-c-falls-back-to-the-candidates-own-mission-doc-test (war_machine_test.clj:1612) pin the section shape and the inline shape is kept under test on text, since the live corpus no longer has an instance of it. WHAT A REPLAY OF THE TWO COMMITTED ARTIFACTS NOW RETURNS, measured rather than predicted, and NEITHER ARTIFACT WAS RE-RUN INTO ITS DIRECTORY OR EDITED: U16 replayed to a temp dir is byte-identical in sections 1, 2 and 4 -- every arm number, the plant sha256 f3e123fd..., 14/14, 4/14, 0/14 -- and differs ONLY in section 3, the mirror control, which now reads CONTROL: FAILS because that control compares the script's mirror against the SHIPPED readback and the shipped readback is what the ruling moved; the script's own :v0-shipped column is unchanged. U12 replayed to a temp dir changes in exactly the two places the ruling touches: :unsatisfied-double-0.0 moves from 4.539889921682063E-5 to 10.000045398900184, which is U12 clause (c)'s defect REPAIRED, and the live readback over the three 2026-09-02 records becomes :absent :unread-outcome because no criterion in that corpus declares a spec and the channels are continuous -- the price J6's ruling knowingly buys. The S4 ingest's three criteria also move from :unresolved-observable to :undeclared-observable, which is the gauge naming a missing PRODUCER where before there was only prose. LEDGER MECHANIC: U17 signed [:choices :c-grain] at 7ad831a and this entry's addition stales that signature, so per TN 9a U17 gains :superseded-by :U18 with its signature untouched, and this row carries :covers-key [:choices :c-grain] so the entry's current value is what U18's reviewer signs. J6 is closed on this evidence. GATES: clj-kondo 0 errors / 0 warnings on all six changed files; futon4/dev/check-parens.sh OK on all six; clojure -X:test over mission-c-test, preferences-cdist-test, preferences-test, c-vector-test, efe-test, efe-risk-mode-test, trace-test, zaif-full-loop-test, free-energy-test, precision-test and mission-control-graph-test (237 tests / 4249 assertions) and war-machine-test (68 tests / 377 assertions), 0 failures 0 errors; negative_controls.sh PASS (18 negative, 11 positive); pointer_check.bb 913 pointers in 3 files, 0 unresolved. No p4ng file touched, no generator run, no figure regenerated, nothing written under data/ or runs/, no run lock taken."
holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:793:   :reviewer-should-check "(1) THE DEFAULT MOVED, WHICH IS THE BIGGEST THING HERE. risk-mis with :outcome-semantics omitted no longer returns the raw pref/log-preference number; it returns the ruled arm's, which on the U12 corpus is a typed ABSENCE where a number used to be. That is what J6 chose, but it means every future replay of U12's script differs from its committed artifact in the two places named in :evidence, and I did NOT re-run either artifact. Decide whether U12's runs/ directory should be re-measured under the ruling as its own row, or left as the record of what the machine did before it. (2) THE EXPLICIT-NIL ESCAPE. I kept the pre-ruling path reachable by passing :outcome-semantics nil explicitly, relying on Clojure's :or defaulting only on an ABSENT key. It is exactly the semantics I want and it is pinned by a test, but it is a subtle distinction to leave load on: a caller who writes :outcome-semantics (get opts :arm) and gets nil silently takes the defective path. The alternative is a named fourth value (:pre-J6-raw) and no reliance on nil, which is louder and adds a vocabulary member. Say if you want that. (3) THE MIRROR CONTROL IN U16'S SCRIPT NOW PRINTS 'CONTROL: FAILS -- do not read section 4'. It fails correctly -- it compares the script's mirror against the shipped readback and the shipped readback took the ruling -- but a future re-runner sees a red control on a green change. I left the script untouched because editing an artifact producer would stale the artifact it produced; the other repair is to teach the mirror the ruled arm and regenerate, which rewrites a measurement record. Neither is obviously right and it is not my call. (4) THE GAUGE TABLE LISTS SIX KEYS FOR THREE CRITERIA, one per id vocabulary, on the claim that futon2 161ac09 ported the ingest's three criteria into the doc verbatim and in order. I verified the order by reading both files; nothing enforces it, so if someone reorders the doc's numbered list the doc ids silently bind to the wrong criteria. The alternative is to bind only the ingest's named ids and let the doc route stay unmeasurable, which is safer and gives up the acceptance's 3/3 on the doc. (5) THE GAUGE OBSERVABLES ARE PRODUCED BY NOTHING. :worklist-acceptance-state, :reporting-gate-test-result and :registry-gap-list-present are not R2 channels and no code emits them, so on a live tick the three criteria read :undeclared-observable -- a missing producer rather than prose. That is honest and it is also the whole remaining distance to a measurable mission; the 3/3 in the acceptance is with the values supplied by a test. Check that you read the acceptance the same way I did. (6) SCOPE I ADDED BEYOND THE ROW: :refused-outcomes and :outcome-semantics on the readback record (argued in :evidence), the :non-binary-value-on-binary-observable reason under arm 2, and validation throws on a malformed :observable-kind or :threshold at construction. Each is small and each is a decision. (7) A POINTER I MOVED AND DID NOT RE-ANCHOR. Inserting the declaration block above c-distribution pushed pref/log-preference's Bernoulli branch from preferences.clj:242 to :292, and :measured and :flip-is-j-gated in the same registry entry still say :242-244 -- text U13 and U17 signed. I recorded the drift in the new key and left the signed prose alone rather than re-anchoring it inside a row whose registry edit is supposed to be additive; pointer_check does not catch this class because it resolves line RANGES and not content, which is the same hole U10 found in C5's pointers. If you would rather the two were re-anchored under the supersession this row already takes, say so. (8) THAT NOTHING LEAKED: git show --stat 5af75e6 touches six files -- three src/scripts, three test -- and this commit touches aif-equations.edn and worklist.edn. No p4ng file, no generator, nothing under data/ or runs/. The three untracked tick-run-record-2026-09-02-*.edn files were in the working tree when I arrived and are not mine; I staged explicit paths, not -a."}
