/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/labs/zaif-harness/runs/build-loop.log-17311-worklist.edn:227:   :review "Reviewed 2026-09-03 by the build-loop review seat (not the work seat; both shas carry Joe's git identity because the checkout is shared, so authorship is by seat: the row is :lane claude-2 WORK-seat output and states 'No live reload by WORK seat'). Diff read for both shas. futon2 zaif-harness tree clean but for untracked receipts; futon3c tree clean, both shas ancestors of master HEAD, one file per commit, no amend (D14's 67faf9f2 intact beneath them). GATES REPRODUCED in isolated sibling worktree futon3c-review-d15 at 99095908, exit codes captured directly, never against the live JVM: clj-kondo on the two changed files 0 errors/0 warnings, exit 0 (one pre-existing info at http.clj:7189 -- D13's review saw the same item at :7169, drifted by D14's insertions, far from the diff); check-parens OK, exit 0; futon3c.transport.invoke-ledger-atomicity-test 9 tests/39 assertions, 0 failures, exit 0 -- the claimed numbers exactly. ACCEPTANCE CLAUSE 1 MEASURED, NOT TAKEN BY CONSTRUCTION. The row offered 'BufferPoolMXBean delta, or by construction with heap streams shown in review'; I measured it. First attempt read a delta of 0 for BOTH the new path and the old one, i.e. the instrument was blind -- the cause is that NIO's scratch buffers live in a ThreadLocal cache, so sampling the pool from the parent thread after join sees buffers the Cleaner has already reclaimed. Sampling from INSIDE the writing thread discriminates cleanly, on a fixture of 3,267,392 bytes: pre-D15 shape (FileChannel write of a heap ByteBuffer) allocates 1 buffer / 3,145,728 bytes; the D15 FileOutputStream/BufferedWriter shape allocates 0 / 0. Running the actual function under review, persist-invoke-jobs-ledger!, on two independent cold threads: direct-pool delta 0 both times, 3,267,392 bytes written, round-trip equal. Instrument validated separately (ByteBuffer/allocateDirect of 4MB moves the same counter 0 -> 1 / 4194304), so the zero is an absence, not a dead probe. The delta of 0 covers the whole function including the closing directory FileChannel force, which is therefore also innocent. ENUMERATION CHECKED rather than trusted: grep -n -E 'FileChannel|getBytes|\\.write |spit |Files/write' src/futon3c/transport/http.clj returns 10 lines; the only surviving FileChannel uses are the import (:116), two mentions inside the new comment (:423,:427) and the directory force (:448), and every spit belongs to an unrelated store (arse entities :2526,:2539, backpacks :2763, session files :3480,:3624,:3652), so 'the ledger persist path touches no ByteBuffer' holds as an all-claim. Structure re-read by hand: tmp create, write+sync, :temp-forced hook, ATOMIC_MOVE+REPLACE_EXISTING, :renamed hook, directory force, committed?/durability error typing and finally deleteIfExists are all intact and in the same order; FileDescriptor.sync is fsync(2) like FileChannel.force(true), and it is reached after an explicit writer flush, so the pre-rename durability boundary is preserved. Incidental improvement worth naming: pr streams to the writer where pr-str materialised the whole ledger as a String and again as a byte array, so a 26MB ledger no longer needs ~50MB of heap to persist. ONE FINDING, FIXED BY THE REVIEWER (futon3c cc099006, path-scoped, test file only): the renamed regression asserts only round-trip and size, both of which the pre-D15 chunked path satisfies too -- it locks nothing about where the bytes were buffered, which is the entire subject of the row. This is the same criticism D13's review left as a residual ('the chunk test proves a 3MB ledger round-trips but not that the write was chunked'), and D15 inherited it unrepaired. Added persist-allocates-no-direct-buffers: samples the pool inside the writing thread, and runs a NEGATIVE CONTROL first that writes the same payload the old way, asserting the control allocated at least 1MB and printing a visible SKIP naming java.version if it did not -- so a future JDK that stops using temporary direct buffers makes the test say so instead of passing for the wrong reason. Post-fix in the same isolated worktree: 10 tests/42 assertions, 0 failures, exit 0; kondo 0/0; parens OK; all exit 0. DISCRIMINATION PROVEN, not assumed: with src/futon3c/transport/http.clj reverted to 5874b3f6^ the new test FAILS, and the number it fails with is itself the row's story -- 1,048,576 bytes, exactly D13's 1MB chunk cap, still cached per thread; D15 takes that to 0. http.clj was then restored byte-identical to 99095908 (git diff HEAD empty) and the suite re-run green before the worktree was removed. THREE RESIDUALS, typed not papered, none blocking: (a) the live-record pin rule has no purchase on either of these two tests and I am not pretending otherwise -- what they exercise is a byte COUNT and an allocation site, not record content, so the fixture is synthetic by necessity (the docstring I added says so); the pinned live record continues to sit in the D13 expiry test in the same file. (b) The second acceptance clause, that persists succeed from a cold thread against the REAL server, is still not demonstrated here and cannot be from this seat: /tmp/futon3c-invoke-jobs.edn is now 2089 bytes with mtime 2026-09-03T06:21:20Z, i.e. it turned over after D13's 06:05Z reload attempt recorded a 26,117,274-byte file and a failed persist, which is consistent with the JVM restart that D13's acceptance-reload note said was the only thing that could clear the legacy holders -- but I read a stat, not the JVM, so I record the file state and NOT a conclusion about the restart or about live dispatch health. The lane owner still owes the live confirmation. (c) D15 removes the mechanism permanently, which is the durable win, but note that the pool exhaustion it was written against was caused by holders that a restart clears anyway; the value of the change is that it cannot recur, not that it relieved the outage."
--
/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/labs/zaif-harness/runs/build-loop.log-18692-holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:140:   :review "Read cdabc41. Entry retires [:R2 :R7] (D5f's addition) and adds [:R8 :R7] :label eps with C10 as basis; the guard change (a :retires pair must be drawn OR decision-added; a pair nothing asserted is still refused, 4g) is the right generalisation and 4r pins both halves. Rendered: R2->R7 shows as a retired addition naming both decisions, R8->R7 as added (code), count 'decision-added 12 (9 code, 3 ruling; 1 conditional)'; the route-grain R2->R7 (measured) correctly stands. Suite PASS 14/10. The agent's tally -- four controls this session failing for changes they did not guard -- is a pattern worth a line in the TN: controls should pin the property, not the sentence." :owner :wm-organization
