-03ed-7c80-aa8a-0f71e0c3b3f5", :event-seq-range [406 432], :reasoning-encrypted-only-count 5, :tool-digest-count 6, :rollout-file "/home/joe/.codex/sessions/2026/07/22/rollout-2026-07-22T20-53-09-019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822.jsonl", :turn 3, :event :turn-round, :truncated? false, :message-count 6, :harvester-version 1, :reasoning-count 5, :profile :codex-rollout, :text "Codex rollout turn 3\n\nREASONING (plaintext available in rollout)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nThat concern is well-founded: P0 proves “can be stored,” but storage alone does not create a retrieval path. I’ll inspect the mission’s current retrieval stages and the existing pattern-selection machinery, then suggest the smallest concrete bridge from memories to patterns.\n\nThat concern is well-founded: P0 proves “can be stored,” but storage alone does not create a retrieval path. I’ll inspect the mission’s current retrieval stages and the existing pattern-selection machinery, then suggest the smallest concrete bridge from memories to patterns.\n\nThe code confirms the gap: today `psr_search` searches only the notions index, `pattern_memory` returns PSR/PUR/PAR records, and the boot packet contains no deliberate memories. So the four saved memories are retrievable only if an agent explicitly remembers to call `memory_search`; nothing currently makes them surface at the point of use.\n\nThe code confirms the gap: today `psr_search` searches only the notions index, `pattern_memory` returns PSR/PUR/PAR records, and the boot packet contains no deliberate memories. So the four saved memories are retrievable only if an agent explicitly remembers to call `memory_search`; nothing currently makes them surface at the point of use.\n\nYes — your confidence should currently be low.\n\nI checked the live store: there are nine `:memory/assert` hyperedges, including the four canonical Zai memories and replay duplicates, but none has a pattern endpoint. More importantly:\n\n- `psr_search` searches only the existing notions/pattern index.\n- `pattern_memory` retrieves PSR/PUR/PAR records, not deliberate memories.\n- The boot packet does not contain a memory projection.\n- Therefore a saved memory appears only if the agent independently decides to call `memory_search` with a good query.\n\nThat is storage, not operational memory.\n\nI think your “via patterns” idea gives us the right P1 architecture:\n\n```text\ncurrent task\n    ↓\npsr_search\n    ↓\ncandidate pattern\n    ↓\nmemories attached to that pattern\n    ↓\npsr_select returns pattern + relevant remembered cases\n    ↓\nagent applies one and cites its evidence id\n```\n\nThe key move is pattern-conditioned recall. A memory remains a concrete episode or fact; a pattern becomes its reusable retrieval handle.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nPattern: mathlib/normalize-field-expressions\n\nMemories:\n- e-6bcbb51e… field_simp did not clear this inverse form\n- e-d9b1739f… HasDerivAt.comp hit an npowRec definitional mismatch\n- e-3c8277ed… direct expansion worked after the composed route failed\n```\n\nWhen a later task causes that pattern to surface, its attached memories should arrive automatically. The solver should not need to remember that `memory_search` exists.\n\nI would revise P1 into a smaller, sharper rung:\n\n1. Give memories explicit pattern endpoints. `memory_record` already accepts `{:ref/type :pattern ...}` subjects, although a librarian may assign these after writing.\n\n2. Backfill the current memories. The existing nine edges need pattern assignments and duplicate curation; presently they connect only to things such as `Mathlib`, `apm/a94A06`, and the session.\n\n3. Enrich `psr_search`. Each pattern candidate should include a bounded list of attached memory hooks and evidence IDs.\n\n4. Make `psr_select` the reliable retrieval trigger. Selection should return the full bodies of perhaps the top three current memories attached to the selected pattern.\n\n5. Record use. PSR/PUR should carry `:memory-ids` so we can measure whether retrieval led to application rather than merely appearing in context.\n\nThere are then two complementary processes:\n\n- Memory → pattern: the librarian classifies concrete memories under reusable patterns.\n- Pattern → memory: ordinary pattern selection automatically retrieves relevant experience.\n\nFacets and FTS can remain useful fallbacks, but neither is as dependable as attaching recall to a workflow the agent is already instructed to perform. Boot projection alone would mostly surface recent memories; pattern-conditioned recall surfaces memories at the decision point where they matter.\n\nI would make the P1 acceptance test concrete: a fresh Lean problem surfaces a pattern through `psr_search`; selecting it returns one of the a94A06 memory IDs; the agent applies it and cites that ID. Until that works end-to-end, I would not claim that the system has memory in the practical sense.\n\nYes — your confidence should currently be low.\n\nI checked the live store: there are nine `:memory/assert` hyperedges, including the four canonical Zai memories and replay duplicates, but none has a pattern endpoint. More importantly:\n\n- `psr_search` searches only the existing notions/pattern index.\n- `pattern_memory` retrieves PSR/PUR/PAR records, not deliberate memories.\n- The boot packet does not contain a memory projection.\n- Therefore a saved memory appears only if the agent independently decides to call `memory_search` with a good query.\n\nThat is storage, not operational memory.\n\nI think your “via patterns” idea gives us the right P1 architecture:\n\n```text\ncurrent task\n    ↓\npsr_search\n    ↓\ncandidate pattern\n    ↓\nmemories attached to that pattern\n    ↓\npsr_select returns pattern + relevant remembered cases\n    ↓\nagent applies one and cites its evidence id\n```\n\nThe key move is pattern-conditioned recall. A memory remains a concrete episode or fact; a pattern becomes its reusable retrieval handle.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nPattern: mathlib/normalize-field-expressions\n\nMemories:\n- e-6bcbb51e… field_simp did not clear this inverse form\n- e-d9b1739f… HasDerivAt.comp hit an npowRec definitional mismatch\n- e-3c8277ed… direct expansion worked after the composed route failed\n```\n\nWhen a later task causes that pattern to surface, its attached memories should arrive automatically. The solver should not need to remember that `memory_search` exists.\n\nI would revise P1 into a smaller, sharper rung:\n\n1. Give memories explicit pattern endpoints. `memory_record` already accepts `{:ref/type :pattern ...}` subjects, although a librarian may assign these after writing.\n\n2. Backfill the current memories. The existing nine edges need pattern assignments and duplicate curation; presently they connect only to things such as `Mathlib`, `apm/a94A06`, and the session.\n\n3. Enrich `psr_search`. Each pattern candidate should include a bounded list of attached memory hooks and evidence IDs.\n\n4. Make `psr_select` the reliable retrieval trigger. Selection should return the full bodies of perhaps the top three current memories attached to the selected pattern.\n\n5. Record use. PSR/PUR should carry `:memory-ids` so we can measure whether retrieval led to application rather than merely appearing in context.\n\nThere are then two complementary processes:\n\n- Memory → pattern: the librarian classifies concrete memories under reusable patterns.\n- Pattern → memory: ordinary pattern selection automatically retrieves relevant experience.\n\nFacets and FTS can remain useful fallbacks, but neither is as dependable as attaching recall to a workflow the agent is already instructed to perform. Boot projection alone would mostly surface recent memories; pattern-conditioned recall surfaces memories at the decision point where they matter.\n\nI would make the P1 acceptance test concrete: a fresh Lean problem surfaces a pattern through `psr_search`; selecting it returns one of the a94A06 memory IDs; the 