ed at the wrong grain”: today the WM chooses a scheduler action first and constructs only one cascade afterward. So a cascade-level prior currently has no policy set on which to operate. I’m tracing the existing multi-cascade machinery now; compliance will require an explicit hierarchical boundary, not merely renaming the present habit field.\n\nThe first structural finding is stronger than “\\(E\\) is attached at the wrong grain”: today the WM chooses a scheduler action first and constructs only one cascade afterward. So a cascade-level prior currently has no policy set on which to operate. I’m tracing the existing multi-cascade machinery now; compliance will require an explicit hierarchical boundary, not merely renaming the present habit field.\n\nI’ve found a usable reduction-safe seam. The offline “slush” and arguing-worlds work already proves that multiple diverse cascade candidates can exist, but the live constructor still returns exactly one. I’m going to land the compliance foundation as a dark, pure cascade-policy prior with topology-sensitive identities and tests, plus a mission contract that forbids calling it live until the WM supplies a genuine same-mission candidate set and cascade score. This changes no scheduled behavior.\n\nI’ve found a usable reduction-safe seam. The offline “slush” and arguing-worlds work already proves that multiple diverse cascade candidates can exist, but the live constructor still returns exactly one. I’m going to land the compliance foundation as a dark, pure cascade-policy prior with topology-sensitive identities and tests, plus a mission contract that forbids calling it live until the WM supplies a genuine same-mission candidate set and cascade score. This changes no scheduled behavior.\n\nThe first reduction run caught a namespace-shadowing bug in the new pure kernel (`keys` was used as a local name and then called as the map-key function). That is confined to the dark code; I’m correcting it and will run the focused tests