nce model, while `strategy × central × feasible` is better treated as a factorized mission proposal/value model than quietly relabelled \\(E\\). I’ll locate the deferred mission and trace those factors through the live scorer, then extend the compliance mission with an explicit mission-pattern layer before advancing persistence.\n\nYes—this changes the architecture in an important way. The scheduler prior should not simply become a second copy of cascade habit: mission selection needs its own generative vocabulary and evidence model, while `strategy × central × feasible` is better treated as a factorized mission proposal/value model than quietly relabelled \\(E\\). I’ll locate the deferred mission and trace those factors through the live scorer, then extend the compliance mission with an explicit mission-pattern layer before advancing persistence.\n\nThe code settles one historical ambiguity: the live mission model is currently an additive weighted blend, not a product. The handoff explicitly rejected multiplication because strategy-spine missions can have zero measured centrality, so a raw product erases them. But your larger point stands: additive rescue is still an engineering utility, and it does not tell us what a mission is as a strategic policy.\n\nThe principled split I’m developing is: typed p4ng control-pattern relations generate and organize the strategic mission landscape; a mission-specific evidence/feasibility model scores it; \\(E_S\\) records strategic habit only. A dedicated embedding can help propose relations, but typed hypergraph links and retrieved memories must supply the auditable support.\n\nThe code settles one historical ambiguity: the live mission model is currently an additive weighted blend, not a product. The handoff explicitly rejected multiplication because strategy-spine missions can have zero measured centrality, so a raw product erases them. But your larger point stands: additive rescue is still an engineering utility, and it does 