ve engineering model** — open-mission /home/joe/code/futon2/holes/wm-bas…\"}"}, :session-id "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :tags [:codex :turn-round :codex-rollout :codex-4], :at "2026-07-23T02:44:36.840Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :agent, :id "codex-4"}, :author "codex-4", :claim-type :step, :id "e-codexroll-019f8b63-t016"} #:evidence{:body {:session-uuid "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :turn-id "019f8ce4-34cb-7662-b5ca-45498bd6db37", :event-seq-range [1095 1137], :reasoning-encrypted-only-count 8, :tool-digest-count 15, :rollout-file "/home/joe/.codex/sessions/2026/07/22/rollout-2026-07-22T20-53-09-019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822.jsonl", :turn 17, :event :turn-round, :truncated? false, :message-count 6, :harvester-version 1, :reasoning-count 8, :profile :codex-rollout, :text "Codex rollout turn 17\n\nREASONING (plaintext available in rollout)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nI think that is the stronger interpretation: the failed product is evidence that its factors were standing in for missing outer-loop structure. I’ll revise the plan so Zaif-style mission memories and actual control-pattern cascades are primary, while the special embedding remains only an optional exploratory instrument.\n\nI think that is the stronger interpretation: the failed product is evidence that its factors were standing in for missing outer-loop structure. I’ll revise the plan so Zaif-style mission memories and actual control-pattern cascades are primary, while the special embedding remains only an optional exploratory instrument.\n\nThat reframing changes the order of work materially: direct patterned recall is now the acceptance path; the embedding experiment cannot block it. Centrality is treated as a hypothesis to ablate—and likely retire—once outer cascades, dependencies, and episodic evidence exist.\n\nThat reframing changes the order of work materially: direct patterned recall is now the acceptance path; the embedding experiment cannot block it. Centrality is treated as a hypothesis to ablate—and likely retire—once outer cascades, dependencies, and episodic evidence exist.\n\nI agree. The three factors now look less like ingredients of a missing formula and more like lossy proxies for missing outer-loop objects:\n\n- `centrality` approximates dependencies and control relevance.\n- `strategic` approximates membership in an outer control cascade.\n- `doable` approximates recalled transition/outcome evidence.\n- A zero was ambiguously standing for “absent,” “unknown,” “blocked,” or “not measured.”\n\nThe more principled route is therefore:\n\n`current control deficit → p4ng control-pattern cascade → direct Zaif-style memory recall → mission candidates with reasons → strategic forward model → selection`\n\nHere the WM writes its own concrete episodic memories, connected to both missions and p4ng patterns. The outer cascade supplies composition, ordering, explicit holes, and explanations. Centrality then becomes something to ablate and probably retire—not something we must rescue mathematically.\n\nI updated [M-wm-strategic-mission-selection.md](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/missions/M-wm-strategic-mission-selection.md:112) accordingly:\n\n- Direct WM memory retrieval is now foundational.\n- Outer-loop control cascades precede the embedding experiment.\n- The acceptance test requires retrieving an episode and related mission without vector search.\n- The special embedding is now an optional S2b experiment whose failure cannot block the architecture.\n- A missing connection remains an explicit policy hole, never a zero score.\n\nThe corresponding argument is now in [main-2026.tex](/home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex:510). The paper still builds successfully at 28 pages.\n\nOnly the theory and staged plan have changed here; the actual WM memory writer/query path and outer-cascade constructor remain the next implementation slice.\n\nI agree. The three factors now look less like ingredients of a mi