ore needs explicit domain and witness boundaries, but not separate schemas. I’ve made the testing ladder explicit: pure projections → mathematics runners → dark WM decisions → live WM clicks. That lets mathematical work falsify common infrastructure assumptions cheaply without pretending a theorem-proving success validates WM mission value.\n\nYes—this is a genuine unification: Zaif and the WM become different inhabitants of one learning architecture, rather than separate systems borrowing metaphors from each other.\n\nThe “nomad” interpretation is productive in a concrete sense: Zaif runners move across problem territories, enact patterns, and leave typed/revisable traces in shared infrastructure. Mathematics becomes the inexpensive experimental domain for testing:\n\n- Memory realization and durable writing.\n- Pattern-conditioned retrieval.\n- Whether recalled memories are actually cited and used.\n- Cascade composition and policy holes.\n- Correction, challenge, and supersession.\n- Calibration and retrieval latency.\n\nThe evidential boundary remains important: shared storage does not mean theorem-proving success certifies a WM mission preference or transition prediction. Domain and witness endpoints keep that distinction inspectable.\n\nI recorded this in:\n\n- [CODEX-HANDOFF-p0-memory-record.md](/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/CODEX-HANDOFF-p0-memory-record.md:60)\n- [M-typed-memories.md](/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md:89)\n- [M-wm-strategic-mission-selection.md](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/missions/M-wm-strategic-mission-selection.md:142)\n- [main-2026.tex](/home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex:512)\n\nThe shared validation ladder is now explicit:\n\n`pure tests → frequent mathematics runs → dark WM decisions → live WM clicks`\n\nP0 remains deliberately narrow; we have not destabilized its accepted contract. The next implementation seam is the common endpoint-retrieval and memory-use receipt path—not a separate WM memory writer. The paper