n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nI’ll start with the store seam: locate the existing hyperedge query and its HTTP/client contracts, add bounded `end + optional type` behavior with regression tests, then build the shared compact projection/use-receipt contract on top. I’ll keep all consumers fixture-only in this phase.\n\nI’ll start with the store seam: locate the existing hyperedge query and its HTTP/client contracts, add bounded `end + optional type` behavior with regression tests, then build the shared compact projection/use-receipt contract on top. I’ll keep all consumers fixture-only in this phase.\n\nThe store fix is small but important: when `end` is present it now owns the query branch, and `type` becomes a conjunctive predicate inside that bounded endpoint query. This avoids the previous whole-type path and client-side filtering.\n\nThe store fix is small but important: when `end` is present it now owns the query branch, and `type` becomes a conjunctive predicate inside that bounded endpoint query. This avoids the previous whole-type path and client-side filtering.\n\nThe shared contract is now a pure namespace owned by futon2 but consumed unchanged by futon3c. It projects audit metadata only—no body-derived value or score—and a use receipt cannot claim use of a memory that was never surfaced.\n\nThe shared contract is now a pure namespace owned by futon2 but consumed unchanged by futon3c. It projects audit metadata only—no body-derived value or score—and a use receipt cannot claim use of a memory that was never surfaced.\n\nThe code-level Phase 1 seam is green. I’m now checking whether the serving store can support a bounded read-only verification without reloading it; because the route implementation is not yet deployed, I’ll distinguish current-live behavior from the tested next code rather than blur them.\n\nThe code-level Phase 1 seam is green. I’m now checking whether the serving store can support a bounded read-only verification without reloading it; because the route implementation is not yet deployed, I’ll distinguish current-live behavior from the tested next code rather than blur them.\n\nThe live read-only probe confirmed the deployment gap exactly: the current server still ignores `end` when `type` is also supplied and returned three unrelated memory edges. The patched in-process server returns the single conjunctive match. I’m leaving the serving JVM untouched and recording deployment as the remaining Phase 1 gate.\n\nThe live read-only probe confirmed the deployment gap exactly: the current server still ignores `end` when `type` is also supplied and returned three unrelated memory edges. The patched in-process server returns the single conjunctive match. I’m leaving the serving JVM untouched and recording deployment as the remaining Phase 1 gate.\n\nStatic gates are clean. I’m running the broader focused consumer set now, including the existing Zaif API tests, so the new controller-stamped domain cannot hide behind the new unit tests alone.\n\nStatic gates are clean. I’m running the broader focused consumer set now, including the existing Zaif API tests, so the new controller-stamped domain cannot hide behind the new unit tests alone.\n\nPhase 1 is implemented and locally verified, but not yet promoted on the serving JVM.\n\nImplemented:\n\n- Conjunctive bounded `end + type` queries in [futon1b_graph.clj](/home/joe/code/futon1b/futon1b_graph.clj:506) and documented in [API-CONTRACT.md](/home/joe/code/futon1b/API-CONTRACT.md:325).\n- Shared compact memory projection and use-receipt validation in [memory_contract.clj](/home/joe/code/futon2/src/futon2/aif/memory_contract.clj:31).\n- Dual mathematics/WM fixtures in [shared_memory_contract_fixtures.edn](/home/joe/code/futon2/fixtures/shared_memory_contract_fixtures.edn).\n- Bounded, domain-isolated recall client in [memory_recall.clj](/home/joe/code/futon3c/src/futon3c/peripheral/memory_recall.clj:17).\n- Controller-stamped memory domains; payloads cannot smuggle them.\n- Explicit exclusion of cross-domain, retracted, superseded, malformed, and body-less records.\n- Query caps, elapsed-time reporting, and store failures returned as data.\n\nVerification:\n\n- futon1b HTTP suite: 53/53 passed.\n- futon2 focused suite: 8 tests / 29 assertions passed.\n- futon3c focused suite: 17 tests / 260 assertions passed.\n- Kondo: zero errors/warnings on changed files.\n- Parentheses/EDN checks: clean.\n- No JVM restart or live mutation.\n\nA bounded live probe exposed the expected deployment gap: the current `:7073` server still follows the old type-first conditional and returned three unrelated memories when given an exact memory endpoint plus `type=memory/assert`. The patched in-process server returns exactly the conjunctive match.\n\nThis evidence is recorded in [M-shared-memory-control-build-test.md](/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/missions/M-shared-memory-control-build-test.md:76). Phase 1’s remaining gate is a controlled serving deployment followed by live mathematics and WM-domain recall checks. No commits were created.\n\nPhase 1 is implemented and locally verified, but not yet promoted on the serving JVM.\n\nImplemented:\n\n- Conjunctive bounded `end + type` queries in [futon1b_graph.clj](/home/joe/code/futon1b/futon1b_graph.clj:506) and documented in [API-CONTRACT.md](/home/joe/code/futon1b/API-CONTRACT.md:325).\n- Shared compact memory projection and use-receipt validation in [memory_contract.clj](/home/joe/code/futon2/src/futon2/aif/memory_contract.clj:31).\n- Dual mathematics/WM fixtures in [shared_memory_contract_fixtures.edn](/home/joe/code/futon2/fixtures/shared_memory_contract_fixtures.edn).\n- Bounded, domain-isolated recall client in [memory_recall.clj](/home/joe/code/futon3c/src/futon3c/peripheral/memory_recall.clj:17).\n- Controller-stamped memory domains; payloads cannot smuggle them.\n- Explicit exclusion of cross-domain, retracted, superse