\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nI’ve resumed at the Phase 3 boundary. I’m first pinning down futon1b’s actual temporal-query semantics, because that determines whether correction can be genuinely bitemporal or only simulated in the controller; then I’ll wire the bounded trial corpus through the same live recall/use/outcome path.\n\nI’ve resumed at the Phase 3 boundary. I’m first pinning down futon1b’s actual temporal-query semantics, because that determines whether correction can be genuinely bitemporal or only simulated in the controller; then I’ll wire the bounded trial corpus through the same live recall/use/outcome path.\n\nThe seam is clearer now: the live authoritative graph is futon1b/XTDB 2, and its HTTP API currently exposes only “now.” The older futon1a accepts valid-time writes but likewise has no HTTP as-of read. So Phase 3’s first real substrate change is justified: add a bounded `as-of` query basis to endpoint/type reads, rather than pretending that timestamps stored in edge properties are time travel.\n\nThe seam is clearer now: the live authoritative graph is futon1b/XTDB 2, and its HTTP API currently exposes only “now.” The older futon1a accepts valid-time writes but likewise has no HTTP as-of read. So Phase 3’s first real substrate change is justified: add a bounded `as-of` query basis to endpoint/type reads, rather than pretending that timestamps stored in edge properties are time travel.\n\nThe temporal spike has passed against an in-memory XTDB 2 node: the same hyperedge is `:current` before its correction-valid-time and `:superseded` now. I’m now joining that substrate primitive to the memory contract. The correction will be append-first: challenge and correcting episode remain immutable evidence; only the attachment projection is versioned or ended.\n\nThe temporal spike has passed against an in-memory XTDB 2 node: the same hyperedge is `:current` before its correction-valid-time and `:superseded` now. I’m now joining that substrate primitive to the memory contract. The correction will be append-first: challenge and correcting episode remain immutable evidence; only the attachment projection is versioned or ended.\n\nThe controller tests now cover the key failure semantics: an externally failed use produces a typed `:challenge`, leaves the original episode intact, and does not manufacture a success label. This exposed and fixed a real contract omission too: the evidence vocabulary lacked the IATC `challenge` act and even lacked `:memory` as an artifact reference, despite the memory theory requiring both.\n\nThe controller tests now cover the key failure semantics: an externally failed use produces a typed `:challenge`, leaves the original episode intact, and does not manufacture a success label. This exposed and fixed a real contract omission too: the evidence vocabulary lacked the IATC `challenge` act and even lacked `:memory` as an artifact reference, despite the memory theory requiring both.\n\nThe Phase 2 residual is no longer merely catalogued: the new bounded route finds the reviewed memory by FTS, follows its current `memory/assert` edge, and proposes `math-formalization/tactic-algebra-interference` at rank 1. The old lexical-index candidates remain visible behind it, so this is additive candidate construction, not an embedding substitution. I’m deploying the separate futon1b service now so the lifecycle’s as-of behavior is live as well.\n\nThe Phase 2 residual is no longer merely catalogued: the new bounded route finds the reviewed memory by FTS, follows its current `memory/assert` edge, and proposes `math-formalization/tactic-algebra-interference` at rank 1. The old lexical-index candidates remain visible behind it, so this is additive candidate construction, not an embedding substitution. I’m deploying the separate futon1b service now so the lifecycle’s as-of behavior is live as well.\n\nA complete live correction now exists in the shared backend: original `e-c9edf4a4…`, correction `e-d0b4ed4d…`, challenge `e-f4511b78…`, with a measured 4.05 s correction lag. An as-of read just before correction returns only the original version; current returns original as superseded plus the correction as current. I’ve also created `zai-7` for the bounded mathematics trials and hot-loaded the Phase 3 controller code.\n\nA complete live correction now exists in the shared backend: original `e-c9edf4a4…`, correction `e-d0b4ed4d…`, challenge `e-f4511b78…`, with a measured 4.05 s correction lag. An as-of read just before correction returns only the original version; current returns original as superseded plus the correction as current. I’ve also created `zai-7` for the bounded mathematics trials and hot-loaded the Phase 3 controller code.\n\nTrial 1 is still running inside its bounded turn. Its contract is deliberately split at the trust boundary: the runner may construct and locally test the proof, but it must stop before PUR; I will rerun Lean independently, persist that checker outcome, then give the runner the outcome ID for its use receipt.\n\nTrial 1 is still running inside its bounded turn. Its contract is deliberately split at the trust boundary: the runner may construct and locally test the proof, but it must stop before PUR; I will rerun Lean independently, persist that checker outcome, then give the runner the outcome ID for its use receipt.\n\nThe live job is healthy in Agency (`running`, one nonterminal job, no queue buildup), though it has not emitted its terminal response yet. The substrate side is not scanning: candidate construction made one bounded FTS request and only bounded endpoint reads for retained memory entries.\n\nThe live job is healthy in Agency (`running`, one nonterminal job, no queue buildup), though it has not emitted its terminal response yet. The substrate side is not scanning: candidate construction made one bounded FTS request and only bounded endpoint reads for retained memory entries.\n\nThe independent checker caught a useful negative case while the runner is still iterating: its first theorem statement was false, so Lean left a real unsolved goal. I’m preserving that as a failed trial outcome rather than “fixing” the evidence. The runner can still revise its artifact; the independent result remains an auditable failed attempt.\n\nThe independent checker caught a useful negative case while the runner is still iterating: its first theorem statement was false, so Lean left a real unsolved goal. I’m preserving that as a failed trial outcome rather than “fixing” the evidence. The runner can still revise its artifact; the independent result remains an auditable failed attempt.\n\nTOOL DIGEST\n{:kind :function_call, :name \"update_plan\", :call-id \"call_eHzwTdSlNzpS2ZxdJm5RvE69\", :args \"{\\\"explanation\\\":\\\"Resuming Phase 3 from the completed Phase 2 state; the first decision is the existing substrate’s temporal-query capability.\\\",\\\"plan\\\":[{\\\"step\\\":\\\"Freeze the Phase 3 baseline and inspect t…\"}\n{:kind :function_call_output, :call-id \"call_eHzwTdSlNzpS2ZxdJm5RvE69\", :output \"Plan updated\"}\n{:kind :function_call, :name \"exec_command\", :call-id \"call_B3MVK92dTK3vkbEq0Gz1K4pF\", :args \"{\\\"cmd\\\":\\\"sed -n '270,420p' API-CONTRACT.md && rg -n \\\\\\\"as-of|valid-time|system-time|basis|hyperedges-query|defn.*query\\\\\\\" . --glob '*.clj' --glob '*.md'\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/futon1b\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms…\"}\n{:kind :function_call_output, :call-id \"call_B3MVK92dTK3vkbEq0Gz1K4pF\", :output \"Chunk ID: 497ac0 Wall time: 0.0000 seconds Process exited with code 0 Original token count: 2644 Output: using `f1w/upsert-hyperedge-doc` (`futon1_write.clj:230-287`). - Penholder: `compat-penholder`.…\"}\n{:kind :function_call, :name \"exec_command\", :call-id \"call_7Lpspp5KoLtITOKeAnRi6Dau\", :args \"{\\\"cmd\\\":\\\"sed -n '360,420p' src/futon1b/app.clj 2>/dev/null || sed -n '360,420p' app.clj\\\\nsed -n '480,650p' futon1b_graph.clj\\\\nsed -n '1010,1260p' routes