ntegrable split rather than an illicit inferred hypothesis.", :body {:hook "Compact support without measurability does not force Bochner integrability: prove the integrable branch by bounded-support domination, and show the nonintegrable branch makes every defining integral zero.", :capture-kind :runner-integrability-split-and-ground-control-axiom-verification, :problem-class "Prove an integral-defined transform is entire from compact support when the statement does not separately assume measurability or integrability.", :key-api ["IsCompact.bddAbove" "hasDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_loc_of_deriv_le" "integral_undef" "analyticOn_const"], :witness {:declaration "laplace_transform_entire_of_compact_support", :axioms ["propext" "Classical.choice" "Quot.sound"], :sorryax false, :statement "`compactSupportOnHalfLine f → AnalyticOn ℂ (laplaceTransform f) Set.univ`"}, :semantic-split {:integrable-branch "Compact support supplies bounded derivative dominators; ordinary parametric integration carries the proof.", :nonintegrable-branch "Mathlib's totalized Bochner integral makes every nonintegrable defining integral zero, so the transform is identically zero and hence analytic."}, :rule "When an integral transform theorem omits measurability, audit the totalized integral semantics explicitly: a proof may need an integrable/nonintegrable split rather than an illicit inferred hypothesis.", :boundary "The nonintegrable branch proves the formal statement through Mathlib's zero convention; it does not claim the classical transform of a nonmeasurable function has analytic content.", :how-to-apply ["Split on `Integrable f` for the positive-half-line measure instead of silently assuming compact support supplies measurability." "In the integrable branch, bound `t`, the exponential factor, and the derivative factor uniformly on the compact support." "Use local dominated differentiation at each complex parameter to obtain entire analyticity." "In the nonintegrable branch, prove multiplication by the nonvanishing exponential kernel cannot make the product integrable." "Apply `integral_undef` at every parameter, rewrite the Laplace transform as the zero function, and use analyticity of constants."]}, :promotion {:reviewed-by "claude-9", :verdict :approve, :warrant "operator-delegated: in-use evaluation (proof-in-pudding; owner draft review claude-6 2026-07-28; Joe's standing direction)"}}, :session-id "M-codex-sorry-loop/duree-lane", :tags [:memory :mathematics :codex-pilot :solve-lane], :at "2026-07-30T23:09:59.842593760Z", :type :memory, :subject #:ref{:type :mission, :id "M-codex-sorry-loop"}, :author "codex-5", :claim-type :assert, :id "e-codexpilot-split-compact-support-Laplace-entireness-on-integrability"}} {:score -15.312539950595976, :entry #:evidence{:body {:event :turn-round, :text "The `hodd` proof now compiles completely! The only remaining error is the outer `sorry` at line 146 — the main theorem body. Now I need to construct `g` and prove the remaining properties.\n\nThe full theorem requires:\n1. `∃ g, AnalyticOn ℂ g (ball 0 1) ∧ ∀ z, ‖z‖ < 1 → g (z²) = h z`\n\nWith `hodd` proven, I need to construct `g`. I'll define `g` via the power series of `h` restricted to even coefficients, show it's analytic on a neighborhood of 0, prove `g(z²) = h(z)` near 0, then extend via the identity theorem.\n\nBut the identity theorem approach requires both `g ∘ (·^2)` and `h` to be analytic on the ball and agree near 0. Since the ball is preconnected, they agree everywhere.\n\nHowever, constructing `g` properly is still complex. Let me think about a shortcut: since the theorem is only needed for the specific `apm_a96J10_f`, and the `apm_a96J10_main` theorem calls `even_factors`, I could try a more direct approach for the main body.\n\nActually, let me try a different approach entirely for the outer proof. Instead of constructing `g` via formal power series, I can define `g` using the identity theorem on a half-disk. But that's also complex.\n\nLet me 