. Updated the following files: M /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a01A09/lean/Main.lean \"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"apply_patch\", :call-id \"call_QTbObyZI59hPD6Vp0Bh8VN9z\", :args \"*** Begin Patch *** Delete File: /tmp/A09Scratch.lean *** Add File: /tmp/A09Scratch.lean +import Mathlib + +open scoped Real MeasureTheory Topology +open Complex MeasureTheory Set Filter + +#check Rea…\"}\n{:kind :patch-apply, :call-id \"call_QTbObyZI59hPD6Vp0Bh8VN9z\", :status :success, :files [:/tmp/A09Scratch.lean], :stdout \"Success. Updated the following files: A /tmp/A09Scratch.lean D /tmp/A09Scratch.lean \", :stderr \"\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_QTbObyZI59hPD6Vp0Bh8VN9z\", :output \"Exit code: 0 Wall time: 0 seconds Output: Success. Updated the following files: A /tmp/A09Scratch.lean D /tmp/A09Scratch.lean \"}\n{:kind :function_call, :name \"exec_command\", :call-id \"call_MYZzoQykVgwUcidgQTe30aI2\", :args \"{\\\"cmd\\\":\\\"lake env lean /tmp/A09Scratch.lean\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/apm-lean\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":30000,\\\"max_output_tokens\\\":16000}\"}\n{:kind :function_call_output, :call-id \"call_MYZzoQykVgwUcidgQTe30aI2\", :output \"Chunk ID: e0c5ff Wall time: 6.6087 seconds Process exited with code 1 Original token count: 765 Output: /tmp/A09Scratch.lean:6:7: error(lean.unknownIdentifier): Unknown constant `Real.HolderConjugate.…\"}\n{:kind :function_call, :name \"exec_command\", :call-id \"call_iSKeGIicXNr7qIRSNRPCpiEz\", :args \"{\\\"cmd\\\":\\\"rg -n \\\\\\\"HolderConjugate.*HolderTriple|HolderTriple.*ofReal|instance.*HolderTriple|def.*HolderTriple\\\\\\\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Data/ENNReal .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib | head -160\\\\nse…\"}\n{:kind :function_call_output, :call-id \"call_iSKeGIicXNr7qIRSNRPCpiEz\", :output \"Chunk ID: 0dc8a4 Wall time: 0.0000 seconds Process exited with code 0 Original token count: 2973 Output: .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Data/ENNReal/Holder.lean:12:This file defines a new class: `ENNR…\"}\n{:kind :function_call, :name \"exec_command\", :call-id \"call_FIUrpfoMdm3ipFq25ietXzR3\", :args \"{\\\"cmd\\\":\\\"sed -n '420,455p' .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Data/Real/ConjExponents.lean\\\\nsed -n '40,85p' .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Analysis/SpecialFunctions/ImproperIntegrals.lean\\\\nsed -n '675,690p…\"}\n{:kind :function_call_output, :call-id \"call_FIUrpfoMdm3ipFq25ietXzR3\", :output \"Chunk ID: deff85 Wall time: 0.0000 seconds Process exited with code 0 Original token count: 1119 Output: obtain ⟨hp, hq⟩ : p ≠ 0 ∧ q ≠ 0 := by constructor all_goals rintro rfl apply hr exact_mod_cast …\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"apply_patch\", :call-id \"call_eUJNasNZ81a5LF8wBh1zZ5IX\", :args \"*** Begin Patch *** Update File: /tmp/A09Scratch.lean @@ #check Real.HolderConjugate.conjExponent +#check Real.HolderConjugate.one_lt +#check Real.HolderConjugate.symm +#check Real.HolderConjugate.non…\"}\n{:ki\n[TRUNCATED AT 16KB BODY CAP]"}, :session-id "019fa2c1-cd5a-73c0-abe5-82a329a2ef2e", :tags [:codex :turn-round :codex-rollout :codex-7], :at "2026-07-30T22:08:26.797Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :agent, :id "codex-7"}, :author "codex-7", :claim-type :step, :id "e-codexroll-019fa2c1-t043"}} {:score -1.3198634474245161, :entry #:evidence{:in-reply-to "emacs-c9ac3e4bca63c1dafa298b3fb4638838", :body {:event "chat-turn", :transport "emacs-claude-repl", :role "assistant", :turn-id "claude-9-turn-141", :text "Good — the park chain has been the mechanism all evening, so it can keep running unattended. Let me make it robust and leave you something readable in the morning. First the protocol doc, so a continuation (or a fresh session) can pick it up without reconstructing context:Now dispatching. Running **two proving runs in parallel** to roughly double overnight throughput — different rows, different runners, so no file conflict. Pre-checking both packets before either bell:Armed. Two runs in flight and the chain is self-sustaining.\n\n**What's running:** a01A06 (codex-7) and a01A07 (codex-6) in parallel — different files, so no conflict — parked awaiting both (`park-8ef4bc02`, 45-min deadline). I switched to two-at-a-time because sequential dispatch was ~4 round trips per problem, which won't clear 49 rows overnight. Both packets passed the pre-dispatch asserts *before* either bell: not blocked, no unreplaced markers, and hint lines 160 and 84 each verified to contain a real `sorry`.\n\n**The contract is written down** — `holes/ops/RESUME-overnight-2026-07-30.md`. It carries the state, the chain mechanism, stop conditions, the three blocked rows, the pre-dispatch and verification checklists, and the receipt rules, so a continuation or a fresh session can inherit the role without reconstructing tonight.\n\nThe one invariant I wrote as an imperative rather than a checklist item: **a turn that does not park ends the loop silently.** Everything else is recoverable — a lost bellback is caught by the deadline wake, a bad row by the pre-dispatch asserts, usage and queue exhaustion by `duree_dispatch.py` (exit 10 / exit 11, not to be bypassed). Only a missing park is unrecoverable.\n\nThree things from today are encoded so they don't depend on my remembering them:\n\n- **Read deleted lines, don't grep-count them** — prose comments contain \"theorem\" and \"lemma\"; that produced four false alarms today.\n- **A failed recall is `:NOT-MEASURABLE`, never zero** — with `:metric-3-exclude-from-benchmark true`. Recording one as zero is what produced this afternoon's false architectural diagnosis.\n- **The scribe's drafting judgement beats mine** — seven declines, seven correct. Supply evidence, let it judge, record declines as good outcomes.\n\n**In the morning:** there'll be a summary at `holes/ops/OVERNIGHT-SUMMARY-2026-07-31.md` with failures and uncertainties first, and the ⊸ register grows as it goes. To check in directly:\n\n```\nscripts/count_sorries.sh --corpus\ncurl -s localhost:7070/api/alpha/parked | python3 -m json.tool\ntail -40 holes/ops/claude-6.md\n```\n\nOne thing to watch that I can't fix from here: the store was ~7 GB RSS and climbing, and it regrew +4.3 GB in six hours after your restart. If it OOMs, recall fails — but the packets now say so explicitly and runners won't misreport it as a terrain gap, so the loop degrades honestly rather than silently. I won't restart it.\n\nStarting from 81/135 clean, 146 sorries. Good night.That changes an instruction in the overnight contract — the RESUME doc currently says not to restart the store and to report it instead. That would have governed the loop all night. Fixing that first, keeping the serving JVM distinction intact:Confirmed — `futon1b-server` MainPID is the store I've been tracking (7.13 GB after 8.5 h). Updating the contract, with a decision rule so the overnight loop doesn't restart reflexively:The park list is **empty** — but I parked `park-8ef4bc02` minutes ago awaiting both jobs. If that's real, nothing will wake me when they finish and the chain is already broken. Investigating before anything else:False alarm on the park, but a real problem with what I gave you. The park is alive — `?agent=claude-9` shows `park-8ef4bc02` awaiting both jobs with a sane deadline (~43 min out). **The bare `/api/alpha/parked` endpoint returns `[]` and only the agent-filtered query shows it** — so the visibility command I handed you would have told you the loop was dead when it wasn't. Fixing that:Store restart authorised and folded into the contract — thank you, that removes the one condition I couldn't recover from.\n\n**The correction mattered more than the permission.** The RESUME doc told the overnight loop *\"do not restart the serving JVM or the store, report it\"* — that instruction would have governed every hour of tonight and left the loop degrading around something recoverable. Now split properly: `futon1b-server` (7073/7072) is restartable; the futon3c serving JVM (7070/6768/3100) stays under I-0.\n\nI wrote a **decision rule** rather than a bare permission, so it doesn't restart reflexively: two consecutive dispatches recording `:store-unavailable`/`:recall-error`/`:timeout`, **or** RSS > ~10 GB, **or** host available < ~4 GB. Plus two things learned tonight — prefer the gap *between* runs, since runners issue error-recall mid-run and a restart fails those; and health-check `:7073` for a 200 before dispatching, because early post-restart reads can 502, and dispatching into that window would manufacture exactly the false \"recall empty\" this whole evening was spent eliminating.\n\nI left it alone for now: 7.13 GB RSS, host 14.4 GB available, below all three thresholds, and two runs in flight. Recorded what a restart does and doesn't buy — resets RSS and metaspace, but returns list reads to an already-slow baseline. **Restart to reclaim memory, not to fix latency.**\n\n**And running my own instructions before leaving caught a bad one.** The operator-visibility command I gave you — `curl -s localhost:7070/api/alpha/parked` — returns `{\"parked\":[]}` even with a live park. Only `?agent=claude-9` shows it. You'd have checked in the morning, seen an empty list, and concluded the chain had died. Both forms are now in the doc with the misleading one labelled.\n\nThat's the same failure shape as the recall-status collapse three hours ago: **a surface that reports \"nothing\" for two different reasons — nothing exists, versus nothing matched an unstated filter — gets read as the alarming one.** Twice today that ambiguity has done real inferential damage. Worth deciding later whether the unfiltered endpoint should return all parks or refuse the query.\n\n**Armed and verified:** `park-8ef4bc02` awaiting both jobs, deadline 2470 s out, a01A06 and a01A07 both running. Corpus at 81/135 clean, 146 sorries.\n\nMorning check:\n\n```\ncurl -s 'localhost:7070/api/alpha/parked?agent=claude-9' | python3 -m json.tool\nscripts/count_sorries.sh --corpus\ncat holes/ops/OVERNIGHT-SUMMARY-2026-07-31.md\n```\n\nGood night.Items 1–5 and 8 I completed across the last two turns. **Harvest and scribe are owed.** Finding both sessions by commit sha, not mtime:Five new turn-rounds — but these sessions are *also* running the current pair, so some may belong to in-flight work. Verifying each body before citing any:Third time this check has earned itself: **`019fa2c1-t043` is a01A09 — the *in-flight* run**, not a01A06. Citing it would have handed the scribe a run that hasn't been verified yet. Dispatching with only the verified ids:I fumbled that park command — it may have created one awaiting a nonexistent job id, which is a mistake I've made before. Checking and fixing:`park-21a2eb14` is awaiting the literal string `SCRIBE29` — a fabricated id, exactly the mistake I made earlier today. Creating the correct park and trying to retire the bogus one:Harvest and scribe are now done, and the checks caught two more things.\n\n**The turn-round body check caught a third misattribution, subtler than the last two.** Harvesting both sessions produced five new turn-rounds; `019fa2c1-t043` sits right alongside a01A06's t041/t042 but its body is **a01A09 — the run still in flight**. Citing it would have handed the scribe an unverified, half-finished run. That's a parallel-specific hazard on top of the per-session one: when runners share long-lived sessions and the loop runs two at a time, harvest output interleaves completed and running work, and only the body distinguishes them.\n\n**And I fabricated a park job id again** — the literal string `SCRIBE29`. Same error as earlier today, same cause: composing the park in the same command block as the dispatch, before the real id existed. There's no unpark endpoint (`DELETE` and `POST /parked/<id>` both 404), so `park-21a2eb14` will burn its full deadline and fire with payload \"placeholder\". I created the correct park separately and named the bogus one in its payload so the wake isn't misread.\n\nThat's now three instances of one root cause tonight — the fabricated id, the earlier one, and the pre-dispatch check that ran in the same breath as its dispatch. **Never compose a step in the same command block as the action it depends on or gates.** Dispatch, read the id back, then park. It's in the register.\n\n**State: 82/135 clean, 142 sorries.** (142 now — a01A09's runner has landed something; I'll verify it properly when its job reports rather than reading the working tree.)\n\nChain armed with three parks:\n- `park-dd282a89` — a01A07 retry + a01A09, ~40 min\n- `park-5e5832b6` — scribe 29, ~45 min\n- `park-21a2eb14` — bogus, will fire and be ignored\n\nScribe 29 has both runs and two genuine judgement calls I flagged as *my* uncertainty rather than steering it: whether the transitive-`sorryAx` distinction is capturable or already covered, and whether a third route-correction instance changes its earlier \"confirming instance\" verdict. I told it not to defer to me on either.Scribe 29 answered both judgement calls by proposing to *strengthen existing memories* rather than mint new ones — and for the transitive-`sorryAx` question it named a different memory than I guessed. Verifying, including that the cited memory exists:The cited memory resolves (HTTP 200): **`separate-lexical-sorry-count-from-real-proof-hole-count`**. So the corpus already knew what I presented tonight as a novel metric-integrity finding — the scribe retrieved it and correctly classified my \"discovery\" as a confirming instance. Promoting:Pass 29 promoted and reviewed — 4 drafts (2 solve, 2 frontier), all into existing patterns, no eleventh component, no handles minted, contamination scan clean for the third consecutive pass.\n\nTwo things from it worth your attention in the morning.\n\n**The corpus already knew my \"metric-integrity finding,\" and the scribe retrieved it.** I reported tonight that `EXECUTABLE-SORRIES` can't distinguish consolidation from discharge as a fresh discovery. `separate-lexical-sorry-count-from-real-proof-hole-count` has been promoted in this corpus for some time, says exactly that, and the scribe classified a01A07 as a confirming instance rather than a new rule. The memory system knew something I didn't and surfaced it — which is precisely the thing I've spent the day repairing. I've logged it as a miss, because I presented a rediscovery as a finding.\n\n**And the scribe invented a third option I hadn't offered it.** I framed both judgement calls as mint-or-decline. It proposed **amend** — raise the existing memory's confidence and add the observed case (direct sorries 4→2, newly-usable declarations 0, both rewrites still transitively `sorryAx`, one genuine helper) rather than draft a near-duplicate. For a corpus with 34% concentration and no mid-tier, strengthening existing memories is strictly better than adding neighbours, and it's the move I should have been asking for all day.\n\nThat surfaces a real gap: **the pipeline can promote but cannot amend.** Those two amendments are owed and undone; I noted it in the promote script's header rather than dropping it silently. It's the first concrete demand for memory amendment as distinct from promotion, and it came from the scribe rather than from the design — worth folding into the Librarian work in E-memory-topology, which is about exactly this kind of curatorial move.\n\n**Overnight state: 83/135 clean, 140 sorries** — twenty closed since this morning's 63, sorries down from 172. The a01A07 retry and a01A09 are still running under `park-dd282a89` (~33 min), and the chain continues from their wake. The bogus park will fire harmlessly and its successor payload says to ignore it.a01A07's retry delivered real content this time — **six new axiom-clean declarations including `norm_le_area_integral_div_area`**, which was remaining-obstruction #1. Two things need checking: whether the truncated route caused it to lean on the transitively-sorried declarations, and its claim that it altered no statements while fixing a custom `volume` instance.Statements fully intact: **six new declarations, zero changed, zero removed** — the runner's claim that it altered no statements while fixing the custom `volume` mismatch is verified, and every volume-related line is an addition. Now the genuine-discharge accounting:Clean accounting, and it contrasts sharply with the first attempt:\n\n| | attempt 1 | attempt 2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| headline sorry delta | −2 | −1 |\n| **genuine discharges** | **0** | **−1** (`norm_le_area_integral_div_area` sorried → clean) |\n| new axiom-clean declarations | 1 | **6** |\n\nNine declarations now clean; the four still carrying `sorryAx` are all downstream of the single remaining sorry. And the truncated route did **no** harm — the part that got through was obstruction #1, which is exactly what the runner proved.The guard fired and stopped me writing a duplicate route again — the row still had **two** `:suggested-route` keys from my earlier bug, so replacing one left the other. Removing both and writing one, with a backup first:Route now single-keyed with the caution placed **first** so truncation can't lose it again; hint refreshed to line 393. Selecting the next pair — as a separate step from dispatch, per tonight's lesson:Pair 2 is fully turned round and pair 3 is running.\n\n**a01A09 solved** — clean 83, twenty problems closed since morning. Its four \"compatibility repairs\" touched theorem signatures, so I diffed every signature and asserted the one substantive change in Lean: `AnalyticOn ℂ f Set.univ ↔ ∀ z, AnalyticAt ℂ f z` elaborates, so the restatement is faithful to \"entire\" and the bare `Analytic` predicate genuinely no longer exists in Mathlib. That's the case where a migration could silently weaken a target with every axiom gate still green.\n\n**a01A07's second attempt is the clean contrast that vindicates splitting the two deltas:**\n\n| | attempt 1 | attempt 2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| headline | −2 | −1 |\n| **genuine discharges** | **0** | **−1** |\n| new axiom-clean decls | 1 | **6** |\n\n`norm_le_area_integral_div_area` moved `sorryAx` → clean, so the radial disk formula — obstruction #1 — is proved. One sorry left. A metric that had been indistinguishable across two runs now separates them correctly.\n\n**And the guard I added an hour ago caught the exact bug it was written for.** Replacing a01A07's route left a *second* `:suggested-route` behind, because the row already had two from my earlier mistake — the assertion stopped the write. Redone by removing every route pair and inserting one, verified by key counts, row count and read-back. The route now leads with the caution, so the next truncation can't drop it.\n\nWorth recording honestly: the truncation *this* time did no harm, but only because the surviving fragment happened to be the part that mattered. Lucky, not fine.\n\n**State: 83/135 clean, 139 sorries, usage 12%.** Pair 3 dispatched (a01A07 attempt 3, a01A10) under `park-3e34db0c`. Scribe pass 30 for a01A09 and a01A07-attempt-2 is owed and named in the park payload.\n\nThe overnight summary will be at `holes/ops/OVERNIGHT-SUMMARY-2026-07-31.md` before you're up, failures first.**a01A07 is closed** — and this is the loop compounding on itself completely. It used both frontier memories that scribe pass 29 drafted from its *own previous attempt* and I promoted an hour ago: `upgrade-diskwise-L1-convergence-to-local-uniform-convergence` (\"its local half-disk route carried the proof\") and `lift-the-circle-submean-bound-to-a-disk-area-bound` (\"already axiom-clean, so it was not rebuilt\"). Verifying:Complete verification: **sorries 0, four declarations added, zero changed, zero removed, and all 17 declarations axiom-clean with zero `sorryAx` occurrences.** The four that carried transitive `sorryAx` are now all clean.**a01A07 closed — clean 84, twenty-one problems closed since this morning** (63 → 84, sorries 172 → 136). All 17 declarations axiom-clean, zero `sorryAx`, 4 added / 0 changed / 0 removed.\n\n**And this is the result I'd most want you to see.** Attempt 3 used *both* frontier memories that scribe pass 29 drafted from a01A07's own attempt 2 — promoted and reviewed about an hour before the dispatch. The runner reports `upgrade-diskwise-L1-convergence-to-local-uniform-convergence` **carried the proof**, via the local half-disk route that avoided the finite-cover construction attempt 1 had discarded; and `lift-the-circle-submean-bound-to-a-disk-area-bound` was used as verified prerequisite support it therefore didn't rebuild.\n\nLemniscateComponents earlier tonight was a *solve*-lane memory supplying architecture. This is a **frontier** memory — a record of what *remained* — closing the very thing it described. The loop wrote down its own unfinished business, read it back, and finished it. That's the mechanism the whole day's repair work was for.\n\n**The three attempts also give the cleanest proof yet that the two sorry-metrics diverge in both directions:**\n\n| | headline | genuine |\n|---|---|---|\n| attempt 1 | −2 | **0** |\n| attempt 2 | −1 | −1, +6 clean |\n| attempt 3 | −1 | **5 declarations unblocked** |\n\nA metric that *overstated* progress on attempt 1 *understates* it on attempt 3 — and splitting those two fields in the receipt schema tonight is the only reason it's visible.\n\nAlso this round: **a01A10** — two targets solved, third proved false by machine-checked refutation, marked `:wontfix-statement-false-pending-repair` with `:decision-owner \"joe\"`. The mathematics is true; only the Bochner-integral formalisation is wrong, and the repair is a one-line decision for you with the proof already done.\n\nQueue: 45 untouched, 34 resolved, 4 held-out, 3 blocked, 2 wontfix. Usage 12%.\n\nScribe pass 30 is owed and now covers three runs (a01A09, a01A07 attempts 2–3, a01A10's refutation). I'll dispatch it with the next pair and park.That's the fabricated `SCRIBE29` park firing on its deadline — exactly as predicted and documented, so it's noise, not a failure. But it's a useful wake: checking whether any real park survives.**Zero live parks — the chain had broken**, and the fabricated park's deadline is the only reason I'm awake. My mistake accidentally functioned as the safety net. That's worth making deliberate rather than accidental. Selecting the next pair first, as its own step:Three rows are now unreachable on stale hints — the pre-dispatch check correctly refuses them, but they'll accumulate. The refresher only targets rows with `:repaired-at` or `:last-commit`, and these have neither. Minimal fix — honour explicit ids:That wake was the fabricated `SCRIBE29` park firing — and it turned out to matter.\n\n**The chain had stopped.** There were zero live parks: `park-3e34db0c` was consumed when both pair-3 jobs finished, I processed them, and I ended that turn without dispatching or re-parking. The loop was dead. **The only thing that woke me was the park I fabricated by mistake** and logged as harmless noise.\n\nSo I've made it deliberate. `park-2c25905f` is now a **heartbeat**: it awaits a deliberately non-existent job so its deadline always fires (~58 min), with a payload telling the waking turn to check whether a real park exists and, if not, restart the loop from the RESUME doc. The accidental version worked; this one has instructions instead of \"placeholder\". The invariant \"every turn must park\" isn't enforceable by good intentions — this makes a missed park recoverable rather than fatal.\n\n**Second fix: three rows had become permanently undispatchable.** Their hints were stale, so the pre-dispatch assertion refused them — correctly — but the refresher couldn't reach them, because it only auto-selects rows the queue *knows* changed. A hint also goes stale for reasons never recorded (a Mathlib bump, work outside this lane). Now an explicitly named row is always refreshed: a02J05 and a01A11 are unstuck, dispatchable rows back to 43 of 45, and I regression-checked that the no-args default still selects only known-changed rows.\n\nWorth noting the pre-dispatch check has now paid for itself four times tonight. It converts a silent waste into a visible SKIP — which is the only reason the accumulation was noticeable at all.\n\n**Running now:** a02J05 + a01A11, plus scribe pass 30 covering three runs. That scribe owes me restated **evidence amendments** in a form I can apply by hand, since the pipeline can promote but not amend — and `override-a-documented-proof-route…` has now been used a fourth time while its amendment sits unapplied.\n\nState: **84/135 clean, 136 sorries**, 21 closed since morning, usage 12%, store 6.9 GB (below the 10 GB restart threshold). Two parks armed: the real one and the heartbeat.a01A11 and scribe 30 I handled in the last two turns. **a02J05 is new**: blocked, but the entire convergence/tail-control half is now axiom-clean — and metric 3 shows **2 memories used**, both drafted from a02J05's *own* earlier attempt this morning. Verifying:Verified: 4 new declarations, **zero changed, zero removed** — target statement intact; 5 of 6 axiom-clean with only the target carrying `sorryAx`. Headline delta **0**, genuine gain **+4 usable declarations** — a third distinct shape of the metric divergence.a02J05 is turned round and the summary is written at `holes/ops/OVERNIGHT-SUMMARY-2026-07-31.md`, failures first.\n\n**a02J05: the whole convergence half is now axiom-clean, and the headline metric reports nothing.** Sorries 1 → 1, but four new clean declarations including the uniform tail bound `|∫ₐᵇ sinc| ≤ 2/a` and the Cauchy-criterion convergence. Verified 4 added / 0 changed / 0 removed. That's a **third divergence shape** — headline 0, four declarations newly usable — after a01A07's −2/0 and −1/5. Three shapes in one night is exactly why the amendment splitting that metric into three quantities was worth applying.\n\n**And it's the third compounding instance, this one a row feeding itself across the day.** Both memories it used were drafted from a02J05's *own* failed attempt this morning — the run that first blocked on this row. One was used to evaluate the inherited Abel route and locate the precise remaining bridge; the other directly informed the tail estimate. A morning failure became the evening's proof of half the problem.\n\n**The one thing waiting on you** is a01A10: `fresnel_integral` is false as stated and the runner *proved* it. Unlike a01A05, the mathematics is true — only the Bochner-integral formalisation is wrong, and the repair is a `Tendsto` restatement. Row marked `:decision-owner \"joe\"` with the proof attached; the other two targets are closed.\n\n**Where it stands: 84/135 clean, 136 sorries, twenty-two problems closed** (from 63/172), plus a construction target and a documented Mathlib frontier. Usage 12%.\n\nThe summary is candid about my own errors — the chain stopping and being rescued by my own mistake, the queue corruption and restore, the duplicate keys I created and then generalised a guard for. It also flags the uncertainty I think matters most: **two compounding channels are running and only one is instrumented.** Proof-corpus reuse happened twice tonight (a00J04 → a01A08, a00J05 → a01A11) and is counted nowhere, so the loop's real self-reinforcement rate is under-reported by an unknown amount.\n\na02J05 attempt 2 and a01A12 are running under `park-3e308208`, heartbeat behind them.**a02J05 closed** — the Dirichlet integral, sorries 1 → 0. And note: *\"Arms (b) Zulip and (c) arXiv were not rerun, as the packet explicitly recorded their previous negative results and instructed against repetition.\"* The negative results I wrote into the route saved two whole search arms. Verifying:Verified: 11 new declarations, **zero removed, zero changed**, all 17 reporting with **zero `sorryAx`**. But the corpus reads **87**, and I predicted 86 — let me check that rather than assert it:Reconciled, and it corrects me: 84 (after a01A07) → **a01A11** → 85 → a01A12 → **86** → a02J05 → **87**. I wrote `:hud-clean-after-committed 85` in the a01A12 receipt, computing from a baseline that had already moved when a01A11 closed. I correctly flagged the contamination and then did the arithmetic off a stale number.**a02J05 closed — twenty-four problems, corpus 87 clean / 130 sorries, measured with nothing in flight.** 11 declarations added, zero changed, zero removed, zero `sorryAx` anywhere in the file.\n\nTwo results from this run are worth more than the close itself.\n\n**The negative results I wrote into the route saved two entire search arms.** The runner: *\"Arms (b) Zulip and (c) arXiv were not rerun, as the packet explicitly recorded their previous negative results and instructed against repetition.\"* Attempt 1 had established that Mathlib's `Sinc` and `ImproperIntegrals` files carry no sinc/Abel theorem, Zulip has no formalised Dirichlet integral, and those two arXiv papers are background only. Recording what was *not* found turned out to be as valuable as recording what was — and it's far cheaper to carry forward than a proof.\n\n**And the same two memories carried this row across all three attempts, spanning a full day.** Both were drafted from durée run 8 yesterday morning — the run that *first* blocked here — used overnight to prove the convergence half, and used again tonight to close it. A single failed run's memories carried a problem to completion. That's the fourth compounding instance and the most persistent; the other three were single-hop.\n\n**One correction of mine.** I recorded `:hud-clean-after-committed 85` on the a01A12 receipt; the true figure was 86. I correctly detected the working-tree reading was contaminated by a02J05 being mid-flight — and then computed a replacement from a stale baseline, forgetting a01A11 had already moved it. The lesson is narrower than the trap I was avoiding: **detecting that a reading is contaminated doesn't license substituting an arithmetic estimate for it.** The right move was to record it as unavailable until nothing was in flight. Correction receipt written.\n\n**State: 87/135 clean, 130 sorries** (from 63/172). Queue: 42 untouched · 37 resolved · 4 held-out · 3 blocked · 2 wontfix. Usage ~12%.\n\nBoth pair-5 jobs are done, so I owe the next dispatch and a scribe for a01A12 + a02J05. I'll take those now and re-park — the heartbeat is still standing behind it.The heartbeat fired as designed. Checking whether the chain is healthy or stopped:**The chain had stopped** — zero parks, all eight agents idle. The heartbeat caught it exactly as designed. Second time the loop died tonight; first time something deliberate rescued it. Restarting — selecting and pre-checking first:The heartbeat fired and **caught a genuinely stopped chain** — validated on its first deliberate use.\n\nAt 02:02: zero live parks, all eight codex agents idle, nothing running. Both pair-5 jobs had completed, I processed them, and the turn ended without a re-dispatch. That's the second time tonight the loop died and the second time a park-awaiting-nothing rescued it — but the first time it was on purpose, with a payload saying what to do rather than \"placeholder\".\n\n**The failure mode is now well characterised, and it isn't carelessness in any single turn.** The chain dies whenever a turn's work happens to end exactly when the last awaited job completes: the park is consumed by that completion, and if the turn then finishes without dispatching, nothing remains to wake anything. Twice in four hours. A dead-man switch is the right *shape* of fix precisely because the invariant can't be maintained by intention — which is the same lesson as the recall-status banner earlier: put the guard where the failure happens, not in someone's memory of having learned it.\n\n**Loop restarted:** a01J01 and a01J02 dispatched, plus scribe pass 31 covering a01A12 and a02J05. All three under `park-a0665fc3`, heartbeat re-armed as `park-afdf57c7`.\n\nI gave scribe 31 two questions I genuinely can't answer: whether *\"record and carry forward what was NOT found, with enough specificity to justify skipping an arm\"* is a capturable rule or already covered by the existing search-order memory; and whether the three-attempt a02J05 arc yields anything transferable about writing up a **blocked** run for its successor. The seven-part drafting hypothesis it recorded in pass 30 now has a second case to test against, so I asked whether it holds or needs revision — which is what a hypothesis is for.\n\n**State: 87/135 clean, 130 sorries, 24 problems closed.** Queue 42 untouched · 37 resolved · 4 held-out · 3 blocked · 2 wontfix. Usage 13%, store 6.91 GB — every stop condition comfortably clear.a01J01 found the **same junk-value defect a third time** — `uniform_small_set_of_L3_bound` omits an integrability hypothesis, so `integral_undef` lets the condition hold **vacuously**. And it proved a counterexample rather than weakening the theorem. Verifying:Verified: 3 new declarations, **zero changed, zero removed**. But one precision the receipt must carry — the committed `counterexample_from_spikes` uses the **L¹** bound `∫‖f n‖ ≤ 1`, while the target's hypothesis is **L³**. So the spike mechanism is proved; the cubic-singularity step that would extend it to the L³ statement is *argued* in a comment, not formalised:Precisely confirmed: `counterexample_from_spikes` is axiom-clean, `uniform_small_set_of_L3_bound` still carries `sorryAx`, and the cubic-singularity step is a **comment** (lines 101–104). The file's own docstring even scopes the counterexample explicitly: *\"witnesses the failure of part (a) if we only assume an L¹ bound.\"* So the defect is **argued with a proved L¹ component**, not machine-checked like a01A10's.All three pair-6 jobs processed. Continuing the loop — selecting, then dispatching as a separate step:Two heartbeats now — `park-afdf57c7` hadn't fired yet when I armed `park-905d7e01`. That breaks my own \"exactly one\" rule. Harmless (an extra wake), but the rule as written invites it. Correcting it and updating the summary:All three pair-6 jobs are processed and the loop continues.\n\n**a01J01 — third junk-value defect tonight, and this one is vacuity rather than falsehood.** `uniform_small_set_of_L3_bound` omits an integrability hypothesis, so `integral_undef` lets a cubic-non-integrable family satisfy the L³ bound trivially while violating the conclusion. The runner proved a counterexample and left the frontier in a comment rather than adding the missing hypothesis to make the target provable.\n\n**But I recorded what's proved versus what's argued, because the two defects aren't equivalent evidence.** a01A10's refutation is complete and machine-checked — the exact negation, axiom-clean. a01J01's is not: the committed counterexample uses the **L¹** bound (the file's own docstring says so), and the L³ extension is a source comment at lines 101–104. Filing an argued defect alongside a proved one would corrupt exactly the evidence base these receipts exist to keep honest.\n\nSo there's now a **defect class**: three APM statements broken by one Lean convention. And **three runners independently declined to silently repair a statement** — a01A10 refused to pick a repair, a01J02 refused to choose between two, a01J01 refused to weaken. None was told to.\n\n**One error of mine:** I armed a second heartbeat while the first still had 2600 s left, breaking my own \"exactly one\" rule. Harmless, but the rule as written invited it — it stated the invariant without a trigger. Rewritten in the RESUME doc as a procedure: count parks whose `awaiting[0]` doesn't start with `invoke-`, arm only if that count is zero. **An invariant stated without a trigger is a wish.**\n\n**Summary updated** with both structural findings and current figures: **87/135 clean, 130 sorries, twenty-four problems closed.** Queue is 36 untouched · 37 resolved · 4 held-out · **8 blocked-and-diagnosed** · 2 wontfix.\n\nThree decisions wait for you in the morning, all with diagnosis done and none acted on unilaterally: a01A10's Fresnel restatement, a01J02's repair choice (one option is a vacuity trap), and whether to formalise a01J01's argued defect.\n\nPair 7 (a01J06, a02J01) is running under `park-55c86809`, with the heartbeats behind it.a02J01 is the **fifth** junk-value defect and the largest — **all three targets false**, again via `integral_undef`. But no commit and sorries 3 → 3, so like a01J01 these counterexamples are **described, not formalised**. Verifying the reasoning holds:Independently confirmed — the counterexample arithmetic is sound. For `schur_pointwise` with `k ≡ 2`, `u = 1_[0,1]` on infinite-volume `ℝᵈ`: the hypothesis `∫ y, ‖2‖ ≤ 1` holds **vacuously** (0 ≤ 1), LHS = 2² = 4, RHS = `∫ 2·1_[0,1] = 2`, so the claim reduces to 4 ≤ 2. False.**a02J01: all three targets are false** — the fifth junk-value defect, and the largest. Same mechanism: on infinite-volume `ℝᵈ` a nonzero constant is non-integrable, so `integral_undef` makes a hypothesis like `∫ y, ‖k x y‖ ≤ 1` hold **vacuously**. I confirmed the `schur_pointwise` arithmetic myself: hypothesis reduces to 0 ≤ 1, and the claim to 4 ≤ 2.\n\n**But these are argued, not machine-checked** — prose plus arithmetic I checked by hand, with no commit. That distinction is now load-bearing across the whole class, so I've recorded it per-problem rather than lumping them:\n\n| problem | defect | proof status | disposition |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| a01A10 | 1 target FALSE | **machine-checked** (exact negation, axiom-clean) | wontfix pending repair |\n| a95J03 | 1 target VACUOUS | confirmed by inspection | **closed and counted clean — disputed** |\n| a01J01 | 1 target VACUOUS | argued (L¹ part proved, L³ step a comment) | blocked |\n| a01J06 | 1 VACUOUS + 1 FALSE | **machine-checked** counterexample | blocked |\n| a02J01 | 3 targets FALSE | argued | blocked |\n\n**Five problems, seven targets, one root cause.** This isn't five unlucky problems — it's a systematic mismatch between how the APM statements were authored and how Lean totalises integrals. Anyone writing `∫ f ≤ C` as a hypothesis over an infinite-measure space has written something that a non-integrable `f` satisfies for free.\n\nThat reframes tonight's headline: **24 problems closed is real, but the corpus contains a defect class that no gate we run detects** — and one already-clean problem (a95J03) sits inside it.\n\nTwo runner behaviours worth noting: a02J01's declined the weak-L²/Vitali memory as *\"directionally related but the targets fail earlier, through `integral_undef`\"* — a near-miss judged on **where in the argument** the failure occurs, which is finer than relevance. And all five runners diagnosed rather than attempted, without being told.\n\nQueue is now 34 untouched, with 11 rows blocked-and-diagnosed across six distinct causes. I'll fold the tally into the summary and keep the loop running — the remaining untouched rows are unaffected by this class.Heartbeat fired again. Checking the chain — and this time counting heartbeats properly before re-arming, per the rule I fixed:**Chain stopped a third time** — 0 real parks, all agents idle. The heartbeat caught it again. One heartbeat alive with 864s left, so per the corrected rule I won't arm another.\n\nBut three occurrences means the ordering is wrong, not my attention. **Dispatching first, before any processing** — so the chain can never depend on remembering to dispatch at the end:The park awaits only the two proving jobs — I composed it before the scribe's id existed, the same batching error in a milder form. Adding a park for the scribe, and writing the dispatch-first rule into the contract:Pair 8 and scribe 32 are running. Now updating the summary — it still says three junk-value problems and the vacuity finding isn't at the top where it belongs:Chain stopped a third time; the heartbeat caught it again. But three occurrences of one shape isn't inattention — it's a bad ordering, so I fixed it structurally.\n\n**Dispatch first, then process.** The failure was identical every time: awaited jobs complete, the completion consumes the park, the turn does its long processing work, and ends without dispatching. Now on every wake I select, pre-check, dispatch, read the ids back, park — and *only then* verify and receipt. Dispatch is short and chain-preserving; processing is long and interruptible. That makes the gap structurally impossible rather than something to remember, which is the same move as the recall banner and the heartbeat itself.\n\nApplied it this turn: **a02J02 and a02J03 were dispatched before I touched anything else**, plus scribe pass 32.\n\nI did make the batching error again in a milder form — composed the park payload before dispatching the scribe, so its id wasn't in the `awaiting` list. Caught it on read-back and added a second park. Same root cause as the two fabricated ids: composing a record before the thing it references exists. That corollary now sits next to the dispatch-first rule rather than buried.\n\n**The summary is restructured**, with the vacuity finding promoted to the top under \"READ THIS FIRST\" — because a problem counted clean that proves nothing outranks any of the closes. The junk-value tally is corrected from three problems to **five problems and seven targets**, with a per-problem proved/argued column, since filing an argued defect alongside a machine-checked one would corrupt exactly the evidence base the receipts exist to keep honest.\n\nOne line I added and believe: **five runners independently declined to silently repair a statement** — refusing to pick a repair, refusing to choose between two, refusing to weaken, refuting rather than patching, diagnosing without touching the file. None was told to. That's the strongest evidence tonight that the review discipline has transferred to the runners themselves.\n\nScribe 32 has the sharpest question of the night turned back on it: **is a vacuity check capturable at all, or is it irreducibly human judgement?** I told it explicitly that \"no rule, this needs a person\" is a good answer and not to invent an unfalsifiable checklist to please me.\n\nState: **87/135 clean (86 genuine + 1 disputed), 130 sorries, 24 closed**, 34 untouched, usage 13%, store 6.91 GB.Dispatching first, per the rule — a02J03 is the **sixth** junk-value problem and this pushes the class to nine targets:Chain armed (1 real park, 1 heartbeat with 337s — so no new heartbeat per the rule). But scribe 32's park is gone from the list. Checking it, and verifying a02J03:a02J03 verified: **zero signature changes**, no placeholders, 3 of 5 axiom-clean with `uniform_bound` and `scaled_integral_vanishes` carrying `sorryAx` — the two the runner says are false. And **scribe 32 is done**; its park was consumed. Reading its answer to the vacuity question:**Scribe 32 corrected my headline claim, and it's right.** I wrote \"five problems, one root cause\" into the summary — at the top. It doesn't hold. The evidence separates into four distinct classes:\n\n- **`integral_undef`**: a01A10, a01J01, a02J01 — and it names an earlier one I didn't know about, a95A08\n- **constant placeholder**: a95J03\n- **missing model invariants**: a01J06\n- **pre-formal elaboration failure**: a01J02\n\nI conflated four defect classes under one banner because they all *felt* like \"the corpus is broken.\" Correcting that first:**The scribe corrected my headline claim, and it was right — the tenth time.**\n\nI wrote *\"five problems, one root cause: `integral_undef`\"* and put it at the top of the overnight summary. It doesn't hold. Scribe 32 separated the evidence into four classes:\n\n- **`integral_undef`** — a01A10, a01J01, a02J01, now a02J03, plus an earlier a95A08 I didn't know about\n- **constant placeholder** — a95J03, a scaffold stub with nothing to do with integrals\n- **missing model invariants** — a01J06\n- **pre-formal elaboration failure** — a01J02 and the audit's four\n\n**I conflated four mechanisms because they shared a feeling — \"the corpus is unsound\" — rather than a cause.** The summary is rewritten as a taxonomy.\n\nWorth naming plainly: that's the **second** time tonight I've generalised too fast from a genuine finding. Earlier I diagnosed pattern-tier fragmentation for what was an infrastructure timeout; now a single root cause for what is four. Same shape both times — a real observation over-extended into a class — and both times something else caught it, not me. That looks like my characteristic failure mode rather than two unrelated slips.\n\nThe scribe also answered the vacuity question better than I framed it. I'd explicitly told it *\"no rule, this needs a person\"* was acceptable; instead it gave the more precise answer — placeholder detection is **partly** mechanisable (unfold central definitions, flag constants, unused inputs, `True` fields), while semantic adequacy still needs human review. And it recommended a95J03 be classified `:closed-over-placeholder` to amend the existing placeholder memory rather than mint a new one. It also turned my third question into a **draft**: elaboration preflight is capturable — which is my a02J06 discovery generalised into a rule by an agent that wasn't there when I made it.\n\n**a02J03 verified**: `indicator_bound` axiom-clean, zero signature changes, no placeholders, and exactly the two reported-false targets carrying `sorryAx`. Recorded as `integral_undef` class, **argued not machine-checked**, with the Borel–Cantelli counterexample mechanism and the `LocallyIntegrable φ` repair left for you.\n\nPair 9 is dispatched and parked (dispatch-first held this time). Scribe 32's two trajectory drafts still need promoting and its four amendments applying — I'll take those next.I processed scribe 32's verdicts and corrected the summary last turn. The promotion and amendments are owed. Verifying the drafts and the patterns it names:My URL guess was wrong — `math/missing-dependency-protocol` returns 404 too and I know that one exists, so the 404s are meaningless. Checking how patterns actually resolve:`math-formalization/notation-semantics-traps` is attested (3 prior uses), but **`math/corpus-trust-protocol` appears in no prior promotion** — the scribe listed it as *reused*. Rather than risk auto-creating an eleventh component, I'll place both drafts in attested patterns and flag the discrepancy:Correction to my own scepticism: `corpus-trust-protocol` **is** in the store — my check only grepped promotion scripts, which was too narrow. The scribe was right; I just looked in the wrong place. Both drafts promoted and reviewed. Now its four amendments:Heartbeat count is zero — the previous one fired. Per the rule, arming exactly one:Scribe 32 is fully landed.\n\n**Two trajectory drafts promoted and reviewed** into attested patterns — the first trajectory-lane drafts of the night, and both are process rules extracted from *my* operational discoveries by an agent that wasn't present for them: elaboration preflight (from the a02J06 unbuilt-dependency case) and model-invariant requirements (from a01J06).\n\n**One correction to my own scepticism.** I doubted the scribe's `math/corpus-trust-protocol` handle, grepped the promotion scripts, found nothing, and was ready to record a discrepancy. The handle *is* in the store — I'd only searched promote-script history, a strict subset. Eleventh time I've been wrong to doubt it, and this time the fault was in my verification method rather than my judgement. Caught before it went anywhere durable.\n\n**Four more amendments applied — thirteen tonight**, all append-only with proposer ≠ applier:\n\n- **The Bochner memory broadened from a technique into a diagnostic**, carrying all six problems with their distinct counterexample mechanisms — and the strongest form of the check, from a02J07: *look at what the applicable Mathlib API requires. When every route demands a hypothesis the scaffold omits, that's a statement defect, not a proof gap.*\n- **a95J03 added to the placeholder memory** as a closed-over-placeholder case that is counted clean.\n- **A `:semantic-status` axis** for the proof-hole meter — all its existing quantities are syntactic and none separates a real close from a vacuous one.\n- **The four-class taxonomy**, with the point that the classes need *different* responses: premise repair, scaffold implementation, structure redesign, build fix.\n\n**State: 87/135 clean (86 genuine + 1 disputed), 126 sorries** — down from 172 this morning. Queue: 31 untouched, 37 resolved, **13 blocked-and-diagnosed across seven causes**, 2 in flight, 4 held-out, 2 wontfix.\n\nChain armed: 1 real park (a02J02 attempt 2), 1 heartbeat. Usage 14%.Chain healthy — a02J02's second attempt is running on codex-7, heartbeat already armed (count 1, so no new one per the rule). But **codex-6 is idle and only one proving run is in flight**, so this wake is best spent filling the empty slot:**First quiet wake of the night** — the chain was healthy. a02J02's second attempt running on codex-7, real park live, heartbeat already armed (count 1, so no new one per the rule). The dead-man switch works in both directions: it caught three stopped chains and now correctly reports a running one.\n\n**But I used the wake rather than treating it as a no-op.** Only *one* proving run was in flight while codex-6 sat idle — the pairing had drifted to a single because a02J02 was re-dispatched alone after its partner finished. So I selected a03J03 (pre-checked, distinct file from the in-flight a02J02), dispatched it, and parked. Both runners are busy again.\n\nThat's a small thing but worth naming: **a wake that finds the chain healthy is an opportunity to check *capacity*, not just liveness.** The heartbeat's payload asked \"is the chain alive?\" — the better question is \"is the chain alive *and* fully loaded?\" I've been watching for the loop stopping and not for it running at half rate.\n\nThe park payload now also carries the **four-class taxonomy** as a receipt requirement, so any statement defect the next run finds gets classified at the point of recording rather than lumped. That's the concrete residue of scribe 32 correcting my single-root-cause claim an hour ago — the correction is now enforced in the workflow, not just noted in a document.\n\n**State: 87/135 clean, 126 sorries** (from 63/172 yesterday morning), 31 untouched, 13 blocked-and-diagnosed across seven causes, usage 14%, store 6.9 GB.\n\nTwo runners busy, two real parks, one heartbeat.**a02J02 closed — `radon_riesz` proved axiom-clean.** That's twenty-five. And the runner improved on my five-step continuation: applying Vitali directly to `fₙ` avoids separately treating `|fₙ−g|^p`.\n\nDispatching first, since codex-7 is now free:**a02J02 closed axiom-clean — twenty-five problems since yesterday morning.** All seven declarations re-elaborate clean with zero `sorryAx`, 3 added / 0 changed / 0 removed, and the placeholder scan is clean, so this isn't a vacuous close.\n\n**The most interesting thing is that my carried-forward route was right about the shape and wrong about one step — and the runner corrected it rather than following it.** Attempt 1 left a five-step Scheffé/Vitali continuation specifying that uniform integrability be transferred to |fₙ − g|^p via the committed `rpow_sub_le`. The runner confirmed the architecture but found a shorter final step: apply Vitali directly to fₙ and skip |fₙ − g|^p entirely.\n\nSo a lemma I'd recorded as a load-bearing prerequisite turned out to be unnecessary for the closing argument. It's still correct and available — but it's a useful caution about how I write these routes. A route that names specific intermediate lemmas can over-constrain; the architecture was the valuable part, and the runner was right to treat the step list as advisory. That's the fourth time tonight a runner has bettered a supplied route.\n\n**Carried-forward negative results saved a search arm for the second time** — arm (b) wasn't rerun because the row recorded attempt 1's Zulip findings. Recording what was *not* found keeps paying, and scribe 31's insistence that it be scoped as a revision-dependent **cache** rather than a fact is what makes it safe to keep doing.\n\nIts sharpest discrimination: `e-afb1eae8` was reviewed as an *analogous* Kadec–Klee strategy and declined as not applicable to general Lp. That's a judgement on **generality**, not relevance — the right kind of thing at the wrong level of abstraction, and it said so precisely.\n\nDispatch-first held again: a92J06 went out before I processed anything, so both runners are loaded (a03J03 and a92J06), with three real parks and a heartbeat.\n\n**State: 87 clean → will recount when nothing is in flight; queue 29 untouched, 38 resolved, 13 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 dispatched.** Scribe 33 is owed, covering a02J02, a02J03 and a02J07.Dispatching first — codex-6 is now free:Verified: 1 declaration added, zero changed, zero removed, no placeholders, 2 of 3 axiom-clean. And the deleted lines are worth noting — the scaffold's inherited notes claimed **\"Translation continuity: `MeasureTheory.Lp.hasTranslation` (partial)\"** and **\"C_c approximation in Lp: partial support\"**, both of which were wrong; Mathlib has full support via `Continuous.compMeasurePreservingLp`.**a03J03 turned up a defect class I hadn't seen — and it's the one my whole verification apparatus is blind to.**\n\nIts scaffold notes claimed *\"Translation continuity: `MeasureTheory.Lp.hasTranslation` (partial)\"* and *\"C_c approximation in Lp: partial support\"*. Both wrong. Mathlib supports translation continuity **fully** via `Continuous.compMeasurePreservingLp` — which is exactly what carried the proof, composed with the bundled `ContinuousLinearMap.lpPairing`. The runner deleted the stale claim and replaced it.\n\n**Every other scaffold defect tonight overstated what was proved** — placeholders, vacuous hypotheses, false targets. **This one understated what was available, and its cost is invisible.** A runner that believed the note would avoid a viable route and either fail or rebuild machinery Mathlib already has. No proof-checking gate can catch it, because nothing is wrong with the proofs.\n\nThat's worth sitting with: sorry counts, axiom census, signature diffs, the placeholder scan I added tonight — all of it is oriented toward catching claims that are **too strong**. This is a claim that was **too weak**, and the only thing that surfaced it was a runner trying the route anyway and finding the note false. I have no systematic check for that, and I'm not sure one exists short of attempting the work.\n\nVerification otherwise clean: 2 declarations newly axiom-clean, 1 added / 0 changed / 0 removed, placeholder scan clean, headline and genuine deltas in agreement.\n\nThe row went back with the correction stated **first** in the route — \"the old notes were wrong in the pessimistic direction; do not trust the partial-support framing\" — plus the two now-available supports, the exact remaining gap (two-factor compact-support approximation and eventual-disjoint-support assembly, which Mathlib doesn't package), and negative search results scoped to the current revision.\n\nDispatch-first held again; both runners loaded with a92J06 and a92J07. Queue: 29 untouched, 38 resolved, 13 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight. Scribe 33 owed, now covering four runs.a92J07 found the **same junk-value shape via a different operation** — not `integral_undef` but `deriv` totalisation: Lean's `deriv` is zero wherever differentiability fails, so a nowhere-differentiable Weierstrass function satisfies all four Cauchy–Riemann hypotheses vacuously. Dispatching first:Confirmed precisely. The hypotheses are **only `Continuous`** — no differentiability anywhere — while `hcr1`/`hcr2` constrain `deriv`. With u = 2 + W(x), v = W(y) for a bounded nowhere-differentiable W with |W| < 1: all four `deriv` terms are 0, `hray` holds since u > 1, and the conclusion \"u, v constant\" is false. Signature diff shows **zero changes**.The docstring already documents the defect precisely. Critical question — did the runner add that, or was it pre-existing and I dispatched a row whose file already said the target was unprovable?The docstring is **new** — the runner added it (all `+` lines, zero occurrences in the parent). So it documented the defect in the file rather than weakening the theorem. No queue-integrity issue on my side.**a92J07 found the junk-value shape via a different operation, and that changes the class.**\n\nEvery instance tonight was `integral_undef`. This one is **`deriv` totalisation**: Lean's `deriv` is zero wherever differentiability fails. `cauchy_riemann_ray_implies_const` assumes only `Continuous` on both components — no differentiability at all — while `hcr1`/`hcr2` constrain `deriv` values. I read the hypothesis list myself and confirmed it's Continuous-only. So a bounded nowhere-differentiable W with |W| < 1, with u = 2 + W(x) and v = W(y), zeroes all four `deriv` terms, satisfies `hray` since u > 1, and leaves both components nonconstant.\n\n**So the class isn't \"integral_undef\" — it's \"junk-value totalisation of a partial operation,\" with at least two operations. But I'm not generalising it myself.** I recorded `:defect-operation :deriv` alongside the class and flagged it for the scribe to judge whether that's one class or two. Scribe 32 corrected me three hours ago for lumping four mechanisms under one cause; the lesson isn't \"never generalise,\" it's \"hand the datum to the reviewer rather than banking the generalisation.\"\n\nA sharp secondary diagnostic worth keeping: **the prose says u and v are C¹ while the formal hypotheses assert only continuity.** A prose/formalisation mismatch on a differentiability hypothesis is exactly where totalisation bites — and it's cheap to check on any statement whose hypotheses mention derivatives or integrals.\n\n`entire_avoids_ray_const` closed axiom-clean (slit-plane logarithm, bounded `exp(I·log f)`, Liouville, `is_const_of_deriv_eq_zero`), with zero signature changes.\n\nI also checked something that would have been my fault rather than the corpus's: the runner added a docstring documenting the defect, and **I verified it's new** — all additions, zero occurrences in the parent — because if it had been pre-existing, I'd have dispatched a row whose own file already said the target was unprovable. It wasn't. Seventh run tonight to refuse a silent repair.\n\nBoth runners loaded (a92J06, a03J03 attempt 2). Queue: 28 untouched, 38 resolved, 14 blocked-and-diagnosed. Scribe 33 owed, now covering five runs.**a92J06: both declarations closed axiom-clean, sorries 2 → 0 — that's twenty-six.** Dispatching first, codex-7 is free:Clean: **zero signature changes** (7 before, 7 after), no placeholders, and the deletions read as an import swap, two SORRY BOUNDARY comments, and the two `sorry` lines. Axioms over every declaration:**a92J06 closed axiom-clean — twenty-six problems since yesterday morning.** Contour integral by partial fractions (interior pole via the circle Cauchy formula, exterior killed by Cauchy–Goursat), then the real integral by unfolding `circleIntegral` and proving the z = e^{iθ} identity pointwise. Zero signature changes, all seven declarations clean, zero `sorryAx`, placeholder scan clean, deletions read rather than counted.\n\n**Two things worth saying plainly.**\n\n**The corpus is not uniformly broken.** After eight consecutive blocked runs — seven of them corpus defects — it becomes very easy to read every block as rot. But a92J06 closed cleanly in that same stretch, and so did a02J02. The defect rate is high in the **J-series specifically**; it isn't the whole corpus. I'd have been over-generalising for a third time tonight if I hadn't noticed the counter-examples sitting in the same window.\n\n**Both public search surfaces failed on this run, and local search carried it.** Loogle URL queries for `integral_sub_inv_of_mem_ball` and `integral_sub_zpow_of_ne` were **rejected by the browsing safety layer**, and LeanSearch returned irrelevant results — while local declaration search found every component needed. That's a recurring pattern tonight rather than a one-off, and it means the search-arm accounting may be crediting arm (a) for work that is really *\"the public tools were unavailable to this runner.\"* I've recorded it on the receipt so the arm statistics stay honest, because \"components carried it\" and \"components were the only thing reachable\" are different claims and the ledger currently can't tell them apart.\n\nDispatch-first held; both runners loaded (a03J03 attempt 2, a93A02). Queue: **27 untouched, 39 resolved, 14 blocked-and-diagnosed**, 3 in flight. Scribe 33 owed, now covering six runs.a93A02: **both targets false via a third distinct mechanism** — not `integral_undef`, not `deriv` totalisation, but **`ℝ≥0∞` type inference**. `∃ M, ∀ n, eLpNorm (f n) 2 μ ≤ M` infers `M : ℝ≥0∞`, so `⟨∞, fun n => le_top⟩` satisfies it unconditionally. Dispatching first:Confirmed by reading it: `(hbdd : ∃ M, ∀ n, eLpNorm (f n) 2 μ ≤ M)` — `eLpNorm` returns `ℝ≥0∞`, so `M` infers to `ℝ≥0∞` and `M = ⊤` satisfies it via `le_top`. And the docstring says *\"with uniform bound M\"* — the prose implies finite, the formalisation admits ∞. Same prose/formal mismatch shape as a92J07. The counterexample checks out: ‖fₙ‖₂² = n+1 (unbounded, so the real hypothesis fails), fₙ → 0 pointwise, ‖fₙ‖₁ = 1.**a93A02: a third mechanism in the vacuous-hypothesis family, and it isn't an operation at all.**\n\n`(hbdd : ∃ M, ∀ n, eLpNorm (f n) 2 μ ≤ M)` — `eLpNorm` is `ℝ≥0∞`-valued, so **M infers to `ℝ≥0∞`** and the hypothesis is discharged unconditionally by `M = ⊤` via `le_top`. The bound constrains nothing. Not `integral_undef`, not `deriv` totalisation — an **extended-type top element**. I read the statement and confirmed the inferred type myself, and checked the counterexample rather than accepting it: fₙ(x) = (n+1)·1_(0,1/(n+1)] gives ‖fₙ‖₂² = n+1, so each fₙ *is* in L² but the norms are unbounded — exactly the family the intended hypothesis should exclude. fₙ → 0 pointwise, ‖fₙ‖₁ = 1 throughout, so both uniform integrability and the asserted L¹ convergence fail.\n\n**The most useful thing here is a cheap check that's falling out of two consecutive runs.** a93A02's docstring says \"uniform bound M\", implying finite, while the formalisation admits ∞. a92J07's prose said C¹ while the hypotheses said only `Continuous`. **In both cases the prose was right and the Lean was wrong — and the gap was visible without proving anything.** Concretely: for any hypothesis of the form `∃ M, … ≤ M`, ask what type M infers to; for any hypothesis mentioning derivatives or integrals, check whether differentiability or integrability is actually assumed.\n\nThat's a preflight check on statements, not proofs, and it would have caught at least three of tonight's defects before a runner slot was spent.\n\nI recorded `:defect-mechanism :extended-type-top-element` and **flagged it for the scribe rather than folding it into the taxonomy myself** — same discipline as the `deriv` case. Three mechanisms now sit under one family with the generalisation deliberately unmade, because I've been wrong twice tonight generalising early and the scribe has been right each time.\n\nEighth run to refuse a silent repair: *\"Changing the target was prohibited, so I left it unchanged.\"*\n\nQueue: 26 untouched, 39 resolved, **15 blocked-and-diagnosed**, 3 in flight. Both runners loaded.**a03J03 closed — `convolution_tendsto_zero` proved axiom-clean, sorries 1 → 0. That's twenty-seven.** Dispatching first:**a03J03 closed axiom-clean — twenty-seven problems since yesterday morning.** `convolution_tendsto_zero` by the compact-support-density argument: approximate both factors by compactly supported continuous functions, bound the two errors by Hölder and translation isometry, and observe the approximants' convolution has compact support and is eventually zero. Zero signature changes, placeholder scan clean, all three declarations axiom-clean.\n\n**The corrected scaffold note paid off directly, and that's the useful result.** Attempt 1 discovered the file's inherited comment claiming *\"`Lp.hasTranslation` (partial)\"* and *\"C_c approximation: partial support\"* was wrong; I put that correction **first** in the route. Attempt 2 then used exactly the machinery the stale note would have discouraged. That's the clearest evidence yet that the understating-defect class costs real work — its cost is normally invisible precisely because nobody tries the discouraged route.\n\n**And two adjacent data points now point opposite ways on a question I'd like resolved.** On a02J02 my five-step route was right about the architecture and *wrong* about one step, and the runner improved on it by skipping |fₙ−g|^p entirely. On a03J03 the route named the argument and the runner executed it as written.\n\nMy instinct is \"name the architecture, not the steps\" — but that's exactly the kind of n=2 generalisation I've been wrong on twice tonight, so I've recorded it as `:route-prescriptiveness-datum` on both receipts and handed it to the scribe rather than acting on it. If a route that over-specifies is actively harmful, that changes how I write every future one, and I'd rather have that judged than assumed.\n\n**State: 40 resolved, 25 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed across seven causes, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a93A04, a93J06). Usage 15%.\n\nThe next park payload carries the new **statement preflight** — check what `M` infers to in any `∃ M, … ≤ M`, check whether differentiability/integrability is actually assumed when hypotheses mention derivatives or integrals, and compare the docstring against the formal hypotheses. Twice tonight the prose was right and the Lean was wrong, and both were visible without proving anything.**a93J06 closed — both proofs axiom-clean, sorries 2 → 0. Twenty-eight.** Dispatching first:Cleanest diff of the night — **the deletions are exactly two `sorry` lines and nothing else**, zero signature changes, no placeholders. Independent axiom check:**a93J06 closed axiom-clean — twenty-eight problems since yesterday morning.** Schwarz lemma applied to `deriv f`, then a radial parametrisation g(t) = f(t·z) with ‖g′(t)‖ ≤ t‖z‖² integrated to ‖z‖²/2. The deletions are **exactly the two `sorry` lines and nothing else** — the cleanest diff of the night.\n\n**Two things I want to flag rather than let slide.**\n\n**The search-arm ledger is now reporting a capability we may not have.** Loogle and LeanSearch returned nothing useful here; local declaration search found all four components including the exact `integral_id`. Same story on a92J06 an hour ago, where Loogle URLs were *rejected outright by the browsing safety layer*. Across tonight, \"arm (a) carried it\" increasingly means **local declaration search**, not the public component tools — and `surface-payoff.edn` can't distinguish them. That's a measurement problem in the artifact we'd show someone: it credits a search capability when the real finding is that runners fall back to grepping Mathlib successfully. I've recorded it as a `:ledger-caveat` on both receipts; splitting the arm label is a small change but it's yours to schedule.\n\n**Scribe 33 is now owed over eight runs, and that's too many.** a02J02, a02J03, a02J07, a03J03, a92J06, a92J07, a93A02, a93J06 — three of which carry *distinct* defect mechanisms deserving individual judgement. A single pass over eight risks shallow treatment of each. The throughput gain from back-to-back proving runs is being paid for in scribe debt, and I've flagged it to dispatch next turn rather than let it grow further.\n\n**State: 41 resolved, 24 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed across seven causes, 3 in flight, 88 rows total.** Both runners loaded (a93A04, a93J07). Usage 15%, store steady.a93A04 is a **genuine Mathlib frontier** — two declarations proved axiom-clean, blocked on a real gap (Mathlib has the monotone-partition → disjoint-intervals direction but not the converse). Dispatching the proving row and **scribe 33**, which I flagged as overdue:**a93A04 is the second honest Mathlib frontier of the night, against nine defect runs.** Two declarations proved axiom-clean, and the obstruction is precisely named: `sum_edist_le_eVariationOn_of_mem_disjWithin` needs to *order* an arbitrary finite pairwise-disjoint family of unoriented `uIoc` intervals and embed its endpoint increments into a monotone variation partition. **Mathlib has only the opposite direction** — building disjoint intervals *from* a monotone partition. A named missing converse is a far better frontier record than \"blocked.\"\n\nI also checked the `sorryAx` **dependency shape** rather than just counting: three declarations clean, and the two contaminated ones are the remaining target and the `iff` that depends on it — exactly what should be affected and nothing more. That's what the transitive census is for, and it's worth saying when it comes out clean rather than only when it doesn't.\n\n**Third consecutive run where public search failed and local declaration search carried the work.** LeanSearch returned only irrelevant finite-sum material. After a92J06 (Loogle URLs rejected by the browsing safety layer) and a93J06 (no useful direct theorem), this isn't anecdotal — and the ledger still credits all of it to \"arm (a) components,\" conflating a working public tool with a runner grepping Mathlib locally. That's a measurement error in an artifact we'd show someone.\n\n**Scribe 33 is dispatched over eight runs, with the three defect runs explicitly prioritised** and permission to ration attention on the closes. I let that backlog grow, and a shallow pass over eight is worse than a deep pass over four. I've asked it to judge whether the three vacuous-hypothesis *mechanisms* are one family or separate rules, whether the statement preflight is a memory or an amendment, and the route-prescriptiveness question where a02J02 and a03J03 point opposite ways — telling it explicitly that declining any of these counts as a good answer.\n\n**State: 41 resolved, 24 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a93J07, a94A01). Usage 15%.Scribe 33 answered all three, and the answers are better than my framing on each.\n\n**(1)** One umbrella — *\"missing semantic-domain guards admit degenerate witnesses\"* — but **keep three mechanism-specific rules and repairs**. Unify the diagnosis, separate the mechanisms, because the repairs differ. Better than either of the options I offered.\n\n**(3)** It **declined my \"architecture only\" instinct** and gave a more precise structure: routes should represent stable architecture *separately from* provisional steps, with confidence and evidence per step. That's the twelfth time it has corrected me and been right.\n\nBoth runners are busy, so capacity is full. Harvesting and verifying:Checking `math/derivative-bounds-api` in the **store** rather than just the promotion scripts — that's the check I got wrong last time:Scribe 33 is fully landed, and it answered all three questions better than I framed them.\n\n**(1) The vacuous-hypothesis family gets one umbrella but three rules.** *\"Missing semantic-domain guards admit degenerate witnesses\"* unifies the diagnosis; the three mechanisms stay separate because **the repairs differ** — add integrability, require `ContDiff`, bind M in `NNReal`. That's neither option I offered. I'd framed it as one-class-or-three; the right answer was to split diagnosis from remedy.\n\n**(2)** The statement preflight is capturable and became a trajectory draft, broader than and complementing the Bochner diagnostic.\n\n**(3) It declined my \"architecture only\" instinct.** The two opposing cases support recording stable architecture *separately from* provisional steps, with confidence per step — so a runner can tell what's load-bearing from what was the last attempt's guess. That's twelfth correction, twelfth time right, and it's a better rule than mine: my version would have thrown away useful detail; theirs keeps it and labels its status.\n\n**Sixteen amendments now applied tonight**, all append-only with proposer ≠ applier. This round restructured the Bochner memory as an umbrella with three mechanism siblings carrying six problems of evidence, refined the route-override memory, and gave the installed-source-search memory **four consecutive confirmations that public search failed while local declaration search carried the work** — with the ledger caveat recorded *inside* the memory, so the conflation is documented where a future reader of the corpus will actually meet it.\n\nTwo smaller things. I checked `math/derivative-bounds-api` **in the store** this time rather than only in prior promote scripts — the too-narrow check that made me wrongly doubt a handle in pass 32. Present, 7 mentions.\n\nAnd the scribe caught something I dropped: I dispatched it *before* writing a93A04's receipt, so that run was missing from its input list. It resolved the receipt itself and noted the omission. A reviewer that repairs its own brief is worth more than one that follows it.\n\n**State: 42 resolved, 23 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed.** Both runners loaded (a93J07, a93A04 attempt 2). Usage 16%.**a93J07: all three Hurwitz declarations closed axiom-clean, sorries 3 → 0. Thirty.** One flag though — it reports an unused-parameter warning for `hz₀`, and unused hypotheses are exactly how a95J03's vacuity showed up. Dispatching first, then checking that:Important refinement, and it corrects the check I added minutes ago: **`hz₀` is used at line 67** in one declaration and unused in another. An unused hypothesis makes a theorem *stronger*, not vacuous — a95J03's vacuity came from its **conclusion** being about a constant function, with the unused hypotheses only a symptom. Verifying the conclusions here are substantive:Confirmed: the conclusion is `∀ᶠ n in atTop, ∀ z ∈ sphere z₀ r, F n z ≠ 0` — a real Hurwitz statement — and `hz₀` is redundant only because `hclosed : closedBall z₀ r ⊆ Ω` already implies it. Harmless redundancy, not vacuity. All three declarations axiom-clean.**a93J07 closed axiom-clean — thirty problems since yesterday morning.** All three Hurwitz declarations, including zero-persistence via Mathlib's *quantitative* open-mapping theorem: a uniform boundary lower bound puts zero in the approximant's image of a smaller disk.\n\n**But the more useful thing is that I added a bad check this turn and this run disproved it within the hour.**\n\na95J03's vacuous close showed unused `hN` and `hz`, so I wrote *\"an unused-parameter warning is a weak vacuity signal\"* into the park payload. a93J07 then reported one — and `hz₀` turns out to be unused only because `hclosed : closedBall z₀ r ⊆ Ω` already implies it, while the conclusion is entirely substantive.\n\n**An unused hypothesis makes a theorem *stronger*, not vacuous.** I had generalised from a symptom to a test. That's the third time tonight I've over-generalised from one case — but the first time I caught it myself, in the same turn, rather than having the scribe catch it.\n\nThe corrected rule is now in the RESUME doc: **test the conclusion, not the hypotheses.** Find the definitions the conclusion mentions, unfold them, ask whether any is a bare constant or a `True`-typed field. a95J03 fails at that step (`windingNumber := 0`); a93J07 passes (every subject is a genuine analytic object). Unused parameters are a hint to look, never the finding.\n\nWorth being plain about the cost of the wrong version: it would have false-positived on every redundantly-stated theorem in the corpus *and* still missed a vacuous one whose hypotheses happened to be used. A check that fires on the symptom rather than the cause is worse than no check, because it manufactures work while leaving the hole open.\n\n**State: 43 resolved, 22 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a93A04 attempt 2, a94A03). Usage 16%. Scribe 34 will be owed for a93J07, a94A01, and whatever the two in-flight runs return.Chain healthy — both runners busy, both jobs running, 2 real parks. But **heartbeat count is zero**, so arming one per the rule. Then using the quiet wake to bring the summary current — it still says 24 closed:Heartbeat fired on a **healthy** chain — second quiet wake. Both runners busy, both jobs running, heartbeat count zero so I armed exactly one. Four firings now: three caught a stopped chain, one reported health correctly. Worth noting because a dead-man switch that only ever fires on failure tells you nothing about its false-positive rate; this one has now been observed in both states.\n\nI used the wake to **bring the overnight summary current** — it still said twenty-four closed when the count is thirty.\n\nThree substantive edits:\n\n**I removed the pinned corpus figure entirely**, replacing it with \"take it when nothing is in flight — see trap 1.\" A static clean/sorries number in a live document is exactly the working-tree trap I documented and then fell into once already tonight. Better to make the reader take the measurement than to hand them a stale one.\n\n**Rewrote the taxonomy to scribe 33's final structure** — which is neither shape I proposed. One umbrella (*\"missing semantic-domain guards admit degenerate witnesses\"*) with three mechanisms kept **separate because the repairs differ**, laid out as a table so the repair sits next to the mechanism. Constant placeholder and pre-formal elaboration failure sit *outside* the umbrella entirely.\n\n**Gave the statement preflight its own section as the most transferable thing tonight produced** — three checks, none needing a proof attempt, each of which caught a real defect. And I put the check I got **wrong** right next to it, because that half is more instructive: \"unused parameter ⇒ vacuity\" was a symptom mistaken for a test, and a93J07 disproved it within the hour. A reader gets the rule and its known false form together.\n\n**Decisions waiting for you went from three to nine rows**, each carrying `:decision-owner \"joe\"`, the mechanism, the proposed repair, and whether the defect is proved or argued — plus a95J03 flagged `:counts-toward-clean :disputed`. Several offer two or three materially different repairs.\n\nThat's the honest shape of the night: **the loop closed thirty problems and surfaced nine statement-level decisions it deliberately did not make.**a94A03 gives two notable results: it **used the memory I've amended twice tonight** (in its original forward direction), and it **corrected the same stale \"Lp translation continuity\" note that a03J03 corrected** — the second instance of the pessimistic-scaffold class. Dispatching first:A third file carries it. Checking whether it's the same stale claim:a95A02's note is a **different** claim — about `AbsolutelyContinuousOnInterval` and the integral representation being \"only partially available.\" I have no evidence it's wrong, and assuming so would be the over-generalisation I've made three times tonight. But the class has been wrong twice, so the calibrated action is to flag it for verification, not to assert it's stale:**a94A03: second instance of the same false pessimistic scaffold note — that's propagation, not coincidence.**\n\nThe file claimed *\"Mathlib does not have Young's convolution inequality or Lp translation [continuity]\"*; the runner replaced it with the accurate statement that `Continuous.compMeasurePreservingLp` provides translation continuity. **a03J03 carried the identical false claim earlier tonight, and correcting it there later unblocked the close.** This defect class costs invisibly: a runner that believes the note simply avoids a viable route, and no gate ever fires.\n\n**I checked how widespread it is, and then deliberately didn't over-claim.** a95A02 carries a third partial-support note — but about `AbsolutelyContinuousOnInterval`, a *different* claim that may well be accurate. I did **not** mark it stale. I added a `:scaffold-note-caution` asking the next runner to verify before accepting, stating explicitly it isn't an assertion the note is wrong. Two instances of a class is enough to warrant checking the third; it isn't enough to convict it.\n\n**And the memory I amended twice tonight was used in its original forward direction within hours.** `derive-integrable-from-nonzero-bochner-integral` derived kernel integrability *from* the nonzero integral, while my amendments extended it to the reverse — using non-integrability to refute statements. The memory is now load-bearing in both directions, which is a better outcome than either the original or a split would have produced.\n\nIt also reported a **precise boundary** on a second memory rather than a bare verdict: `reduce-probability-kernel-L2-contraction-to-young` correctly identifies the missing Young estimate but is scoped to p = 2, while this target needs general finite p — as is the repository's own `YoungL2.lean`. A memory reported as *accurate-but-too-narrow* is more useful than a hit or a miss, because it names the extension that would make it apply.\n\nThe remaining obstruction is a genuine Mathlib gap: the integral Minkowski/Young step for general finite p, with negative search results carried forward and revision-scoped.\n\n**State: 43 resolved, 22 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a93A04 attempt 2, a94A04). Usage 16%.**a93A04 closed — and the runner did the opposite of what I instructed.** I told it that if the named converse was confirmed absent, mark the row as a Mathlib frontier. Instead it **proved the converse itself** — `sum_edist_le_eVariationOn_of_mem_disjWithin`, added as a reusable lemma. That's thirty-one. Dispatching first:**a93A04 closed axiom-clean — thirty-one problems — and the runner got there by contradicting my instruction.**\n\nAttempt 1 named the missing Mathlib converse precisely. I recorded it as a frontier and told the next runner, in the park payload, to mark the row `:blocked-mathlib-frontier` if the converse was confirmed absent — citing rouche as the cautionary case against re-dispatching. **It built the lemma instead, in one run**: filter degenerate intervals, sort the active subtype by left endpoint via `Tuple.sort`/`Tuple.monotone_sort` through an arbitrary `Fin` equivalence, then use pairwise disjointness to embed the increments in a single monotone partition. All six declarations axiom-clean.\n\n**The lesson is mine and it's a real one: a named Mathlib gap is not automatically a blocker.** I was one instruction away from marking a *constructible* lemma as a permanent frontier. And my own cited precedent undercut me — `rouche-root-count-transfer` had **three** independent attempts before it was marked; a93A04 had one. Naming a gap precisely is what makes it **attemptable**, not what makes it permanent. That rule is now in the RESUME doc.\n\nTwo consequences worth noting. The new lemma is **reusable** beyond this problem — ordering a disjoint interval family into a variation partition is generic. And **scribe 33 had already drafted a frontier memory for exactly this gap**, which is now resolved by *construction* rather than discovery; that memory needs amending to `:resolved` with the lemma named, the same shape as the two a01A07 frontier memories earlier tonight. Owed to scribe 34.\n\nAlso: third time tonight that carried-forward negative results saved a search arm.\n\n**State: 44 resolved, 21 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a94A04, a94A03 attempt 2). Usage 16%.a94A04: statements false for arbitrary μ, via a **sharper structure than plain vacuity**. I checked the arithmetic: with μ = ∑δ_{xₘ}, xₘ = 1−1/(m+2), f(xₘ) = 1/(m+1) — then ∑1/(m+1)² converges so f ∈ L², but xₘⁿf(xₘ) ≥ 2⁻ⁿ/(m+1) is harmonic-divergent, so every monomial integral is junk-zero. **And p(X)=1−X gives p(xₘ)f(xₘ) = 1/((m+1)(m+2)), which is genuinely integrable with telescoping integral 1 ≠ 0** — that's a real counterexample, not just a vacuous hypothesis. Dispatching first:Confirmed by reading both: they quantify over **arbitrary `μ : Measure ℝ` with no finiteness constraint**, and neither assumes integrability of the monomial products. The statement preflight would have caught this — hypotheses mention integrals with nothing guaranteeing they're defined.**a94A04: both targets false, and the counterexample is sharper than vacuity.**\n\nBoth statements quantify over an **arbitrary `μ : Measure ℝ` with no finiteness constraint** and assume no integrability — I read them to confirm rather than take the report. The counterexample uses an atomic measure: μ = ∑δ at xₘ = 1−1/(m+2), f(xₘ) = 1/(m+1). Then f ∈ L² since ∑1/(m+1)² converges, but xₘⁿf(xₘ) ≥ 2⁻ⁿ/(m+1) is harmonic-divergent, so every monomial integral is junk-zero while f is nonzero at every atom.\n\n**The second half is a genuinely stronger form of evidence, and I've folded it into the preflight.** For `orthogonal_to_polynomials`, taking p(X) = 1−X gives p(xₘ)f(xₘ) = 1/((m+1)(m+2)) — **genuinely integrable, telescoping to 1, not 0**. So the conclusion is refuted on an instance where the integral *is* defined. The hypothesis is vacuous, but the falsity is real rather than merely unconstrained.\n\nThat's now step 3 of the statement preflight: **when a hypothesis is found vacuous, ask whether some instance of the conclusion is computable and false — that upgrades \"vacuous\" to \"refuted.\"** It's a meaningful distinction for the morning's decisions: a vacuous statement might be salvaged by adding a hypothesis, but a refuted one is wrong on its own terms.\n\nOne more thing the row records carefully: **the repairs differ per target.** The standalone polynomial lemma needs integrability folded into the moment hypothesis; the main theorem needs finite measure on [0,1], after which L² ⊆ L¹ and the intended Stone–Weierstrass proof works. One mechanism, two repairs — exactly why scribe 33 insisted the mechanisms stay separate under the umbrella.\n\n**State: 44 resolved, 20 untouched, 16 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a94A03 attempt 2, a94A06). Usage 16%. Scribe 34 owed over five runs.a94A03's second attempt found something actionable: **`a95J08` contains the desired general Young architecture but still has four sorries** — a cross-problem dependency. And it used a memory to *prevent* a misuse rather than to build. Dispatching first:Verified — **a95J08 does contain `young_convolution_inequality` and `convolution_memLp`**, exactly the architecture a94A03 is blocked on, with 4 sorries. The cross-problem dependency is real, so dispatching out of priority order was justified.**First queue reorder driven by a runner-reported cross-problem dependency — and I verified it before acting.**\n\na94A03's runner reported that a95J08 holds the general Young architecture it's blocked on. I checked: a95J08 declares `young_convolution_inequality` and `convolution_memLp`, with 4 sorries. Real. So I dispatched a95J08 **deliberately out of priority order** and recorded the link on both rows — `:depends-on-problem \"a95J08\"` on one, `:unblocks-problem \"a94A03\"` on the other — so it survives whoever picks them up. Closing a95J08 is worth more than its own sorry count suggests, and nothing in the priority sort could have known that.\n\n**a94A03 attempt 2 is another headline-zero run with three declarations newly usable** — including the fixed-kernel normalisation `gaussConv_eq_integral_unitKernel`. Six of eight declarations in that file are now axiom-clean; only the two blocked targets carry `sorryAx`.\n\n**And a memory was used to prevent a mistake rather than to build something.** `separate-compact-bump-convergence-from-full-support-poisson-kernels` stopped the runner applying compactly-supported bump convergence to the full-support Gaussian. Nothing was proved with it; something wrong was not done. Counting that as a \"use\" is right, but a memory that averts an error and one that carries a proof are different goods — and `:memory-use/use-mode` is currently the only place that distinction lives. If metric 3 is meant to measure whether the corpus helps, it should probably separate those.\n\nIt also **considered then declined** a memory drafted tonight from a03J03, on the precise ground that it's a related density technique that doesn't supply Young's inequality. The corpus is surfacing its own fresh memories and judging them on what they actually provide rather than topical similarity.\n\n**State: 44 resolved, 20 untouched, 16 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a94A06, a95J08). Usage 16%.**a94A06 closed — both declarations axiom-clean, sorries 2 → 0. Thirty-two.** Dispatching a proving row and **scribe 34**, which is at six runs and shouldn't grow further:The selector picked a94A03 — but that's the row **blocked on a95J08, which is running right now**. Dispatching it would waste a slot on a known blocker. Using the dependency I just recorded and skipping to the next:**a94A06 closed axiom-clean — thirty-two problems since yesterday morning.** All ten declarations in the file re-elaborate clean with zero signature changes; the largest fully-clean file of the night.\n\n**One observation from it is worth more than the close itself.** The proof used `integral_Ioi_of_hasDerivAt_of_nonneg'`, which **produces integrability as an output** — deriving it from a monotone antiderivative with a finite limit rather than demanding it as a hypothesis.\n\nThat's the structural opposite of the junk-value family that broke six problems tonight. a01A10, a01J01, a02J01, a02J03, a02J07 and a94A04 all *assumed* an integral was well-behaved and were satisfied vacuously when it wasn't. A scaffold written around FTC-style lemmas that **establish** integrability wouldn't have been vulnerable to that family at all. That's a design observation about how these problems should be stated, not just a fact about one proof — and it's the closest thing tonight has produced to a *preventive* answer rather than a diagnostic one.\n\n**The dependency I recorded an hour ago immediately earned its keep.** The selector's next pick was a94A03 — blocked on a95J08, currently in flight. Dispatching it would have burned a slot re-attempting a known blocker. I added a skip rule: never dispatch a row whose `:depends-on-problem` is running. It fired on its first use, which is the point of recording a link the priority sort can't see.\n\n**Scribe 34 is dispatched** over six runs, asking it to judge the frontier memory it drafted in pass 33 that was **resolved by construction** hours later — and whether my \"named gap = blocker\" instruction is capturable or *just operator error worth recording as evidence*. I told it to say plainly if it's the latter.\n\n**State: 45 resolved, 19 untouched, 16 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Usage 17%.a95J08 advanced (4 → 2 sorries, three declarations clean) but **`young_convolution_inequality` itself is still open** — so the honest answer to my question is that **it did not unblock a94A03**. The dependency dispatch bought progress, not the unblock. Dispatching first:**The dependency dispatch didn't pay off, and the reason is a granularity error of mine.**\n\nI reordered the queue to send a95J08 early because it holds the Young architecture a94A03 needs. a95J08 advanced — 4 → 2 sorries, three declarations now axiom-clean (translation invariance, convolution integrability via a split at level one, and strong measurability). But **`young_convolution_inequality` itself is still open**, which is the exact lemma a94A03 waits on.\n\n**A dependency is on a lemma, not on a problem.** I recorded it at problem granularity, and my skip rule — \"skip a94A03 while a95J08 is in flight\" — would have released it the moment a95J08 returned, straight back into the same wall. Both rows now name the specific lemma, and the skip persists until *that* closes.\n\n**And a third memory use-mode appeared, the most interesting yet: memories used as scope guards against over-claiming.** The runner consumed `bound-automatic-frontier-descent-when-a-leaf-recurses` and `bound-the-interface-adapter-heuristic-with-genuine-construction-cases` to conclude that *\"the completed existence/measurability layer does not imply the remaining Young estimate is adapter work.\"*\n\nThose are **meta-memories about claim discipline**, used to stop the runner declaring the remaining work trivial. Neither carried a proof; both constrained an inference about progress. That's the claim-discipline lane paying off *inside a proving run* — the first time I've seen it happen rather than asserted it would.\n\nWith a94A03's prevented-misapplication case, that's now two use-modes the benchmark scores identically with \"a memory carried the proof.\" A memory that stops a runner from a wrong turn and one that supplies the proof are both wins, but they're different wins, and metric 3 currently flattens them.\n\n**State: 45 resolved, 19 untouched, 16 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Usage 17%.Scribe 34 answered all three and confirmed each. Notably on (1): my \"named gap = blocker\" error **is** capturable — as a frontier-lifecycle rule: *\"library absence identifies a construction target, not automatically a durable blocker; record attempt history before making that transition.\"* And it caught the a94A06 receipt I'd omitted from the brief — second time it has repaired its own inputs.\n\nBoth runners are busy, so processing the scribe:**Zero real parks — the chain has stopped again.** Caught it by checking at turn end rather than waiting for the heartbeat. Checking what happened to the two in-flight jobs:Both completed while I was processing the scribe — their results are pending delivery and the chain has no park. **Dispatching two immediately**, both runners idle:Scribe 34 landed — but the more important thing is what I found checking park state at the end of the turn.\n\n**Pass 34 confirmed all three questions.** On the frontier one it declined the exit I offered (\"or is it just operator error\"): my mistake **is** capturable, as a **frontier-lifecycle rule** — *library absence identifies a construction target, not automatically a durable blocker; record attempt history before making that transition*. That's more useful than either \"claude-9 erred\" or a vague caution, because it names the missing datum: rouche had three failed attempts before marking, a93A04 had one.\n\nIt also accepted **both** new use-modes — `:prevented-misapplication` and `:scope-boundary-confirmed` — and I applied four more amendments, **twenty tonight**, all append-only with proposer ≠ applier. Three drafts promoted and reviewed into existing patterns. It again repaired its own brief, catching the a94A06 receipt I'd omitted.\n\n**Then: the chain had stopped a fourth time, and the cause was new.** a94J04 and a94J07 both completed *while I was processing the scribe*. Their parks were consumed by completion, the long promote-and-amend block occupied the turn, and I ended with zero real parks.\n\n**Dispatch-first protects against ending a turn without dispatching. It does not protect against a turn long enough for the in-flight work to finish inside it.** The rule that follows: after any long processing block, re-check whether the chain still has a real park before finishing.\n\nI caught this myself rather than being rescued by the heartbeat — the first time. Dispatched two immediately and parked.\n\n**And two results are unprocessed.** a94J04 and a94J07 completed but were never verified or receipted; their bellbacks haven't arrived as turns. I've named both job ids in the new park payload with an instruction to collect them via the jobs endpoint first. A completed job whose result was never processed is