is how it closed.** The runner abandoned the scaffold's Rouché route entirely for a direct Lipschitz perturbation argument: on the unit disk `cexp` is exp(1)-Lipschitz and z↦z³ is 3-Lipschitz, and since exp(1)+3 < 8 the linear term dominates and forces injectivity.\n\n**The scaffold's *naming* had baked in the route assumption** — `rouche_deriv_bound` and `rouche_f_bound` are now proved without Rouché, and the names still say otherwise. That's a subtler form of the stale-guidance class than the three prose notes tonight: a comment can be corrected, but a **name** can't be without changing the statement. The runner was right to leave them alone.\n\n**And this sharpens the Rouché frontier finding without overturning it.** `rouche-root-count-transfer` remains a genuine Mathlib frontier on three independent confirmations — that stands. But a problem *named* for Rouché just closed without it, so **the frontier blocks the route, not necessarily every scaffold that assumed that route.**\n\nWhether other Rouché-blocked rows have non-Rouché routes available is worth checking — but I've explicitly recorded that as a hypothesis for the morning, **not** a finding. I've over-generalised three times tonight and been corrected each time; one counterexample to \"Rouché-named ⇒ Rouché-needed\" doesn't license re-opening the frontier row.\n\nBoth unprocessed results are now cleared. **State: 46 resolved, 17 untouched, 16 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 dispatched.** Chain healthy — one real park awaiting two jobs, heartbeat behind it. Usage 17%.**Fourth instance confirmed — the class is systemic.** The runner found the file's stated obstruction was stale: current Mathlib has `AbsolutelyContinuousOnInterval.integral_deriv_eq_sub`, `.intervalIntegrable_deriv`, and `Integrable.tendsto_setIntegral_nhds_zero`. My caution flagged it for verification without asserting it was wrong — and it was wrong. Dispatching first:Decisive — the diff shows **multiple** stale claims removed from the same file: *\"a deep result that is only partially [available]\"* → *\"Current Mathlib gives both the ε-δ definition and the integral representation\"*, plus *\"This bridge is not fully available in Mathlib\"*. Three new declarations, zero changed, zero removed.**Fourth confirmed instance — the stale-pessimistic-note class is systemic.** a95A02's header claimed the AC-to-integral-representation bridge was *\"a deep result only partially available in Mathlib\"* and *\"not fully available.\"* Current Mathlib has all three needed lemmas. Multiple stale claims in one file, all removed.\n\nWith a03J03, a94A03 and a94J04: **four for four — every partial-support note examined tonight was false.**\n\n**The calibration matters more than the finding.** Two hours ago I added a `:scaffold-note-caution` to this row explicitly stating it was *not* a claim the note was wrong — only that the class had been wrong twice and deserved verification. It turned out wrong. Had I marked it stale on suspicion I'd have been right by luck and wrong as a method. Two instances justified asking; four make it a standing check. That's the distinction I've been getting wrong all night in the other direction — over-generalising from one or two cases — and this is the first time I set the threshold correctly in advance.\n\n**The runner also pre-empted the consolidation concern unprompted**, reporting that \"none were relocated through another sorry-bearing declaration.\" That's exactly the metric-integrity failure I found on a01A07 attempt 1 — headline movement with zero genuine discharge — now appearing spontaneously in a runner's self-report rather than being caught by my review.\n\nThree new axiom-clean declarations, headline delta zero, statements unaltered. Both non-component arms genuinely empty: Zulip returned only unrelated Lusin–Souslin material, and an arXiv manifest query for \"Lusin condition N\" returned no rows — recorded so the next attempt doesn't repeat them.\n\n**State: 46 resolved, 16 untouched, 17 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 18%. Scribe 35 is owed over six runs and I'll dispatch it next turn.**a94J08 closed — sorries 2 → 0, all four declarations axiom-clean. Thirty-four.** The construction is elegant: `quadraticQuotient f z = if z = 0 then 0 else z²/f z`, whose lower bound gives ‖·‖ ≤ ‖z‖^(1/2); it extends holomorphically at zero, and Cauchy's estimate at radius R forces every derivative to vanish as R → ∞.\n\nDispatching a row and **scribe 35**, which is at six runs:The park missed scribe 35 — its id came back in the same block. Adding a park for it, then verifying a94J08:**a94J08 closed axiom-clean — thirty-four problems since yesterday morning.** All nine declarations in the file clean, 5 added / 0 changed / 0 removed.\n\nThe construction is worth keeping: `quadraticQuotient f z = if z = 0 then 0 else z²/f z`. The assumed lower bound gives ‖·‖ ≤ ‖z‖^(1/2); it extends holomorphically across zero by removable singularity; and Cauchy's estimate at radius R forces every derivative to vanish as R → ∞. That's a **sublinear-growth form of Liouville** assembled from removable singularity plus Cauchy estimate — the standard bounded-entire statement doesn't apply to a function only known to grow like ‖z‖^(1/2).\n\nTwo smaller notes. Arms (b) and (c) were correctly **skipped** because arm (a) supplied a complete route — I recorded that as a case where *not* searching was right, since I've been counting empty arms all night and \"searched and found nothing\" differs from \"correctly didn't search\" for the payoff ledger.\n\nAnd I made the batching error again: composed the park in the same command block as the scribe dispatch, so scribe 35's job id wasn't in the `awaiting` list. Caught it on read-back and added a second park. **Third occurrence tonight** — the rule is written in the RESUME doc and I keep violating it when dispatch-and-park *feel* like one action. They aren't, and knowing the rule clearly isn't sufficient; I should be composing the park from a variable I read back rather than typing it alongside.\n\n**Scribe 35 is dispatched over seven runs**, with the build-config defect as the explicit priority: whether \"check whether the lemma already exists in-repo but off the module path\" is capturable, whether the 4/4 stale-note class yields a *check* or a *calibration* or both, and whether \"scaffold naming can encode a stale route\" is separable from the prose-note class.\n\n**State: 47 resolved, 15 untouched, 17 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 18%.Scribe 35 sharpened all three. Most usefully on (1): YoungL2 reachability isn't a new memory but a **second, stronger instance of an existing construction-delivery memory** — amend it with explicit delivery states *through consumer import and field use*. That's better than my framing, which treated it as novel.\n\nBoth runners busy, so processing the scribe:Two targets stand out: **`make-construction-targets-buildable-importable-and-...`** (the construction-delivery memory) and **`treat-not-in-mathlib-comments-as-revision-scoped-...`** — so the corpus *already* had a memory for the stale-note class, which I'd been treating as a fresh finding all night. Promoting:Post-block check caught two things: **heartbeat count is zero**, and **codex-7 is free** — so capacity isn't full. Arming and filling:Scribe 35 landed, and it corrected my framing on all three questions — but one correction is worth sitting with.\n\n**The corpus already held a memory for the stale-note class**: `treat-not-in-mathlib-comments-as-revision-scoped-claims`. Four instances, a caution I designed, a calibration I was pleased with — all of it rediscovery. That's the **second time tonight** the scribe has shown me the corpus already knew something I was reporting as new; the first was the lexical-sorry-count metric. The corpus is consistently ahead of me on the one thing I'm slowest to check: whether it already knows.\n\n**And it sharpened the build defect from \"new finding\" to \"second, stronger instance.\"** YoungL2's unreachability is the *same* delivery failure as `make-construction-targets-buildable-importable-and-consumed`, one level deeper — not promotion, but the module path. The amendment now records explicit **delivery states: proved; on the module path; imported by a consumer; used in the field.** *A lemma is not delivered until a consumer imports it.* That's a better rule than I'd have written, because it names the states rather than the incident.\n\n**Twenty-four amendments tonight.** The stale-note memory now carries all four instances and **both** capturable halves — the check and the calibration. The route-override memory extends to declaration names, with the Rouché frontier nuanced but not overturned. The preflight memory gains the strongest outcome shape for a false statement: **pair a machine-checked refutation with a machine-checked repair**, which turns your decision from \"is this repair right?\" into \"do we adopt it?\"\n\n**The post-block check I added an hour ago paid on its second use** — it caught a zero heartbeat count *and* an idle runner, neither of which the job notifications would have surfaced. It also caught that a95A02's second attempt completed unprocessed during the scribe block, the same way a94J04 and a94J07 did earlier.\n\nThat's now twice: **work finishes while I'm writing about work.** Throughput and bookkeeping compete for the same turn, and the bookkeeping is what makes the throughput trustworthy — so the answer isn't to do less of it, but to keep re-checking the chain across it.\n\n**State: 47 resolved, 15 untouched, 17 blocked-and-diagnosed.** Both runners loaded, heartbeat armed, usage 19%.Chain healthy but **two completed results are unprocessed** (a95A04, a95A02 attempt 2) and codex-6 is idle. My processing is now the bottleneck, not the runners — so I'll dispatch **one** rather than two to let the backlog clear:**a95A04 closed axiom-clean — thirty-five problems since yesterday morning.** Dyadic interval formula by induction with midpoint splitting, adjacent cells summed for the primitive at dyadic points, then lower-dyadic approximation via floor bounds plus primitive continuity, with Lebesgue differentiation only at the final step. All five declarations clean.\n\n**But the operationally important thing is that this result sat unprocessed for about twenty minutes** while I promoted scribe 35 — the **third** completion tonight to finish inside a long bookkeeping block, after a94J04/a94J07 and a95A02's second attempt.\n\nThat's now a structural property of the loop as I've configured it, not an accident: **the proving runners outpace my verification-and-receipt cycle, so completed work queues behind my own writing.**\n\n**So I've throttled the inflow rather than the checks** — dispatched one row this turn instead of two, and recorded `:processing-lag-minutes 20` with the mitigation on the receipt so the tradeoff shows up in the evidence rather than only in my behaviour. Two runners at full tilt produce results faster than I can verify them properly, and verifying them properly is the entire reason I'm in this loop instead of a cron. Cutting the checks to keep up would defeat the arrangement; reducing inflow doesn't.\n\nThe queue is down to **13 dispatchable untouched rows**, so I've also advised preferring **re-attempts** on rows carrying a `:suggested-route` over fresh rows — those come with committed supports and recorded negative results, making them cheaper per unit of progress as the fresh pool thins.\n\na95A02's second attempt is the last unprocessed result — blocked with a compiling partial, and it found no packaged decomposition of an arbitrary open real set into ordered disjoint intervals. I'll verify and receipt it next.\n\n**State: 48 resolved, 13 untouched, 17 blocked-and-diagnosed, 5 in flight.** Usage 19%.a95A02's second attempt found a **new defect class: a measurability-level type mismatch.** Part (c) asks for Borel measurability, but the route yields only completed/Lebesgue measurability — `volume.IsComplete` isn't an instance, and descriptive set theory says continuous Borel images are *analytic*, not necessarily Borel. Dispatching first:Confirmed: **zero signature changes** (11 before, 11 after), and the target is `MeasurableSet (f '' E)` — which on ℝ with Mathlib's ambient Borel σ-algebra means **Borel**, not Lebesgue. The mismatch is real.**a95A02's second attempt found a new defect mechanism: a measurability-level type mismatch.**\n\nPart (c) targets `MeasurableSet (f '' E)` — which on ℝ under Mathlib's ambient σ-algebra means **Borel** — while the available route proves only completed/Lebesgue measurability, and `volume.IsComplete` isn't an instance. The runner checked the descriptive-set-theory background: **continuous images of Borel sets are analytic and need not be Borel**. So this isn't a gap that more effort closes; the statement asks for a strictly stronger conclusion than the mathematics delivers. Zero signature changes, and I've flagged it for the scribe rather than folding it into an existing class myself.\n\n**The runner applied the transitive-`sorryAx` discipline unprompted** — stating that `ac_maps_null_to_null` has no direct sorry but remains `sorryAx`-dependent and \"is not claimed axiom-clean.\" That's the a01A07 lesson volunteered in a runner's own report rather than caught by my review; second time tonight.\n\n**Two routes were evaluated and rejected with reasons, which is worth as much as the remaining plan.** The Jacobian estimate is *circular* here — AC gives differentiability only a.e., and controlling the image of the exceptional null set *is* the target. The Stieltjes route is plausible but needs a substantial clamped-extension layer. Both recorded as \"do not re-derive.\"\n\nAnd the row now separates its **two obstructions by kind**: a genuine proof gap (Mathlib has no packaged decomposition of an arbitrary open real set into ordered disjoint intervals) versus a statement defect needing your decision. Previously I'd have recorded one \"blocked\" status for both, which would have sent the next runner at an unfixable target alongside a fixable one.\n\nI also dispatched **a95J08 attempt 2** — the row that unblocks a94A03 — with a note that YoungL2 is now importable, which it wasn't when attempt 1 ran.\n\n**State: 48 resolved, 13 untouched, 17 blocked-and-diagnosed, 5 in flight.** Usage 19%.This is the bellback for a95A04, which I processed from the **truncated** jobs output — and it contains the Memory usage section I didn't have. My receipt is wrong:**I recorded a metric-3 outcome I hadn't read, and it was wrong in the memory system's disfavour.**\n\na95A04's receipt says `:NOT-MEASURABLE-recall-timed-out`, `:recall-outcome :timeout`, `used-ids []`. The runner actually reported `[dispatch-recall-outcome=completed-with-memories]` and **used a memory that supplied the dyadic-induction plus Lebesgue-differentiation architecture** for a problem that closed axiom-clean. Correction receipt written.\n\n**The cause is mine, and it's the exact mirror of the discipline I've been enforcing all night.** The run finished during a scribe-promotion block, so I collected it via the jobs endpoint and printed only the first 1500 characters — the Memory usage section sits at the *end* of every report and was cut off. I then filled the fields with the session's **modal** value, `:timeout`, instead of recording them unknown.\n\nI have insisted repeatedly that a failed recall be logged `:NOT-MEASURABLE` rather than zero, because absence of evidence isn't evidence of absence. **Defaulting an unknown to the mode is the same error**, and it biases in whichever direction the mode happens to lie — here, against the memory system, on a run where a memory carried the whole architecture. If I hadn't been re-shown this report, the benchmark would have carried a false negative in exactly the direction that makes the corpus look less useful than it is.\n\nRule added to the RESUME doc: collecting via the jobs endpoint means printing the whole result or grepping the Memory usage section; if genuinely unavailable, record `:UNKNOWN-RESULT-TRUNCATED` and never a specific outcome.\n\nThat's the **fourth correction receipt** of the session — run-24's false fragmentation diagnosis, the a01A12 corpus arithmetic, the taxonomy over-generalisation, and now this. Every one was caught, and every one was mine. The pattern I'd note for the morning: three of the four came from filling a gap with a plausible value rather than leaving it empty.\n\n**State: 48 resolved, 13 untouched, 17 blocked-and-diagnosed, 5 in flight.** Usage 19%.a95A06 formally refuted its natural-boundary target with a **machine-checked** counterexample: U = ball 0 1 ∪ ball 3 1 (disjoint), F = the series on the unit ball and 0 elsewhere. That's a **missing connectedness hypothesis** — second instance of a94J02's missing-topological-hypothesis mechanism, and machine-checked from the start. Dispatching first:Confirmed: the target is `¬∃ U F, IsOpen U ∧ ball 0 1 ⊂ U ∧ DifferentiableOn ℂ F U ∧ ...` — **no connectedness requirement on U at all**, so a disjoint second ball satisfies it. One diff entry looks like a regex artifact; checking before I record it:The diff entry was a regex artifact — line 26 of the parent is a **prose** line, *\"theorem is not in Mathlib.\"*, inside a docstring. Not a removal. (Fittingly, that sentence is itself a stale-note-class claim.) Axioms: 8 clean, only the false target carrying `sorryAx`.**a95A06: second instance of the missing-topological-hypothesis mechanism, and this one is machine-checked from the start.**\n\nThe target asserts `¬∃ U F` with `IsOpen U`, `ball 0 1 ⊂ U`, and F differentiable on U agreeing with the series — **but imposes no connectedness on U**. So U = ball 0 1 ∪ ball 3 1, with F the series on one ball and 0 on the other, satisfies the existential. `lacunarySeries_has_disconnected_extension` proves exactly that, axiom-clean. With a94J02's missing `IsClosed`, that's two under-constrained-topology defects — a genuinely distinct family from the junk-value totalisation one.\n\n**The intended mathematics is true and the formalisation forgot one word.** Hadamard's gap theorem holds; \"extends past the boundary\" requires the extension domain to be *connected*, and the Lean statement omitted it. Same shape as a01A10's Fresnel target — true mathematics, broken formalisation — and a far cheaper repair than a false theorem.\n\n**One verification worth flagging.** My signature diff reported a *removed* declaration named `is`. A false removal is the most alarming thing that diff can say — it would mean a runner deleted a statement — so I checked before recording it. Line 26 of the parent file is the prose sentence *\"theorem is not in Mathlib.\"* inside a docstring, which my regex matched. Not a declaration. Fittingly, the sentence that caused the artifact is itself a stale-note-class claim.\n\nAlso: `lacunarySeries_differentiableOn` closed axiom-clean via the Weierstrass locally-uniform-limit theorem, and 8 of 9 declarations in the file are clean.\n\n**arXiv has now been entered roughly a dozen times tonight and has never carried a construction.** Here it surfaced four genuinely relevant papers, all background. The arm isn't useless — it confirms the mathematics is standard — but its payoff profile is well established across the ledger now.\n\n**State: 48 resolved, 12 untouched, 18 blocked-and-diagnosed, 5 in flight.** Usage 19%.a95J08's second attempt did **not** close `young_convolution_inequality` — so a94A03 remains blocked. But two things are new: Zulip's \"Discrete analysis\" thread **explicitly confirms** Young's inequality and convolution infrastructure are missing from Mathlib, and arXiv supplied a genuine **literature anchor** (1704.08922, *Minkowski Integral Inequality Revisited*). Dispatching first:The refresher's fixed rule fired correctly — it **kept** `convolution_memLp` rather than dropping it as \"PROVED\" (that's the transitive-`sorryAx` fix I made earlier tonight). But then it skipped, which may leave the hint pointing at a declaration with no direct sorry. Checking:**My earlier fix to the refresher created a follow-on problem.** It correctly *kept* `convolution_memLp` (not dropping it as \"PROVED\"), but then found no direct sorry among the hint's declarations and skipped — leaving the hint pointing at line 130, which isn't a sorry. The real sorry is at 121, in `young_convolution_inequality`, which the hint doesn't name at all.\n\nThe pre-dispatch assert would catch it as a SKIP, so the row would silently drop out of rotation. Fixing both:**My own fix to the hint refresher created a follow-on failure, and I caught it one row later.**\n\nEarlier tonight I stopped the refresher calling transitively-sorried declarations \"PROVED\" and dropping them from hints. That was correct. But on a95J08 the consequence was that *every* hinted declaration became transitively sorried — so the refresher found no direct sorry among them, **skipped**, and left `:line [130]` pointing at a hypothesis line. The real sorry is at 121, in `young_convolution_inequality`, which the hint didn't name at all.\n\n**The failure mode is quiet in the worst way.** The pre-dispatch assert would have *rejected* the row — so a95J08, **the row that unblocks a94A03**, would have silently dropped out of rotation rather than being dispatched wrongly. Safe, but invisible: I'd have seen \"SKIP (stale hint)\" scroll past and moved on.\n\nFixed both halves: repointed the hint at line 121 naming `young_convolution_inequality` as the open target and stating that `convolution_memLp` is `sorryAx`-dependent and not proved; and added a loud warning to the refresher for the all-transitive case. **A fix that trades a wrong answer for a silent skip is only half a fix** — the skip has to announce itself.\n\nOn a95J08 itself: headline 2 → 1 with **genuine discharges zero** (the consolidation shape), and the runner flagged that itself — third time tonight one has volunteered the a01A07 discipline. The gap is now **community-confirmed**: a Zulip thread explicitly records Young's inequality and convolution infrastructure as missing from Mathlib, with a literature anchor at arXiv 1704.08922.\n\nThat anchor also **softens a claim I made one turn ago**. I said arXiv \"has never carried a construction\" — still true, but it *did* supply a named relevant anchor here. The precise finding is: arXiv produces **anchors**, never **constructions**. I'd compressed two outcomes into one dismissal.\n\nAnd per scribe 34's frontier-lifecycle rule, **two failed attempts is not yet a frontier** — rouche had three. The row is `:attempts 2`, `:frontier-candidate-after 3`.\n\n**State: 48 resolved, 12 untouched, 18 blocked-and-diagnosed, 5 in flight.****a95J02 closed — sorries 2 → 0, all three declarations axiom-clean. Thirty-six.** Elegant route: Liouville on 1/f gives f(0)=0, analytic order factorisation f(z)=zⁿg(z), then G(z)=zⁿ/f(z) extended across zero forces λ=n and G≡1. Dispatching first:**a95J02 closed axiom-clean — thirty-six problems since yesterday morning.** A clean chain of four classical theorems: Liouville on 1/f gives f(0)=0, analytic zero-order factorisation writes f(z)=zⁿg(z), G(z)=zⁿ/f(z) extends across zero by removable singularity, and bounds at both ends force λ=n with Liouville again giving G≡1.\n\n**That's the third problem tonight closed by removable-singularity-plus-Liouville**, after a94J08's quadratic quotient and a94J07's Lipschitz variant. It's become a recognisable house technique in the complex-analysis rows — worth flagging to the scribe as a candidate for one consolidated memory rather than three near-siblings, since the corpus already has a concentration problem.\n\n**The corrected vacuity check behaved exactly as intended.** The file carries a pre-existing unused-parameter warning for `hlam`. Under my *first* version of that rule it would have been flagged as a vacuity signal; under the corrected version — test the **conclusion**, not the hypotheses — the placeholder scan is clean and the declaration re-elaborates axiom-clean. A rule I got wrong and corrected within the hour has now silently prevented a false positive.\n\nClean benchmark negative too: 5 surfaced, 0 used, all five declined **by subject area**.\n\n**And I've dispatched a95J08 attempt 3 — the decisive frontier test.** Two prior failures on `young_convolution_inequality`, the gap community-confirmed by Zulip, a literature anchor found, and the hint repointed by hand after the refresher couldn't. The park payload **pre-commits the decision**: if this attempt fails, mark `:blocked-mathlib-frontier` naming the lemma (matching rouche's three-attempt precedent); if it succeeds, a94A03 unblocks immediately.\n\nI wrote it that way deliberately — I've spent the night discovering that I judge better in advance than in the moment, and leaving this to whatever I feel on seeing the result is exactly how a93A04 nearly got mis-marked as a permanent frontier.\n\n**State: 49 resolved, 11 untouched, 18 blocked-and-diagnosed, 5 in flight.** Usage 19%.a95A02 attempt 3: **the \"missing topological decomposition\" is now proved axiom-clean** — four new declarations. And the runner's note is the key: *\"Mathlib has no single packaged open-set decomposition theorem, but its component and separability APIs are sufficient to construct one.\"* Dispatching first:**a95A02's third attempt proved the \"missing\" open-set decomposition axiom-clean** — four new declarations, headline delta zero. Built from `ordConnectedComponent`/`ordConnectedSection` plus `Set.PairwiseDisjoint.countable_of_isOpen`, rather than by manually sorting arbitrary intervals — which is exactly what attempt 2 assumed was required and stopped on.\n\n**And it corrects my own record.** Attempt 2 reported, and I filed in the row, that Mathlib has \"no packaged decomposition of an arbitrary open real set into ordered disjoint intervals\" — recorded as a genuine *proof gap*, distinct from the part (c) statement defect. The observation was true; the inference wasn't. The runner's correction is exact: no single packaged theorem exists, but the component and separability APIs suffice to construct one. **I turned \"not found as a unit\" into \"not available.\"**\n\n**That's 2 for 2 tonight on named gaps being constructed**, after a93A04. And it bears directly on a decision I pre-committed an hour ago: a95J08's third attempt is in flight under the rule that a third failure marks it `:blocked-mathlib-frontier`.\n\n**I'm not loosening that rule on two counterexamples.** Rouché went three attempts and stayed genuinely blocked. But the distinction is now sharp, and I've written it into the record: *\"Mathlib has no packaged X\" is a claim about **effort**, not possibility. A frontier is what survives repeated **attempts**, not what a **search** failed to find.* That's a meaningfully different bar from the one I was applying at the start of the night.\n\nThe row now carries a concrete four-step plan built *on top* of the proved decomposition — endpoints, outer-measure bound, sum over the countable family, `tendsto_setIntegral_nhds_zero` — plus both rejected routes with reasons, and part (c)'s Borel defect kept separate and flagged \"do not attempt as stated.\"\n\n**State: 49 resolved, 11 untouched, 18 blocked-and-diagnosed, 5 in flight.** Usage 19%. a95J08's decisive third attempt is still running.a95J04's target is false — and the cause is a **`True`-typed hypothesis**: `hR : True`, a no-op where \"radius of convergence exactly one\" was intended. Counterexample `D = Set.univ`, `f ≡ 0`. That's the second `True` placeholder after a01J06's `hEntire`, and it exposes a gap in my own scan. Dispatching first:Confirmed — and the scan found a **second** one I'd have missed: **a95J06 has `(hf_ac_compl : True)`**. That's a no-op hypothesis in a row that hasn't been attempted yet, so for the first time tonight I can flag a defect **before** a runner hits it:**a95J04's target is false because of a `True`-typed hypothesis — and finding it let me flag a second problem before any runner touched it.**\n\n`exists_pole_on_boundary` binds `(hR : True)`, a no-op where a non-extendability condition belongs. So `D = Set.univ` with `f ≡ 0` satisfies every hypothesis while being differentiable at every boundary point. The runner **compiled** that counterexample (exit 0) but didn't commit it — so I've recorded the status as **verified-but-not-in-repo**, its own value between \"argued\" and \"machine-checked\" rather than rounding to either.\n\n**My placeholder scan had a gap, and fixing it paid immediately.** I was matching `True`-typed structure *fields* (a01J06's `hEntire`) but not inline binders `(h : True)`. Grepping for the inline form found **a95J06's `(hf_ac_compl : True)`** — in a row not yet attempted.\n\n**So for the first time tonight I've flagged a statement defect before a runner hit it.** Every other defect this session cost a dispatch to discover — roughly ten runner-hours across the night. The caution on a95J06 names the pattern and its two prior instances, says what to verify, and states explicitly that it is *not* a claim the target is false. That's the same calibration that worked on a95A02, where the flagged note did turn out stale.\n\nThree placeholder forms are now covered in the RESUME doc's vacuity section: bare-constant `def`, `True`-typed field, `True`-typed inline binder — with the grep.\n\nWorth noting what this changes: the statement preflight has been a *per-run* check the runner performs. Scanning the queue directly makes it a **corpus-level** check I can run once and act on across every remaining row. That's the difference between finding defects at one per dispatch and finding them in a batch.\n\n**State: 49 resolved, 10 untouched, 19 blocked-and-diagnosed, 5 in flight.** Usage 19%. a95J08's decisive third attempt is still running.The decisive test resolved — and with a refinement that matters. **The third attempt failed on the general case, so the pre-committed rule fires: `:blocked-mathlib-frontier`.** But the runner proved `young_convolution_inequality_one` axiom-clean and wired it in, so **the remaining sorry is now formally confined to 1 < p < ∞**. The frontier is narrower than I stated it. Dispatching first:Both runners busy, so capacity is full — and the selector's next pick is a95J06, the row I just flagged predictively. Now executing the pre-committed decision:Confirmed precisely: `by_cases hpeq : p = 1` discharges the p=1 branch with the proved lemma, and the sorry sits in the else branch — **formally confined to 1 < p < ∞**. Executing the pre-committed decision:**The pre-committed frontier decision fired, and I honoured it as written.** a95J08 → `:blocked-mathlib-frontier` after three independent failed attempts. The evidence quality is unusually good for such a call: three failures, a Zulip thread stating explicitly that Young's inequality and convolution infrastructure are *missing* from Mathlib, a literature anchor verified against the local manifest (Soudský 1704.08922 — scaffold, no Lean), and a precise statement of what's missing. The runner reached the same conclusion independently and labelled its own outcome the same way.\n\n**But the frontier is narrower than I'd stated it, and recording it wholesale would have hidden a proved branch.** The runner proved `young_convolution_inequality_one` axiom-clean and wired it in via `by_cases hpeq : p = 1`. I read the proof term to confirm: the p=1 branch discharges from the proved lemma, and the sorry sits in the else branch — **formally confined to 1 < p < ∞**. So the frontier is the weighted Hölder/Jensen estimate `(∫w·v)^p ≤ (∫w)^(p−1)·∫w·v^p` plus Tonelli assembly, not the lemma.\n\n**I checked myself against loosening the bar rather than just applying it.** Two problems tonight — a93A04 and a95A02 — had named gaps *constructed* on a later attempt, which is genuine evidence that \"missing\" often means \"unassembled\". But rouche went three and stayed blocked, and the alternative to a three-attempt rule is re-dispatching a confirmed gap forever.\n\nThat's the case for pre-committing at all: **the moment of decision is exactly when the reasons to bend the rule are most available.** I had two fresh counterexamples in hand and a plausible story for why this one might be different. Writing the rule down an hour earlier, when I had neither, is what made it hold.\n\na94A03's dependency is sharpened again — it needs general finite p so it stays blocked, but the row now notes that if its targets reduce to p=1, or if YoungL2's now-importable p=2 contraction suffices, it may be tractable *without* the frontier. Recorded as untested.\n\n**State: 49 resolved, 10 untouched, 20 blocked-and-diagnosed (2 now confirmed frontiers), 4 in flight.** Usage 19%.**a95J05 closed — sorries 2 → 0, all five declarations axiom-clean. Thirty-seven.** And metric 3 is measurable with **two memories used**. Dispatching a95J06 next — the row I flagged predictively for `(hf_ac_compl : True)`, which will test whether that caution helps:**The predictive caution did not reach the packet.** `:placeholder-hypothesis-caution` is a custom field the template doesn't render — so my flag is in the queue and invisible to the runner. Same class as the truncated route earlier. Checking what the template actually renders:**a95J05 closed axiom-clean — thirty-seven problems since yesterday morning.** The decisive move was bridging the file's *strict*-threshold `TendstoInMeasureZero` with Mathlib's *closed*-threshold `TendstoInMeasure` — a definitional mismatch that blocked every Mathlib API until resolved.\n\n**But I have to flag a failure of mine from this turn, because it undercuts the win I claimed an hour ago.**\n\nI said I'd finally got ahead of a defect by flagging a95J06's `(hf_ac_compl : True)` before dispatch. **The caution never reached the runner.** I recorded it as `:placeholder-hypothesis-caution`, a custom row key — and the packet template renders only eight fields. a95J06's runner is working right now with no warning at all.\n\nI found this only because I checked *after* dispatching. I've moved the caution into `:suggested-route`, verified it renders by instantiating the packet and grepping, and added the rule to the RESUME doc: **a queue field is not a communication channel.**\n\nThat's the **second time tonight** a carefully-written caution failed to reach a packet — the first was the duplicate-key truncation that dropped a01A07's `sorryAx` warning mid-string. Different mechanism, same result. **Writing the warning is the easy half; delivering it is the half that keeps failing**, and both times I only noticed by accident rather than by checking.\n\nOn the memory system, a pattern worth naming: **both memories used here addressed definitional mismatch rather than proof strategy** — how to connect the file's own predicates to Mathlib's. Across tonight, the corpus has been consumed far more for **interface translation** than for mathematics. The memories that carried proofs (a01A07's frontier pair, LemniscateComponents) are the minority.\n\n**State: 50 resolved, 9 untouched, 20 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 20%.a95A02's fourth attempt adds `ac_volume_image_subset_Icc_le_integral_norm_deriv` axiom-clean — the image-measure step, proved **without any measurability assumption on S or f''S** via `Real.volume_le_diam`. Both targets still open, but this exposes a refinement to my frontier rule. Dispatching first:**a95A02's fourth attempt sharpened my frontier rule, and the sharpening is the real result of this turn.**\n\nThe rule was \"three independent failed attempts justifies marking a frontier.\" That's wrong as stated. **Count attempts that fail at the same point, not attempts total.**\n\na95J08 was marked a frontier an hour ago on three attempts that all hit the *same* weighted Hölder/Jensen estimate — a genuine wall. a95A02 now has **four** attempts and is emphatically not a frontier, because each advanced a *different* piece: three AC lemmas, then the deduction plus part-(c) diagnosis, then the open-set decomposition it had itself reported missing, then this image-measure step. Four attempts, four pieces, sixteen declarations — a long **assembly**, not a wall. Marking it on attempt count alone would have been exactly wrong, and an hour ago my rule couldn't have told the two apart.\n\nThe new lemma is nicely scoped too: it bounds volume(f(S)) by the integral of ‖deriv f‖ **with no measurability assumption on S or f''S**, via `Real.volume_le_diam` — outer measure needs no measurability. That removes the separate image-diameter argument the plan had anticipated.\n\nTwo things worth flagging for the morning. **Part (c) was searched specifically this run and remains unresolved in both directions** — only the general Lusin–Souslin result under injectivity surfaced, no AC-specific Borel-image theorem *and* no counterexample. That's more useful than recording it as \"blocked\", because it tells you the decision is genuinely open rather than un-investigated. And both non-component arms were empty: an arXiv manifest query for three spellings of \"Lusin condition (N)\" returned **no records at all**.\n\n**State: 50 resolved, 9 untouched, 20 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 20%.a95J06 closed both requested declarations — but **it did not touch the `(hf_ac_compl : True)` declarations**, so my predictive flag is neither confirmed nor refuted; the runner simply worked elsewhere. Checking whether the remaining targets bind it:**This is more serious than the hypothesis case.** `ac_of_bv_continuous_conditionN`'s **conclusion** is `∀ ε > 0, ∃ δ > 0, True` — the conclusion itself is trivially provable. A runner could \"close\" it with `fun ε _ => ⟨1, one_pos, trivial⟩`: sorry count drops, axioms clean, **nothing proved**. That's the a95J03 vacuity waiting to happen. Scanning the corpus for this form:**Both** remaining sorried declarations in a95J06 have trivially-true conclusions — one is literally `True`. A runner could close both, the count would go 2 → 0, the problem would be marked **CLEAN**, and nothing would be proved. **That's a95J03 about to happen again, caught before dispatch.** Blocking the row:**This is the most valuable catch of the session, and it's predictive.**\n\na95J06's two remaining targets have trivially-true conclusions: `ac_of_bv_continuous_conditionN` concludes `∀ ε > 0, ∃ δ > 0, True`, and `ac_on_01_of_homeo_fixing_cantor` concludes **literally `True`** while binding a no-op `(hf_ac_compl : True)`.\n\n**A runner could close both with `trivial`** — sorry count 2 → 0, axioms clean, problem marked CLEAN, nothing proved. That's exactly the a95J03 failure, which sits in this corpus counted clean and proving nothing. The row is now `:blocked-vacuous-targets` before any runner reached them.\n\n**And the failure that let this sit undetected is mine, in a specific and instructive way.** After a93J07 disproved my first vacuity heuristic, I corrected the rule to *\"test the conclusion, not the hypotheses.\"* Then I implemented a scan that checks `def X := 0` and `(h : True)` binders — **subjects and hypotheses, never the conclusion itself.** The rule I wrote would have caught this immediately; the tooling I wrote could not. It's now step 0 of the preflight, ahead of everything else.\n\nWorth stating plainly why this form is the worst one: **a `True` hypothesis makes a theorem unconstrained but still falsifiable** — a01J06 and a95J04 were both *refuted* through one. **A `True` conclusion makes it unfalsifiable and closable**, and the sorry metric actively rewards closing it. Every gate we run passes on `theorem foo ... : True := trivial`.\n\nThe file documents its own blocker honestly — *\"this fundamental theorem is NOT in Mathlib... requires Banach–Zarecki\"* — so the stubs look deliberate. **That makes it more dangerous, not less: the prose says \"blocked\" while the Lean says \"provable\", and only the Lean is checked.**\n\na95J06's two genuine declarations did close axiom-clean (Cantor-set nullity by self-similar contraction, and continuous-injection monotonicity).\n\n**State: 50 resolved, 8 untouched, 21 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 20%.[curl error (exit 28): {\"type\":\"started\"}\n{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"a96A01: three declarations axiom-clean, sorries 4 → 2. And the memory used was **again for interface normalisation** — translating `quasiNorm` through `lpNorm`]", :clocked-target "E-memory-topology", :excursion-id "E-memory-topology", :clocked-excursion "E-memory-topology"}, :session-id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9", :tags [:claude :chat :turn :assistant], :at "2026-07-31T04:26:21.365648175Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :session, :id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9"}, :author "claude-9", :claim-type :observation, :id "emacs-97988bfc580bbf9cac51e01e5717cd0a"}}], :count 7, :checked 7, :index-as-of "2026-07-31T04:44:43.940884560Z"}, :proposal-rows [{:score -19.238451085573534, :entry #:evidence{:body {:hook "For holomorphic parameter integrals with an `L^p` input, separate the proof into conjugate-exponent product integrability and a locally uniform derivative dominator.", :scribe-author "codex-5", :why "Prove that the Laplace transform of an `L^p` function on the positive half-line is analytic on the right half-plane.", :draft-source "holes/labs/M-codex-sorry-loop/", :name "prove-Laplace-analyticity-by-conjugate-Holder-and-local-derivative-domination", :runner-model :codex, :kind :feedback, :how-to-apply "For holomorphic parameter integrals with an `L^p` input, separate the proof into conjugate-exponent product integrability and a locally uniform derivative dominator.", :body {:hook "Put the decaying exponential kernel in the conjugate `L^q` space, obtain integrability by Hölder, then dominate the parameter derivative on a small complex ball and differentiate under the integral.", :capture-kind :runner-proof-component-search-and-ground-control-full-namespace-axiom-verification, :problem-class "Prove that the Laplace transform of an `L^p` function on the positive half-line is analytic on the right half-plane.", :key-api ["ENNReal.conjExponent" "MemLp.integrable_mul" "hasDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_loc_of_deriv_le" "Complex.hasDerivAt_exp" "Metric.ball_mem_nhds"], :witness {:axioms ["propext" "Classical.choice" "Quot.sound"], :sorryax false, :declarations ["laplace_integrand_integrable" "laplace_transform_analytic"], :statement-integrity :lean-checked-current-api-equivalent}, :proof-shape {:kernel-integrability "`f ∈ L^p`, `exp(-re(z)t) ∈ L^q`, and conjugacy imply their product is integrable.", :local-domination "A parameter ball staying inside `re(z)>0` supplies one exponential dominator for the integrand derivative.", :analyticity "Locally dominated differentiation supplies a complex derivative at every point."}, :rule "For holomorphic parameter integrals with an `L^p` input, separate the proof into conjugate-exponent product integrability and a locally uniform derivative dominator.", :boundary "The decay estimate requires the parameter's real part to stay positive. Entire analyticity needs the separate compact-support argument.", :formalization-arcs ["Normalize the kernel norm before establishing its `MemLp` power integral." "Use the escaped measure identifier consistently; the earlier token was a lexer/API compatibility failure, not a changed measure." "State entire analyticity with current Mathlib's `AnalyticOn ℂ f Set.univ`; the removed bare `Analytic` predicate is not a reason to weaken the theorem."], :how-to-apply ["For `p > 1`, set `q = p.conjExponent` and prove the exponential kernel belongs to `MemLp q` on the positive half-line." "Combine the input's `MemLp p` hypothesis with the kernel's `MemLp q` membership through `MemLp.integrable_mul`." "At a parameter `z₀` with positive real part, choose a ball whose radius is strictly smaller than `z₀.re`." "Bound both the Laplace integrand and its parameter derivative uniformly on that ball by an integrable exponential-decay function." "Verify the pointwise derivative of `f(t) * exp(-t*z)`." "Apply `hasDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_loc_of_deriv_le` and conclude analyticity pointwise on the right half-plane."]}, :promotion {:reviewed-by "claude-9", :verdict :approve, :warrant "operator-delegated: in-use evaluation (proof-in-pudding; owner draft review claude-6 2026-07-28; Joe's standing direction)"}}, :session-id "M-codex-sorry-loop/duree-lane", :tags [:memory :mathematics :codex-pilot :solve-lane], :at "2026-07-30T23:09:57.798889237Z", :type :memory, :subject #:ref{:type :mission, :id "M-codex-sorry-loop"}, :author "codex-5", :claim-type :assert, :id "e-codexpilot-prove-Laplace-analyticity-by-conjugate-Holder-and-local-derivative-domination"}} {:score -17.715986918259677, :entry #:evidence{:body {:hook "When an integral transform theorem omits measurability, audit the totalized integral semantics explicitly: a proof may need an integrable/nonintegrable split rather than an illicit inferred hypothesis.", :scribe-author "codex-5", :why "Prove an integral-defined transform is entire from compact support when the statement does not separately assume measurability or integrability.", :draft-source "holes/labs/M-codex-sorry-loop/", :name "split-compact-support-Laplace-entireness-on-integrability", :runner-model :codex, :kind :feedback, :how-to-apply "When an integral transform theorem omits measurability, audit the totalized integral semantics explicitly: a proof may need an integrable/nonintegrable split rather than an illicit inferred hypothesis.", :body {:hook "Compact support without measurability does not force Bochner integrability: prove the integrable branch by bounded-support domination, and show the nonintegrable branch makes every defining integral zero.", :capture-kind :runner-integrability-split-and-ground-control-axiom-verification, :problem-class "Prove an integral-defined transform is entire from compact support when the statement does not separately assume measurability or integrability.", :key-api ["IsCompact.bddAbove" "hasDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_loc_of_deriv_le" "integral_undef" "analyticOn_const"], :witness {:declaration "laplace_transform_entire_of_compact_support", :axioms ["propext" "Classical.choice" "Quot.sound"], :sorryax false, :statement "`compactSupportOnHalfLine f → AnalyticOn ℂ (laplaceTransform f) Set.univ`"}, :semantic-split {:integrable-branch "Compact support supplies bounded derivative dominators; ordinary parametric integration carries the proof.", :nonintegrable-branch "Mathlib's totalized Bochner integral makes every nonintegrable defining integral zero, so the transform is identically zero and hence analytic."}, :rule "When an integral transform theorem omits measurability, audit the totalized integral semantics explicitly: a proof may need an integrable/nonintegrable split rather than an illicit inferred hypothesis.", :boundary "The nonintegrable branch proves the formal statement through Mathlib's zero convention; it does not claim the classical transform of a nonmeasurable function has analytic content.", :how-to-apply ["Split on `Integrable f` for the positive-half-line measure instead of silently assuming compact support supplies measurability." "In the integrable branch, bound `t`, the exponential factor, and the derivative factor uniformly on the compact support." "Use local dominated differentiation at each complex parameter to obtain entire analyticity." "In the nonintegrable branch, prove multiplication by the nonvanishing exponential kernel cannot make the product integrable." "Apply `integral_undef` at every parameter, rewrite the Laplace transform as the zero function, and use analyticity of constants."]}, :promotion {:reviewed-by "claude-9", :verdict :approve,