:session-uuid "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :turn-id "019f8c89-03ed-7c80-aa8a-0f71e0c3b3f5", :event-seq-range [406 432], :reasoning-encrypted-only-count 5, :tool-digest-count 6, :rollout-file "/home/joe/.codex/sessions/2026/07/22/rollout-2026-07-22T20-53-09-019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822.jsonl", :turn 3, :event :turn-round, :truncated? false, :message-count 6, :harvester-version 1, :reasoning-count 5, :profile :codex-rollout, :text "Codex rollout turn 3\n\nREASONING (plaintext available in rollout)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nThat concern is well-founded: P0 proves “can be stored,” but storage alone does not create a retrieval path. I’ll inspect the mission’s current retrieval stages and the existing pattern-selection machinery, then suggest the smallest concrete bridge from memories to patterns.\n\nThat concern is well-founded: P0 proves “can be stored,” but storage alone does not create a retrieval path. I’ll inspect the mission’s current retrieval stages and the existing pattern-selection machinery, then suggest the smallest concrete bridge from memories to patterns.\n\nThe code confirms the gap: today `psr_search` searches only the notions index, `pattern_memory` returns PSR/PUR/PAR records, and the boot packet contains no deliberate memories. So the four saved memories are retrievable only if an agent explicitly remembers to call `memory_search`; nothing currently makes them surface at the point of use.\n\nThe code confirms the gap: today `psr_search` searches only the notions index, `pattern_memory` returns PSR/PUR/PAR records, and the boot packet contains no deliberate memories. So the four saved memories are retrievable only if an agent explicitly remembers to call `memory_search`; nothing currently makes them surface at the point of use.\n\nYes — your confidence should currently be low.\n\nI checked the live store: there are nine `:memory/assert` hyperedges, including the four canonical Zai memories and replay duplicates, but none has a pattern endpoint. More importantly:\n\n- `psr_search` searches only the existing notions/pattern index.\n- `pattern_memory` retrieves PSR/PUR/PAR records, not deliberate memories.\n- The boot packet does not contain a memory projection.\n- Therefore a saved memory appears only if the agent independently decides to call `memory_search` with a good query.\n\nThat is storage, not operational memory.\n\nI think your “via patterns” idea gives us the right P1 architecture:\n\n```text\ncurrent task\n    ↓\npsr_search\n    ↓\ncandidate pattern\n    ↓\nmemories attached to that pattern\n    ↓\npsr_select returns pattern + relevant remembered cases\n    ↓\nagent applies one and cites its evidence id\n```\n\nThe key move is pattern-conditioned recall. A memory remains a concrete episode or fact; a pattern becomes its reusable retrieval handle.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nPattern: mathlib/normalize-field-expressions\n\nMemories:\n- e-6bcbb51e… field_simp did not clear this inverse form\n- e-d9b1739f… HasDerivAt.comp hit an npowRec definitional mismatch\n- e-3c8277ed… direct expansion worked after the composed route failed\n```\n\nWhen a later task causes that pattern to surface, its attached memories should arrive automatically. The solver should not need to remember that `memory_search` exists.\n\nI would revise P1 into a smaller, sharper rung:\n\n1. Give memories explicit pattern endpoints. `memory_record` already accepts `{:ref/type :pattern ...}` subjects, although a librarian may assign these after writing.\n\n2. Backfill the current memories. The existing nine edges need pattern assignments and duplicate curation; presently they connect only to things such as `Mathlib`, `apm/a94A06`, and the session.\n\n3. Enrich `psr_search`. Each pattern candidate should include a bounded list of attached memory hooks and evidence IDs.\n\n4. Make `psr_select` the reliable retrieval trigger. Selection should return the full bodies of perhaps the top three current memories attached to the selected pattern.\n\n5. Record use. PSR/PUR should carry `:memory-ids` so we can measure whether retrieval led to application rather than merely appearing in context.\n\nThere are then two complementary processes:\n\n- Memory → pattern: the librarian classifies concrete memories under reusable patterns.\n- Pattern → memory: ordinary pattern selection automatically retrieves relevant experience.\n\nFacets and FTS can remain useful fallbacks, but neither is as dependable as attaching recall to a workflow the agent is already instructed to perform. Boot projection alone would mostly surface recent memories; pattern-conditioned recall surfaces memories at the decision point where they matter.\n\nI would make the P1 acceptance test concrete: a fresh Lean problem surfaces a pattern through `psr_search`; selecting it returns one of the a94A06 memory IDs; the agent applies it and cites that ID. Until that works end-to-end, I would not claim that the system has memory in the practical sense.\n\nYes — your confidence should currently be low.\n\nI checked the live store: there are nine `:memory/assert` hyperedges, including the four canonical Zai memories and replay duplicates, but none has a pattern endpoint. More importantly:\n\n- `psr_search` searches only the existing notions/pattern index.\n- `pattern_memory` retrieves PSR/PUR/PAR records, not deliberate memories.\n- The boot packet does not contain a memory projection.\n- Therefore a saved memory appears only if the agent independently decides to call `memory_search` with a good query.\n\nThat is storage, not operational memory.\n\nI think your “via patterns” idea gives us the right P1 architecture:\n\n```text\ncurrent task\n    ↓\npsr_search\n    ↓\ncandidate pattern\n    ↓\nmemories attached to that pattern\n    ↓\npsr_select returns pattern + relevant remembered cases\n    ↓\nagent applies one and cites its evidence id\n```\n\nThe key move is pattern-conditioned recall. A memory remains a concrete episode or fact; a pattern becomes its reusable retrieval handle.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nPattern: mathlib/normalize-field-expressions\n\nMemories:\n- e-6bcbb51e… field_simp did not clear this inverse form\n- e-d9b1739f… HasDerivAt.comp hit an npowRec definitional mismatch\n- e-3c8277ed… direct expansion worked after the composed route failed\n```\n\nWhen a later task causes that pattern to surface, its attached memories should arrive automatically. The solver should not need to remember that `memory_search` exists.\n\nI would revise P1 into a smaller, sharper rung:\n\n1. Give memories explicit pattern endpoints. `memory_record` already accepts `{:ref/type :pattern ...}` subjects, although a librarian may assign these after writing.\n\n2. Backfill the current memories. The existing nine edges need pattern assignments and duplicate curation; presently they connect only to things such as `Mathlib`, `apm/a94A06`, and the session.\n\n3. Enrich `psr_search`. Each pattern candidate should include a bounded list of attached memory hooks and evidence IDs.\n\n4. Make `psr_select` the reliable retrieval trigger. Selection should return the full bodies of perhaps the top three current memories attached to the selected pattern.\n\n5. Record use. PSR/PUR should carry `:memory-ids` so we can measure whether retrieval led to application rather than merely appearing in context.\n\nThere are then two complementary processes:\n\n- Memory → pattern: the librarian classifies concrete memories under reusable patterns.\n- Pattern → memory: ordinary pattern selection automatically retrieves relevant experience.\n\nFacets and FTS can remain useful fallbacks, but neither is as dependable as attaching recall to a workflow the agent is already instructed to perform. Boot projection alone would mostly surface recent memories; pattern-conditioned recall surfaces memories at the decision point where they matter.\n\nI would make the P1 acceptance test concrete: a fresh Lean problem surfaces a pattern through `psr_search`; selecting it returns one of the a94A06 memory IDs; the agent applies it and cites that ID. Until that works end-to-end, I would not claim that the system has memory in the practical sense.\n\nTOOL DIGEST\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_J5yfVijYHxefmVeWhFVQ4a4r\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"git status --short --branch && rg -n '^### Stage|^### P[0-9]|recall|retriev|pattern|facet|star|boot projection|curation' holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md | t…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_J5yfVijYHxefmVeWhFVQ4a4r\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.2 seconds Output: ## master...origin/master [ahead 14] M deps.edn M emacs/agent-follow-mode.el M scripts/apm_formal_zai_cron.py M scripts/dev-laptop-env M scripts/test_apm…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_FsBiwrRD0HtrTrW6DnkRcmQD\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"sed -n '510,690p' holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md\\\\nsed -n '700,855p' holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md\\\\nsed -n '340,405p' src/futon3c/peripheral/memory_bac…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_FsBiwrRD0HtrTrW6DnkRcmQD\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.1 seconds Output: controller already prices a `:retrieve` arm in its G-terms: standing queries are the natural feeder for what that arm retrieves. Guard (per the gamificat…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_dQlQIGO08lraQgDMHsyASxV2\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"curl -sS --max-time 30 -H 'Accept: application/edn' 'http://127.0.0.1:7073/api/alpha/hyperedges?type=memory/assert&limit=50' > /tmp/futon3c-memory-assert.edn &…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_dQlQIGO08lraQgDMHsyASxV2\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 5.1 seconds Output: {:count 9, :pattern-endpoints []} #:hx{:id \\\"hx-mem-23a568fd-8ce5-4c79-8180-5697212724bf\\\", :endpoints [\\\"e-23a568fd-8ce5-4c79-8180-5697212724bf\\\" \\\"Mathlib\\\" …\"}"}, :session-id "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :tags [:codex :turn-round :codex-rollout :codex-4], :at "2026-07-23T01:13:41.930Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :agent, :id "codex-4"}, :author "codex-4", :claim-type :step, :id "e-codexroll-019f8b63-t003"} #:evidence{:body {:session-uuid "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :turn-id "019f8c8f-a91c-7413-b807-dbed60445f22", :event-seq-range [433 447], :reasoning-encrypted-only-count 2, :tool-digest-count 4, :rollout-file "/home/joe/.codex/sessions/2026/07/22/rollout-2026-07-22T20-53-09-019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822.jsonl", :turn 4, :event :turn-round, :truncated? false, :message-count 2, :harvester-version 1, :reasoning-count 2, :profile :codex-rollout, :text "Codex rollout turn 4\n\nREASONING (plaintext available in rollout)\n\n\n\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nI agree: the pattern-conditioned retrieval loop should be expressed as an AIF mechanism, not added as an unrelated recommender. I’ll read the R-number facet definitions in the paper and map memory formation, pattern attachment, retrieval, and use onto those exact terms.\n\nI agree: the pattern-conditioned retrieval loop should be expressed as an AIF mechanism, not added as an unrelated recommender. I’ll read the R-number facet definitions in the paper and map memory formation, pattern attachment, retrieval, and use onto those exact terms.\n\nTOOL DIGEST\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_W0Peqz9oNziIrNy9hFwxid1z\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"rg -n 'R[0-9]|facet|AIF|active inference|policy|preference|observation|memory|pattern|epistemic|generative' main-2026.tex | head -320\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/cod…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_W0Peqz9oNziIrNy9hFwxid1z\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.2 seconds Output: Warning: truncated output (original token count: 22180) Total output lines: 145 216: pdftitle={Patterns for a New Generation: AIF and Embeddings}, 226:\\\\t…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_ox5D0W07zMtV3WPD9Ji6yT0L\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"rg -n '\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\subsection\\\\\\\\{|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\subsubsection\\\\\\\\{|R1\\\\\\\\b|R2\\\\\\\\b|R3\\\\\\\\b|R4\\\\\\\\b|R5\\\\\\\\b|R6\\\\\\\\b|R7\\\\\\\\b|R8\\\\\\\\b|R9\\\\\\\\b|R10\\\\\\\\b|R11\\\\\\\\b|R12\\\\\\\\b|R13\\\\\\\\b|R14\\\\\\\\b|R15\\\\\\\\b|R16\\\\\\\\b|R17\\\\\\\\b' ma…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_ox5D0W07zMtV3WPD9Ji6yT0L\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.1 seconds Output: Warning: truncated output (original token count: 25494) Total output lines: 227 445:\\\\paragraph{Belief state $\\\\mu$.} The Greek letter $\\\\mu$ names the syst…\"}"}, :session-id "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :tags [:codex :turn-round :codex-rollout :codex-4], :at "2026-07-23T01:20:54.610Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :agent, :id "codex-4"}, :author "codex-4", :claim-type :step, :id "e-codexroll-019f8b63-t004"} #:evidence{:body {:session-uuid "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :turn-id "019f8c8f-a91c-7413-b807-dbed60445f22", :event-seq-range [448 503], :reasoning-encrypted-only-count 10, :tool-digest-count 21, :rollout-file "/home/joe/.codex/sessions/2026/07/22/rollout-2026-07-22T20-53-09-019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822.jsonl", :turn 5, :event :turn-round, :truncated? false, :message-count 8, :harvester-version 1, :reasoning-count 10, :profile :codex-rollout, :text "Codex rollout turn 5\n\nREASONING (plaintext available in rollout)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nThe strongest fit is R6, with R17 governing how episodes eventually become or reshape patterns. I’m checking the handoff’s present structure now; I’ll turn this into a concrete “thin build now, AIF semantics preserved” amendment rather than leaving it as a conceptual aside.\n\nThe strongest fit is R6, with R17 governing how episodes eventually become or reshape patterns. I’m checking the handoff’s present structure now; I’ll turn this into a concrete “thin build now, AIF semantics preserved” amendment rather than leaving it as a conceptual aside.\n\nThe paper currently overclaims the seam: it equates “queryable” with “remembered,” and says R16 follows almost automatically from immutability. The live store disproves the first claim, while R16 requires an independently witnessed outcome. I’m tightening that language and making pattern-conditioned recall the R6 bridge.\n\nThe paper currently overclaims the seam: it equates “queryable” with “remembered,” and says R16 follows almost automatically from immutability. The live store disproves the first claim, while R16 requires an independently witnessed outcome. I’m tightening that language and making pattern-conditioned recall the R6 bridge.\n\nThe documents now separate three claims that had been collapsed: recorded, retrieved, and independently shown useful. I’ve also preserved zai-3’s direct-build route: P1 can start as a thin `psr_search`/`psr_select` enrichment without waiting for a full EFE controller or Librarian. I’m doing a final consistency check on the wording and LaTeX.\n\nThe documents now separate three claims that had been collapsed: recorded, retrieved, and independently shown useful. I’ve also preserved zai-3’s direct-build route: P1 can start as a thin `psr_search`/`psr_select` enrichment without waiting for a full EFE controller or Librarian. I’m doing a final consistency check on the wording and LaTeX.\n\nI’ve adapted the memory work around the AIF story.\n\nThe central distinction is now:\n\n- A memory is a typed R2 observation.\n- A pattern is an R6 policy hypothesis and retrieval handle.\n- Memory–pattern attachment is a revisable claim.\n- Applying a memory does not validate it; only an independently witnessed result provides R16 grounding.\n\nI updated:\n\n- [CODEX-HANDOFF-p0-memory-record.md](/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/CODEX-HANDOFF-p0-memory-record.md)\n- [M-typed-memories.md](/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md)\n- [main-2026.tex](/home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex)\n\nThe proposed direct P1 build is now:\n\n`task → R6 pattern candidates → attached supporting/challenging memories → selection citing memory IDs → action → external outcome → R3/R7 update`\n\nThis addresses the concrete live problem: the stored memories currently have no pattern endpoints, so lexical searchability does not make them operationally retrievable.\n\nI also corrected the paper’s stale claims that writes were still only designed and that R16 followed nearly automatically from immutable storage. The paper now distinguishes “recorded,” “retrieved,” and “worked.”\n\n`main-2026.tex` builds successfully with `latexmk`. No commits were made; the two futon3c documents were already untracked.\n\nI’ve adapted the memory work around the AIF story.\n\nThe central distinction is now:\n\n- A memory is a typed R2 observation.\n- A pattern is an R6 policy hypothesis and retrieval handle.\n- Memory–pattern attachment is a revisable claim.\n- Applying a memory does not validate it; only an independently witnessed result provides R16 grounding.\n\nI updated:\n\n- [CODEX-HANDOFF-p0-memory-record.md](/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/CODEX-HANDOFF-p0-memory-record.md)\n- [M-typed-memories.md](/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md)\n- [main-2026.tex](/home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex)\n\nThe proposed direct P1 build is now:\n\n`task → R6 pattern candidates → attached supporting/challenging memories → selection citing memory IDs → action → external outcome → R3/R7 update`\n\nThis addresses the concrete live problem: the stored memories currently have no pattern endpoints, so lexical searchability does not make them operationally retrievable.\n\nI also corrected the paper’s stale claims that writes were still only designed and that R16 followed nearly automatically from immutable storage. The paper now distinguishes “recorded,” “retrieved,” and “worked.”\n\n`main-2026.tex` builds successfully with `latexmk`. No commits were made; the two futon3c documents were already untracked.\n\nTOOL DIGEST\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_pzIUSRg5l0I9ZByU1Sj3DO6S\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"rg -n -A16 -B3 '\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label\\\\\\\\{def:R1[7-9]\\\\\\\\}|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label\\\\\\\\{def:R20\\\\\\\\}|R17|Structure learning|structure learning' main-2026.tex\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yi…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_pzIUSRg5l0I9ZByU1Sj3DO6S\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.1 seconds Output: Warning: truncated output (original token count: 8725) Total output lines: 142 472- 473-\\\\paragraph{Embedding space.} An embedding turns text into a point…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_CvNCazSSmXEyY8nXwRXEF7jl\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"sed -n '526,550p' main-2026.tex\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":10000,\\\"max_output_tokens\\\":6000}); text(r.output) \"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_CvNCazSSmXEyY8nXwRXEF7jl\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.1 seconds Output: \\\\subsubsection*{Learning, planning, and closing the loop (R12--R17)} \\\\paragraph{Two-Layer Calibration (R12).} \\\\textbf{If} predicted-versus-realised pairs…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_pxhALIwMDShYNvnyz0KJE43K\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"sed -n '1,260p' holes/CODEX-HANDOFF-p0-memory-record.md && git status --short -- holes/CODEX-HANDOFF-p0-memory-record.md holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md\\\",\\\"w…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_pxhALIwMDShYNvnyz0KJE43K\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.1 seconds Output: # CODEX-HANDOFF — P0 `memory_record`: a Zai runner can write a memory Mission: `holes/missi