cord.md M /home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md M /home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex \", :stderr \"\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_HHROL2xWZ33alVoFxNucHd9w\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.0 seconds Output: {}\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_dKZakShtj16fZ43KKEIlyVne\", :args \"const results = await Promise.all([ tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"git diff --check && git diff -- main-2026.tex\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":10000,\\\"max_output_tokens\\\":12000}), tools.exe…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_dKZakShtj16fZ43KKEIlyVne\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.2 seconds Output: ---RESULT 1--- diff --git a/main-2026.tex b/main-2026.tex index 511dff7..888726e 100644 --- a/main-2026.tex +++ b/main-2026.tex @@ -645,7 +645,7 @@ A dir…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_W9FYluxCOjPmGL4rwhjsGwOL\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"latexmk -pdf -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error main-2026.tex\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":30000,\\\"max_output_tokens\\\":5000}); text(JSON…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_W9FYluxCOjPmGL4rwhjsGwOL\", :output \"Script running with cell ID 10 Wall time 11.0 seconds Output: \"}\n{:kind :function_call, :name \"wait\", :call-id \"call_QweHdqkdkuLYKcBKPaSEP3nb\", :args \"{\\\"cell_id\\\":\\\"10\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":30000,\\\"max_tokens\\\":5000}\"}\n{:kind :function_call_output, :call-id \"call_QweHdqkdkuLYKcBKPaSEP3nb\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 1.8 seconds Output: Warning: truncated output (original token count: 5175) Total output lines: 1 {\\\"chunk_id\\\":\\\"fa9f58\\\",\\\"wall_time_seconds\\\":14.999852506,\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"origina…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_eB4Qif2ZD1Eh00w9NhceMgmT\", :args \"const results = await Promise.all([ tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"git status --short\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":10000,\\\"max_output_tokens\\\":3000}), tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"git status -…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_eB4Qif2ZD1Eh00w9NhceMgmT\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.2 seconds Output: ---RESULT 1--- M main-2026.tex ?? .latexmkrc ?? Emacs926.svg ?? IDEA.txt ?? NOTE-box1-residuals.md ?? README-empirics.md ?? aif-embeddings-paper-outline.…\"}"}, :session-id "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :tags [:codex :turn-round :codex-rollout :codex-4], :at "2026-07-23T01:21:44.954Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :agent, :id "codex-4"}, :author "codex-4", :claim-type :step, :id "e-codexroll-019f8b63-t005"} #:evidence{:body {:session-uuid "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :turn-id "019f8c93-3529-7ea0-a279-ebd117dbd305", :event-seq-range [504 531], :reasoning-encrypted-only-count 5, :tool-digest-count 7, :rollout-file "/home/joe/.codex/sessions/2026/07/22/rollout-2026-07-22T20-53-09-019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822.jsonl", :turn 6, :event :turn-round, :truncated? false, :message-count 6, :harvester-version 1, :reasoning-count 5, :profile :codex-rollout, :text "Codex rollout turn 6\n\nREASONING (plaintext available in rollout)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nYou’re right. I covered retrieval among existing patterns and later split/merge, but omitted the generative route from an unmatched episode to a genuinely new candidate pattern. That needs its own mint lane; R17/BMR alone cannot supply it because reduction can merge or prune structure but cannot invent a new state. I’ll add that missing lane explicitly.\n\nYou’re right. I covered retrieval among existing patterns and later split/merge, but omitted the generative route from an unmatched episode to a genuinely new candidate pattern. That needs its own mint lane; R17/BMR alone cannot supply it because reduction can merge or prune structure but cannot invent a new state. I’ll add that missing lane explicitly.\n\nThe missing mechanism is now explicit: failure to retrieve an adequate pattern is itself a typed result, not a dead end. It produces a policy-hole and an evidence-backed candidate pattern, which enters R6 without earned authority and must survive external trials before promotion.\n\nThe missing mechanism is now explicit: failure to retrieve an adequate pattern is itself a typed result, not a dead end. It produces a policy-hole and an evidence-backed candidate pattern, which enters R6 without earned authority and must survive external trials before promotion.\n\nYou’re right—the earlier outline was catalogue-conservative. It could retrieve, attach, split, or merge patterns, but not originate one.\n\nThe missing mint lane is now:\n\n`no adequate R6 candidate → reason-bearing policy-hole → gather unmatched memories → propose candidate pattern → admit with an unearned prior → trial in a cascade → external outcome → revise/promote/merge/prune`\n\nThe important distinction is that creating a pattern is model expansion. R17’s Bayesian Model Reduction can subsequently test whether the expanded model should be retained, merged, or pruned, but BMR cannot invent the new pattern.\n\nA new candidate carries:\n\n- IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE structure\n- The policy-hole that prompted it\n- Supporting and challenging memory IDs\n- Nearest existing patterns\n- A predicted contextual distinction or outcome that can fail\n- Explicit `candidate` status and an unearned prior\n\nSemantic similarity or repeated memories may nominate it, but cannot promote it. Promotion requires use plus an independently witnessed outcome.\n\nI added this mint lane to all three documents, including a dedicated P1b acceptance test in [M-typed-memories.md](/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md). The paper still builds successfully.\n\nYou’re right—the earlier outline was catalogue-conservative. It could retrieve, attach, split, or merge patterns, but not originate one.\n\nThe missing mint lane is now:\n\n`no adequate R6 candidate → reason-bearing policy-hole → gather unmatched memories → propose candidate pattern → admit with an unearned prior → trial in a cascade → external outcome → revise/promote/merge/prune`\n\nThe important distinction is that creating a pattern is model expansion. R17’s Bayesian Model Reduction can subsequently test whether the expanded model should be retained, merged, or pruned, but BMR cannot invent the new pattern.\n\nA new candidate carries:\n\n- IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE structure\n- The policy-hole that prompted it\n- Supporting and challenging memory IDs\n- Nearest existing patterns\n- A predicted contextual distinction or outcome that can fail\n- Explicit `candidate` status and an unearned prior\n\nSemantic similarity or repeated memories may nominate it, but cannot promote it. Promotion requires use plus an independently witnessed outcome.\n\nI added this mint lane to all three documents, including a dedicated P1b acceptance test in [M-typed-memories.md](/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md). The paper still builds successfully.\n\nTOOL DIGEST\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_du8KU6f1sN8IXNCx8LB4sv0t\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"sed -n '720,750p' main-2026.tex\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":10000,\\\"max_output_tokens\\\":5000}); text(r.output) \"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_du8KU6f1sN8IXNCx8LB4sv0t\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.2 seconds Output: \\\\begin{table}[h] \\\\caption{The zaif routemap: what is landed, and the dependency-ordered remainder.} \\\\label{tab:zaif-routemap} \\\\small \\\\begin{tabular}{p{0.…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_thLynPSDcmn08Hhnh0F7LYDj\", :args \"const patch = \\\"*** Begin Patch\\\\n*** Update File: /home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/CODEX-HANDOFF-p0-memory-record.md\\\\n@@\\\\n-The intended next read path is pattern-conditioned:\\\\n+The intended next read path …\"}\n{:kind :patch-apply, :call-id \"exec-4777bae2-9e6c-4e27-be43-21a85cbdad48\", :status :success, :files [:/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/CODEX-HANDOFF-p0-memory-record.md :/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md :/home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex], :stdout \"Success. Updated the following files: M /home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/CODEX-HANDOFF-p0-memory-record.md M /home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md M /home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex \", :stderr \"\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_thLynPSDcmn08Hhnh0F7LYDj\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.0 seconds Output: {}\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_jHs8aO2Zp23gGL5NC0BuUIHI\", :args \"const results = await Promise.all([ tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"git diff --check && latexmk -pdf -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error main-2026.tex\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":300…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_jHs8aO2Zp23gGL5NC0BuUIHI\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 10.9 seconds Output: ---RESULT 1--- Warning: truncated output (original token count: 14916) Total output lines: 1194 Rc files read: .latexmkrc Latexmk: This is Latexmk, John…\"}"}, :session-id "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :tags [:codex :turn-round :codex-rollout :codex-4], :at "2026-07-23T01:24:46.554Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :agent, :id "codex-4"}, :author "codex-4", :claim-type :step, :id "e-codexroll-019f8b63-t006"} #:evidence{:body {:session-uuid "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :turn-id "019f8c96-6391-78c0-86cc-59974ba9b201", :event-seq-range [532 564], :reasoning-encrypted-only-count 7, :tool-digest-count 10, :rollout-file "/home/joe/.codex/sessions/2026/07/22/rollout-2026-07-22T20-53-09-019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822.jsonl", :turn 7, :event :turn-round, :truncated? false, :message-count 4, :harvester-version 1, :reasoning-count 7, :profile :codex-rollout, :text "Codex rollout turn 7\n\nREASONING (plaintext available in rollout)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nI think you’ve found a real gap, but it has two layers: the paper has a live pattern-mint route operationally, while its formal AIF account mostly describes selection and reduction over an already-given state/action vocabulary. I’m tracing those two stories against the exact R6, R13, R16, and R17 claims so we can say precisely whether cascades solve it—or merely rearrange existing pieces.\n\nI think you’ve found a real gap, but it has two layers: the paper has a live pattern-mint route operationally, while its formal AIF account mostly describes selection and reduction over an already-given state/action vocabulary. I’m tracing those two stories against the exact R6, R13, R16, and R17 claims so we can say precisely whether cascades solve it—or merely rearrange existing pieces.\n\nYes—there is a real gap in the model as a whole.\n\nThe key terminological trap is that an AIF “generative model” is not automatically a creative generator. It predicts how hidden states and actions produce observations: roughly, “if the world is in state \\(s\\) and I follow policy \\(\\pi\\), what observations should I expect?” It does not, by itself, invent new hidden-state categories, actions, or patterns.\n\nThe paper currently contains three different layers:\n\n1. Operational generative model, R1–R4  \n   Hidden operational states such as blockage, progress, and pattern fit predict tests, traces, diffs, and feedback.\n\n2. Policy construction, R6/R13  \n   Existing patterns are action primitives; cascades compose them into policies. Cascades provide combinatorial novelty, but only from known pieces.\n\n3. Model-structure learning, R17  \n   BMR merges or prunes concepts after evidence accumulates. This simplifies an existing model; it does not invent a missing concept.\n\nWhat’s absent from the formal catalogue is model expansion: how a previously unavailable action primitive or state category becomes representable.\n\nThere is, however, already a live route in the paper. The preregistered loop says that fold boxes which cannot be grounded become `policy-holes`, which route to a mint lane; new candidate patterns enter at a uniform prior and earn status through witnessed outcomes. It even reports one double-witnessed candidate in that lane. See [main-2026.tex](/home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex:703).\n\nSo the mechanism exists operationally, but the catalogue never gives it a first-class R-pattern. Instead, it says the underlying pattern collection is “not detailed here,” while R6 assumes a bounded set of existing pattern IDs. That is the formal hole.\n\nI think the repaired architecture is:\n\n```text\nFast loop:   fixed current model\nR1–R6 → cascade → fold → act → R16 outcome\n\n                          policy-hole / persistent prediction error\n                                           ↓\nSlow loop:                    candidate pattern generation\n                                           ↓\n                         uniform-prior candidate in R6\n                                           ↓\n                           witnessed trials and review\n                                           ↓\n                    retain / revise / merge / prune via R17\n                                           ↓\n                              updated current model\n```\n\nThe missing catalogue pattern could be something like “Pattern Genesis from Policy-Holes”:\n\n- If R6 repeatedly has no adequate action or a fold exposes an ungrounded step;\n- however cascades can only compose existing patterns and BMR can only reduce existing structure;\n- then bundle the policy-hole, circumstances, failed candidates, memories, and a falsifiable expected outcome into a candidate pattern, admitting it at an unearned prior;\n- because a learning system must sometimes expand the vocabulary in which it represents possible action.\n\nThat also clarifies the status transition:\n\n`memory → evidence for a policy-hole → candidate pattern → trialled pattern → established pattern`\n\nThe paper’s existing definition already says a candidate becomes a pattern only after repeated enactment and review ([main-2026.tex](/home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex:424)). What is missing is the formal account of how the candidate is born.\n\nI would make this a first-class node between R16 and R17—or perhaps the missing R18, after checking the historical use of that number. R17 would then be the disposer of proposed structure, not its sole generator.\n\nYes—there is a real gap in the model as a whole.\n\nThe key terminological trap is that an AIF “generative model” is not automatically a creative generator. It predicts how hidden states and actions produce observations: roughly, “if the world is in state \\(s\\) and I follow policy \\(\\pi\\), what observations should I expect?” It does not, by itself, invent new hidden-state categories, actions, or patterns.\n\nThe paper currently contains three different layers:\n\n1. Operational generative model, R1–R4  \n   Hidden operational states such as blockage, progress, and pattern fit predict tests, traces, diffs, and feedback.\n\n2. Policy construction, R6/R13  \n   Existing patterns are action primitives; cascades compose them into policies. Cascades provide combinatorial novelty, but only from known pieces.\n\n3. Model-structure learning, R17  \n   BMR merges or prunes concepts after evidence accumulates. This simplifies an existing model; it does not invent a missing concept.\n\nWhat’s absent from the formal catalogue is model expansion: how a previously unavailable action primitive or state category becomes representable.\n\nThere is, however, already a live route in the paper. The preregistered loop says that fold boxes which cannot be grounded become `policy-holes`, which route to a mint lane; new candidate patterns enter at a uniform prior and earn status through witnessed outcomes. It even reports one double-witnessed candidate in that lane. See [main-2026.tex](/home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex:703).\n\nSo the mechanism exists operationally, but the catalogue never gives it a first-class R-pattern. Instead, it says the underlying pattern collection is “not detailed here,” while R6 assumes a bounded set of existing pattern IDs. That is the formal hole.\n\nI think the repaired architecture is:\n\n```text\nFast loop:   fixed current model\nR1–R6 → cascade → fold → act → R16 outcome\n\n                          policy-hole / persistent prediction error\n                                           ↓\nSlow loop:                    candidate pattern generation\n                                           ↓\n                         uniform-prior candidate in R6\n                                           ↓\n                           witnessed trials and review\n                                           ↓\n                    retain / revise / merge / prune via R17\n                                           ↓\n                              updated current model\n```\n\nThe missing catalogue pattern could be something like “Pattern Genesis from Policy-Holes”:\n\n- If R6 repeatedly has no adequate action or a fold exposes an ungrounded step;\n- however cascades can only compose existing patterns and BMR can only reduce existing structure;\n- then bundle the policy-hole, circumstances, failed candidates, memories, and a falsifiable expected outcome into a candidate pattern, admitting it at an unearned prior;\n- because a learning system must sometimes expand the vocabulary in which it represents possible action.\n\nThat also clarifies the status transition:\n\n`memory → evidence for a policy-hole → candidate pattern → trialled pattern → established pattern`\n\nThe paper’s existing definition already says a candidate becomes a pattern only after repeated enactment and review ([main-2026.tex](/home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex:424)). What is missing is the formal account of how the candidate is born.\n\nI would make this a first-class node between R16 and R17—or perhaps the missing R18, after checking the historical use of that number. R17 would then be the disposer of proposed structure, not its sole generator.\n\nTOOL DIGEST\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_RtMh1Tug7XRPOwRY25lF4gsa\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"rg -n -A12 -B5 'Candidate Pattern Action Space|policy-hole|mint lane|mint|new pattern|candidate pattern|form concepts|generative model|cascade.*R6|R6.*cascade|…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_RtMh1Tug7XRPOwRY25lF4gsa\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 1.3 seconds Output: Warning: truncated output (original token count: 16027) Total output lines: 214 Warning: truncated output (original token count: 17474) Total output line…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_M1iQs9MF0jWa43VDAhFAiawu\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"sed -n '493,528p' main-2026.tex\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":10000,\\\"max_output_tokens\\\":9000}); text(r.output) \"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_M1iQs9MF0jWa43VDAhFAiawu\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.1 seconds Output: \\\\paragraph{No self-certification.} No self-certification means the system cannot count evidence it manufactured as if it were independent proof. This is …\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_NeZPjeCm1FdU7LLp31Vigyfh\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"rg -n '\\\\\\\\(R18\\\\\\\\)|\\\\\\\\(R19\\\\\\\\)|R18|R19' main-2026.tex | head -40\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":10000,\\\"max_output_tokens\\\":5000}); text(r.output) \"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_NeZPjeCm1FdU7LLp31Vigyfh\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.2 seconds Output: 551:\\\\paragraph{Interoceptive Tripwires (R20).}\\\\label{pat:r20} \\\\textbf{If} you have hardened the loop's constructed core (R1--R16) and want the machinery …\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_bAeEIEGT1npLas9nrXl5BJwb\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"rg -n 'R18|R19|pattern mint|mint lane|policy-hole' . --glob '!main-2026.*' --glob '!*.pdf' --glob '!*.svg' | head -120\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yield_…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_bAeEIEGT1npLas9nrXl5BJwb\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.1 seconds Output: ./questions-for-codex-2.md:74:terminals, and policy-holes\\\" and is checked by \\\"Clojure/Babashka gates\\\" and a ./empirics.tex:18:The live scheduler adds two…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_ZdDE09T24m0AJpsR5f5huyya\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"sed -n '285,410p' sequel-notebook.org; sed -n '130,180p' NOTE-box1-residuals.md\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":10000,\\\"max_output_tokens\\\":1400…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_ZdDE09T24m0AJpsR5f5huyya\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.2 seconds Output: | 4 | ~30% mechanical ceiling; want-text lifts F | Q-B: mean ΔF +0.093, ~29.5% F>0 | DONE | | 5 | Precision is a learned posterior (γ) | E5 futon2 e86802…\"}"}, :session-id "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :tags [:codex :turn-round :codex-rollout :codex-4], :at "2026-07-23T01:28:16.763Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :agent, :id "codex-4"}, :author "codex-4", :claim-type :step, :id "e-codexroll-019f8b63-t007"} #:evidence{:body {:session-uuid "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :turn-id "019f8c9b-3742-7782-b8e3-7b9748c965a9", :event-seq-range [565 595], :reasoning-encrypted-only-count 6, :tool-digest-count 8, :rollout-file "/home/joe/.codex/sessions/2026/07/22/rollout-2026-07-22T20-53-09-019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822.jsonl", :turn 8, :event :turn-round, :truncated? false, :message-count 6, :harvester-version 1, :reasoning-count 6, :profile :codex-rollout, :text "Codex rollout turn 8\n\nREASONING (plaintext available in rollout)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nGood—then we should treat that explainer as the prior ontology and avoid casually reusing R18. I’ll trace what R18 meant there, how R17 and the seeds/mint route were wired around it, and where the paper diverged from that authoritative summary.\n\nGood—then we should treat that explainer as the prior ontology and avoid casually reusing R18. I’ll trace what R18 meant there, how R17 and the seeds/mint route were wired around it, and where the paper diverged from that authoritative summary.\n\nThe authoritative explainer sharpens this considerably: R17 was originally the broad “model expansion / niche” node—explicitly “new hidden causes, new policies, new kinds of action; the model grows.” R18 was the meta-audit over every other quantity. The paper later narrowed R17 to BMR, so the expansion half fell out of the formal account even though the operational mint/seeds route survived elsewhere. I’m locating that surviving route now.\n\nThe authoritative explainer sharpens this considerably: R17 was originally the broad “model expansion / niche” node—explicitly “new hidden causes, new policies, new kinds of action; the model grows.” R18 was the meta-audit over every other quantity. The paper later narrowed R17 to BMR, so the expansion half fell out of the formal account even though the operational mint/seeds route survived elsewhere. I’m locating that surviving route now.\n\nYes. Tracing from the authoritative explainer changes the diagnosis.\n\nR17 already owned pattern creation. R18 should remain faithfulness.\n\nIn the explainer:\n\n- R17 is explicitly “Structure learning — model expansion / niche.”\n- Its concept says: “new hidden causes, new policies, new kinds of action—the model grows.”\n- Its promise says the War Machine could “invent new action-classes when its current repertoire can’t cope.”\n- R18 is explicitly a meta-level “Faithfulness lens,” auditing whether quantities bearing AIF names actually compute those quantities.\n\nSee [aif-wiring-explainer.html](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/aif-wiring-explainer.html:142).\n\nSo the original conceptual allocation was already right. The problem arose because the implementation description under R17 concentrated on BMR, and the paper later narrowed R17 almost entirely to reduction.\n\nThe historical mechanism\n\nThe operational record reveals a fairly concrete pattern-creation loop:\n\n```text\ncircumstance\n    ↓\nretrieve existing patterns\n    ↓\nconstruct cascade\n    ↓\nthin cascade or fold policy-hole\n    ↓\nlibrary-gap finding\n    ↓\nauthor candidate seed\n    ↓\ningest into embeddings + phylogeny + prior\n    ↓\ncandidate becomes eligible for future cascades\n    ↓\nenactment and external outcome\n    ↓\nearn, revise, merge, or prune\n```\n\nThat is not speculative. The pipeline wiring contains live edges:\n\n```text\ncascade.library-gap-findings\n    → authoring.library-gap-findings\nauthoring.authored-seeds\n    → pattern-library.authored-seeds\n```\n\nSee [wm-pipeline-wiring.edn](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/wm-pipeline-wiring.edn:22).\n\nThe seed work also discovered that authoring a pattern was insufficient. A new pattern had to cross three gates before it could influence action:\n\n1. It had to enter the embedding index, so retrieval could find it.\n2. It had to enter the phylogeny, so cascade construction considered it eligible.\n3. It needed an honest prior, because zero historical use otherwise imposed a prohibitive complexity cost.\n\nThat is documented in [E-live-loop-1-s2-escrow-design.md](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/E-live-loop-1-s2-escrow-design.md:159).\n\nThe later slush experiment then names essentially the same mechanism the “mint lane”:\n\n`policy-hole → candidate-pattern/open → uniform prior → flown outcomes → library status`\n\nIt even records a worked precedent and one double-witnessed candidate. See [SPEC-full-loop-gfn.md](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/labs/slush-demo/SPEC-full-loop-gfn.md:82).\n\nWhat cascades do—and don’t do\n\nCascades provide compositional novelty. They can create a new policy from known patterns.\n\nThey cannot create a missing primitive.\n\nTheir role in pattern genesis is diagnostic: when a cascade is thin, disconnected, or produces a policy-hole, it provides evidence that the present action vocabulary is inadequate. The cascade is therefore both:\n\n- a policy assembled from the current model; and\n- a sensor for where that model needs expansion.\n\nThe authoring/mint lane performs the expansion.\n\nWhere the paper drifted\n\nThe paper currently has all the pieces, but they are separated:\n\n- The opening definition says candidates become patterns through repeated enactment and review.\n- R6 selects from a bounded set of existing pattern IDs.\n- R13 composes existing patterns into cascades.\n- The experiment describes policy-holes entering a mint lane.\n- R17 formalizes BMR reduction.\n- The catalogue introduction says the underlying pattern collection is “not detailed here.”\n\nThat last gap is exactly where the authoritative R17 story disappeared.\n\nThe clean repair is not a new R-number. It is to restore R17’s original breadth:\n\n| R17 phase | Function |\n|---|---|\n| Expansion | Policy-holes and unexplained memories generate candidate patterns |\n| Admission | Candidates enter the action space at an explicitly unearned prior |\n| Testing | Cascades enact them; R16 supplies independent outcomes |\n| Consolidation | Evidence updates standing; BMR merges or prunes redundant structure |\n\nBMR is therefore the disposer, not the whole of R17. Embeddings, memories, policy-holes, and authoring are candidate generators. R17 names the complete structure-learning cycle.\n\nAnd R18?\n\nIt is slightly strange because it is not part of the generative model. But the R-numbers already mix core inference and supporting apparatus: trace, scheduling, validation, hierarchy, and so forth. R18 is best described explicitly as a meta-pattern or audit lens over the loop, not another latent state or inference operator.\n\nThe paper’s faithfulness badges and retract table are effectively the current realization of R18, even though its dedicated catalogue entry has disappeared.\n\nSo my present conclusion is:\n\n- There is no missing number.\n- There is a missing half of R17 in the paper.\n- The live system has a partially operational expansion route.\n- Cascades expose demand for new patterns but do not themselves author them.\n- Memory-pattern work gives that original R17 route a much better evidence supply.\n\nYes. Tracing from the authoritative explainer changes the diagnosis.\n\nR17 already owned pattern creation. R18 should remain faithfulness.\n\nIn the explainer:\n\n- R17 is explicitly “Structure learning — model expansion / niche.”\n- Its concept says: “new hidden causes, new policies, new kinds of action—the model grows.”\n- Its promise says the War Machine could “invent new action-classes when its current repertoire can’t cope.”\n- R18 is explicitly a meta-level “Faithfulness lens,” auditing whether quantities bearing AIF names actually compute those quantities.\n\nSee [aif-wiring-explainer.html](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/aif-wiring-explainer.html:142).\n\nSo the original conceptual allocation was already right. The problem arose because the implementation description under R17 concentrated on BMR, and the paper later narrowed R17 almost entirely to reduction.\n\nThe historical mechanism\n\nThe operational record reveals a fairly concrete pattern-creation loop:\n\n```text\ncircumstance\n    ↓\nretrieve existing patterns\n    ↓\nconstruct cascade\n    ↓\nthin cascade or fold policy-hole\n    ↓\nlibrary-gap finding\n    ↓\nauthor candidate seed\n    ↓\ningest into embeddings + phylogeny + prior\n    ↓\ncandidate becomes eligible for future cascades\n    ↓\nenactment and external outcome\n    ↓\nearn, revise, merge, or prune\n```\n\nThat is not speculative. The pipeline wiring contains live edges:\n\n```text\ncascade.library-gap-findings\n    → authoring.library-gap-findings\nauthoring.authored-seeds\n    → pattern-library.authored-seeds\n```\n\nSee [wm-pipeline-wiring.edn](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/wm-pipeline-wiring.edn:22).\n\nThe seed work also discovered that authoring a pattern was insufficient. A new pattern had to cross three gates before it could influence action:\n\n1. It had to enter the embedding index, so retrieval could find it.\n2. It had to enter the phylogeny, so cascade construction considered it eligible.\n3. It needed an honest prior, because zero historical use otherwise imposed a prohibitive complexity cost.\n\nThat is documented in [E-live-loop-1-s2-escrow-design.md](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/E-live-loop-1-s2-escrow-design.md:159).\n\nThe later slush experiment then names essentially the same mechanism the “mint lane”:\n\n`policy-hole → candidate-pattern/open → uniform prior → flown outcomes → library status`\n\nIt even records a worked precedent and one double-witnessed candidate. See [SPEC-full-loop-gfn.md](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/labs/slush-demo/SPEC-full-loop-gfn.md:82).\n\nWhat cascades do—and don’t do\n\nCascades provide compositional novelty. They can create a new policy from known patterns.\n\nThey cannot create a missing primitive.\n\nTheir role in pattern genesis is diagnostic: when a cascade is thin, disconnected, or produces a policy-hole, it provides evidence that the present action vocabulary is inadequate. The cascade is therefore both:\n\n- a policy assembled from the current model; and\n- a sensor for where that model needs expansion.\n\nThe authoring/mint lane performs the expansion.\n\nWhere the paper drifted\n\nThe paper currently has all the pieces, but they are separated:\n\n- The opening definition says candidates become patterns through repeated enactment and review.\n- R6 selects from a bounded set of existing pattern IDs.\n- R13 composes existing patterns into cascades.\n- The experiment describes policy-holes entering a mint lane.\n- R17 formalizes BMR reduction.\n- The catalogue introduction says the underlying pattern collection is “not detailed here.”\n\nThat last gap is exactly where the authoritative R17 story disappeared.\n\nThe clean repair is not a new R-number. It is to restore R17’s original breadth:\n\n| R17 phase | Function |\n|---|---|\n| Expansion | Policy-holes and unexplained memories generate candidate patterns |\n| Admission | Candidates enter the action space at an explicitly unearned prior |\n| Testing | Cascades enact them; R16 supplies independent outcomes |\n| Consolidation | Evidence updates standing; BMR merges or prunes redundant structure |\n\nBMR is therefore the disposer, not the whole of R17. Embeddings, memories, policy-holes, and authoring are candidate generators. R17 names the complete structure-learning cycle.\n\nAnd R18?\n\nIt is slightly strange because it is not part of the generative model. But the R-numbers already mix core inference and supporting apparatus: trace, scheduling, validation, hierarchy, and so forth. R18 is best described explicitly as a meta-pattern or audit lens over the loop, not another latent state or inference operator.\n\nThe paper’s faithfulness badges and retract table are effectively the current realization of R18, even though its dedicated catalogue entry has disappeared.\n\nSo my present conclusion is:\n\n- There is no missing number.\n- There is a missing half of R17 in the paper.\n- The live system has a partially operational expansion route.\n- Cascades expose demand for new patterns but do not themselves author them.\n- Memory-pattern work gives that original R17 route a much better evidence supply.\n\nTOOL DIGEST\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_61eXoERPJmQ3fEJVpSa4FUzX\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"rg -n -A28 -B12 'R18|faithful|faithfulness|R17|seed|mint|policy-hole|new pattern|structure learning|generative model' holes/aif-wiring-explainer.html\\\",\\\"workdir…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_61eXoERPJmQ3fEJVpSa4FUzX\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 1.7 seconds Output: Warning: truncated output (original token count: 16253) Total output lines: 345 1-<!DOCTYPE html> 2-<html lang=\\\"en\\\"> 3-<head> 4-<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\"/> 5…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_FFjGGjN6gTg4fRzk7NgpcGHj\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"rg -n -A24 -B6 'id:\\\\\\\"R17\\\\\\\"|id:\\\\\\\"R18\\\\\\\"|r:\\\\\\\"R17\\\\\\\"|r:\\\\\\\"R18\\\\\\\"' holes/aif-wiring-explainer.html\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/futon2\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":10000,\\\"max_output…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_FFjGGjN6gTg4fRzk7NgpcGHj\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.2 seconds Output: 136- {id:\\\"TRACE\\\", r:\\\"R8\\\", t:\\\"Per-tick trace\\\", s:\\\"memory tape\\\", x:298,y:506, st:\\\"done\\\", ap:true, 137- concept:\\\"Memory of what happened: a per-tick record …\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_0zfI3rfxlFWp7uuCVcS3g1kd\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"rg -n -i 'seeds pipeline|seed.*pattern|pattern.*seed|mint lane|policy-hole.*pattern|virtual pattern|candidate pattern.*uniform|new patterns become' holes src s…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_0zfI3rfxlFWp7uuCVcS3g1kd\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.3 seconds Output: holes/N-backlog-as-cascade.md:20:| policy-hole = no pattern grounds this | **hole = no MISSION grounds this** — a mission that should exist and doesn't |…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_kOa2DyUAyZMloGmKPvcy5II1\", :args \"const results = await Promise.all([ tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"sed -n '24,112p' holes/labs/slush-demo/SPEC-full-loop-gfn.md\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/futon2\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":10000,\\\"max_output_tokens\\\":9…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_kOa2DyUAyZMloGmKPvcy5II1\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.3 seconds Output: ---RESULT 1--- 4 FOLD: cascade → construction (classical / LLM / embedding — any impl against the interface) 5 ESCROW: ft-*.edn deposit, pins, ΔG 6 ARM &…\"}"}, :session-id "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :tags [:codex :turn-round :codex-rollout :codex-4], :at "2026-07-23T01:33:31.349Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :agent, :id "codex-4"}, :author "codex-4", :claim-type :step, :id "e-codexroll-019f8b63-t008"} #:evidence{:body {:session-uuid "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :turn-id "019f8c9f-d65e-74a0-b492-5400fce43036", :event-seq-range [596 634], :reasoning-encrypted-only-count 8, :tool-digest-count 12, :rollout-file "/home/joe/.codex/sessions/2026/07/22/rollout-2026-07-22T20-53-09-019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822.jsonl", :turn 9, :event :turn-round, :truncated? true, :message-count 6, :harvester-version 1, :reasoning-count 8, :profile :codex-rollout, :original-text-bytes 16957, :text "Codex rollout turn 9\n\nREASONING (plaintext available in rollout)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nThat is a promising division: R17′ controls unresolved structural uncertainty, R17″ generates reduction hypotheses from embedding nearness, and R17‴ could cover constructive expansion from lived evidence. I’ll read zai-3’s new section and its notes file closely, then reconcile its implementation-first account with the older War Machine seed/mint route and the AIF distinction we just recovered.\n\nThat is a promising division: R17′ controls unresolved structural uncertainty, R17″ generates reduction hypotheses from embedding nearness, and R17‴ could cover constructive expansion from lived evidence. I’ll read zai-3’s new section and its notes file closely, then reconcile its implementation-first account with the older War Machine seed/mint route and the AIF distinction we just recovered.\n\nOne correction to keep the trace honest: after following the older wiring, I did find a new-pattern route—but it is only partially closed. The live pipeline has `library-gap-findings → authoring → authored-seeds → pattern-library`; the later slush spec generalizes that as `policy-hole → candidate-pattern/open → uniform prior`. What is missing is a single formal R17 account and a fully automatic evidence-to-candidate transition. I’m checking a few actual library specimens so R17‴ can be illustrated by real positive and negative cases rather than abstract machinery.\n\nOne correction to keep the trace honest: after following the older wiring, I did find a new-pattern route—but it is only partially closed. The live pipeline has `library-gap-findings → authoring → authored-seeds → pattern-library`; the later slush spec generalizes that as `policy-hole → candidate-pattern/open → uniform prior`. What is missing is a single formal R17 account and a fully automatic evidence-to-candidate transition. I’m checking a few actual library specimens so R17‴ can be illustrated by real positive and negative cases rather than abstract machinery.\n\nI agree: R17‴ is the natural place, and Zaif exposes exactly what it should contain.\n\nOne correction first: after tracing the older material, I did find a partially live authoring route:\n\n`cascade library-gap → authoring → authored seed → pattern library`\n\nThe later slush specification expresses it as:\n\n`policy-hole → candidate-pattern/open → uniform prior → witnessed use`\n\nSo the War Machine can ingest new patterns, but the route is sparse, partly manual, and never became a coherent catalogue pattern. That is what R17‴ should repair.\n\nThe three supplements would then have a clean division:\n\n- R17′ — keep unresolved structural hypotheses explorable. Posterior spread is an honest uncertainty bonus, not EIG.\n- R17″ — generate possible reductions from embedding nearness. “These existing concepts might be mergeable.”\n- R17‴ — generate expansions from evidence-bearing holes. “The existing vocabulary cannot adequately describe this recurring situation.”\n\nA draft pattern would be:\n\n> Pattern Genesis from Evidence-Bearing Holes (R17‴).  \n> If independently recorded episodes, failed retrievals, or policy-holes repeatedly expose a situation that no existing pattern adequately handles; however cascades can only compose existing patterns, embeddings supply similarity rather than justification, and BMR can only compare or reduce represented structure; then bundle the triggering circumstances, memories, rejected near-matches, counterexamples, and a falsifiable predicted outcome into a candidate pattern, admit it to R6 at an explicitly unearned prior, and require repeated witnessed enactment-and-review before promotion. Because structure learning requires expansion of the model’s action vocabulary as well as refinement and reduction of what it already contains.\n\nR18 would audit this claim. R17‴ would probably receive an amber or gray faithfulness classification: it is a principled structure-hypothesis generator, but not itself BMR or a canonical EFE quantity.\n\nWhat Zaif contributes\n\nThe War Machine route begins with relatively coarse policy-holes. Zaif can supply a much denser evidential substrate:\n\n```text\ntyped memories\n    ↓\nLibrarian comparison with existing patterns\n    ├── attach as an instance\n    ├── challenge or refine an existing pattern\n    ├── retain as a reference only\n    └── mint a candidate pattern\n                 ↓\n         pattern-conditioned recall\n                 ↓\n         cited use in a later task\n                 ↓\n         independently witnessed outcome\n                 ↓\n       promote / revise / merge / prune\n```\n\nThat four-way Librarian decision is crucial. Memory accumulation must not turn automatically into pattern proliferation.\n\nA revealing issue in zai-3’s notes\n\nZai-3 maps three a94A06 memories to `math-formalization/tactic-algebra-interference`. But the actual pattern is specifically about `rw`, `simp_rw`, and `convert` interfering with `Tendsto` or binder structure.\n\nThe `field_simp` memory is related only at a much broader level. There is an existing `field-simp-reciprocal` pattern, but that covers proving denominators nonzero—not the observed problem where the hypothesis and denominator are mathematically equal but syntactically differently normalized.\n\nTherefore the honest classification may be:\n\n- `tactic-algebra-interference`: a nearby pattern, but not a clean instantiation.\n- `field-simp-reciprocal`: another near-match, still incomplete.\n- The repeated lesson “normalize the denominator into the hypothesis’s syntactic form before `field_simp`” may justify a new candidate such as `math-formalization/normalize-before-field-simp`.\n\nThat is almost a perfect R17‴ worked example: memories reveal that nearest-neighbour attachment would conceal a meaningful structural distinction.\n\nOther paper examples\n\nA short examples box could include:\n\n- `structure/two-projections-of-one-quantity`: positive precedent. It was cited before it existed, the missing pattern was caught, then it was minted and later discharged its originating hole.\n- `process-coherence/status-refresh-before-work`: learned from the recurring failure of agents and selectors acting on stale mission banners.\n- `process-coherence/is-this-even-a-problem`: learned after 674 expensive ticks worked questions whose answers were already present in primary documents.\n- `normalize-before-field-simp`: prospective Zaif-derived candidate, grounded in typed proof episodes.\n- `polyrith unavailable`: negative example. This is a volatile environment fact, not a reusable pattern; retain it as reference memory.\n\nThese illustrate why new patterns are useful: they add a previously missing action to the repertoire. The two process-coherence patterns make “read and re-derive current reality first” eligible as the first box in future cascades. A field-simplification pattern would make a previously private Lean workaround retrievable across proofs.\n\nOne architectural adjustment to zai-3’s proposal follows from this. The system should not simply attach every memory to the best-scoring existing pattern. It needs an explicit `no-adequate-pattern` result. Otherwise retrieval similarity will absorb every novel episode into the nearest old category, and R17‴ will never fire.\n\nI would also model memory–pattern attachment as its own provenance-bearing, revisable claim rather than silently mutating the original `:memory/assert` edge. That lets the Librarian say who proposed the classification, with what confidence, against which rejected alternatives—and later revise it without rewriting the memory episode.\n\nI agree: R17‴ is the natural place, and Zaif exposes exactly what it should contain.\n\nOne correction first: after tracing the older material, I did find a partially live authoring route:\n\n`cascade library-gap → authoring → authored seed → pattern library`\n\nThe later slush specification expresses it as:\n\n`policy-hole → candidate-pattern/open → uniform prior → witnessed use`\n\nSo the War Machine can ingest new patterns, but the route is sparse, partly manual, and never became a coherent catalogue pattern. That is what R17‴ should repair.\n\nThe three supplements would then have a clean division:\n\n- R17′ — keep unresolved structural hypotheses explorable. Posterior spread is an honest uncertainty bonus, not EIG.\n- R17″ — generate possible reductions from embedding nearness. “These existing concepts might be mergeable.”\n- R17‴ — generate expansions from evidence-bearing holes. “The existing vocabulary cannot adequately describe this recurring situation.”\n\nA draft pattern would be:\n\n> Pattern Genesis from Evidence-Bearing Holes (R17‴).  \n> If independently recorded episodes, failed retrievals, or policy-holes repeatedly expose a situation that no existing pattern adequately handles; however cascades can only compose existing patterns, embeddings supply similarity rather than justification, and BMR can only compare or reduce represented structure; then bundle the triggering circumstances, memories, rejected near-matches, counterexamples, and a falsifiable predicted outcome into a candidate pattern, admit it to R6 at an explicitly unearned prior, and require repeated witnessed enactment-and-review before promotion. Because structure learning requires expansion of the model’s action vocabulary as well as refinement and reduction of what it already contains.\n\nR18 would audit this claim. R17‴ would probably receive an amber or gray faithfulness classification: it is a principled structure-hypothesis generator, but not itself BMR or a canonical EFE quantity.\n\nWhat Zaif contributes\n\nThe War Machine route begins with relatively coarse policy-holes. Zaif can supply a much denser evidential substrate:\n\n```text\ntyped memories\n    ↓\nLibrarian comparison with existing patterns\n    ├── attach as an instance\n    ├── challenge or refine an existing pattern\n    ├── retain as a reference only\n    └── mint a candidate pattern\n                 ↓\n         pattern-conditioned recall\n                 ↓\n         cited use in a later task\n                 ↓\n         independently witnessed outcome\n                 ↓\n       promote / revise / merge / prune\n```\n\nThat four-way Librarian decision is crucial. Memory accumulation must not turn automatically into pattern proliferation.\n\nA revealing issue in zai-3’s notes\n\nZai-3 maps three a94A06 memories to `math-formalization/tactic-algebra-interference`. But the actual pattern is specifically about `rw`, `simp_rw`, and `convert` interfering with `Tendsto` or binder structure.\n\nThe `field_simp` memory is related only at a much broader level. There is an existing `field-simp-reciprocal` pattern, but that covers proving denominators nonzero—not the observed problem where the hypothesis and denominator are mathematically equal but syntactically differently normalized.\n\nTherefore the honest classification may be:\n\n- `tactic-algebra-interference`: a nearby pattern, but not a clean instantiation.\n- `field-simp-reciprocal`: another near-match, still incomplete.\n- The repeated lesson “normalize the denominator into the hypothesis’s syntactic form before `field_simp`” may justify a new candidate such as `math-formalization/normalize-before-field-simp`.\n\nThat is almost a perfect R17‴ worked example: memories reveal that nearest-neighbour attachment would conceal a meaningful structural distinction.\n\nOther paper examples\n\nA short examples box could include:\n\n- `structure/two-projections-of-one-quantity`: positive precedent. It was cited before it existed, the missing pattern was caught, then it was minted and later discharged its originating hole.\n- `process-coherence/status-refresh-before-work`: learned from the recurring failure of agents and selectors acting on stale mission banners.\n- `process-coherence/is-this-even-a-problem`: learned after 674 expensive ticks worked questions whose answers were already present in primary documents.\n- `normalize-before-field-simp`: prospective Zaif-derived candidate, grounded in typed proof episodes.\n- `polyrith unavailable`: negative example. This is a volatile environment fact, not a reusable pattern; retain it as reference memory.\n\nThese illustrate why new patterns are useful: they add a previously missing action to the repertoire. The two process-coherence patterns make “read and re-derive current reality first” eligible as the first box in future cascades. A field-simplification pattern would make a previously private Lean workaround retrievable across proofs.\n\nOne architectural adjustment to zai-3’s proposal follows from this. The system should not simply attach every memory to the best-scoring existing pattern. It needs an explicit `no-adequate-pattern` result. Otherwise retrieval similarity will absorb every novel episode into the nearest old category, and R17‴ will never fire.\n\nI would also model memory–pattern attachment as its own provenance-bearing, revisable claim rather than silently mutating the original `:memory/assert` edge. That lets the Librarian say who proposed the classification, with what confidence, against which rejected alternatives—and later revise it without rewriting the memory episode.\n\nTOOL DIGEST\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_0qJbJ6qez85tz8PS0WFppWbj\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"git status --short -- holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md holes/CODEX-HANDOFF-p0-memory-record.md; sed -n '1,48p' holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md; sed -n '89…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_0qJbJ6qez85tz8PS0WFppWbj\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.3 seconds Output: ?? holes/CODEX-HANDOFF-p0-memory-record.md ?? holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md # M-typed-memories — memories as typed dialogue acts over the futon1b hy…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_UVMX4ZnzpvG1XYMQsE0Zx6q9\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"sed -n '1,270p' holes/notes/retrieval-strategy-pattern-conditioned-recall.md\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/futon3c\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":10000,\\\"max_output_tokens\\\":2200…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_UVMX4ZnzpvG1XYMQsE0Zx6q9\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.2 seconds Output: # Retrieval Strategy: Pattern-Conditioned Memory Recall Date: 2026-07-23 Author: zai-3 (at Joe's direction) Status: **Strategy proposal for review** ## T…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_y0DrGmCvAy29Z7mPqQiCziti\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"rg --files library | rg '(two-projections-of-one-quantity|status-refresh-before-work|is-this-even-a-problem|tactic-algebra-interference)'\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_y0DrGmCvAy29Z7mPqQiCziti\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.2 seconds Output: library/structure/two-projections-of-one-quantity.flexiarg library/math-formalization/tactic-algebra-interference.flexiarg library/process-coherence/stat…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_1fiW5EFMiCllGL3ZknAfGelj\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"for f in library/structure/two-projections-of-one-quantity.flexiarg library/process-coherence/status-refresh-before-work.flexiarg library/process-coherence/is-…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_1fiW5EFMiCllGL3ZknAfGelj\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.2\n[TRUNCATED AT 16KB BODY CAP]"}, :session-id "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :tags [:codex :turn-round :codex-rollout :codex-4], :at "2026-07-23T01:38:34.371Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :agent, :id "codex-4"}, :author "codex-4", :claim-type :step, :id "e-codexroll-019f8b63-t009"} #:evidence{:body {:session-uuid "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :turn-id "019f8cb7-1527-7400-922d-d6744bf15753", :event-seq-range [635 663], :reasoning-encrypted-only-count 6, :tool-digest-count 6, :rollout-file "/home/joe/.codex/sessions/2026/07/22/rollout-2026-07-22T20-53-09-019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822.jsonl", :turn 10, :event :turn-round, :truncated? false, :message-count 4, :harvester-version 1, :reasoning-count 6, :profile :codex-rollout, :text "Codex rollout turn 10\n\nREASONING (plaintext available in rollout)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nYes—the retract should not be “design patterns are canonical AIF objects.” It should be: a pattern occupies the implementation role of a policy primitive, and R17‴ addresses expansion of the policy/control-state alphabet when the current generative model cannot express an adequate policy. I’m checking the primary AIF literature for the most exact theoretical home—policy priors, model expansion, and structure learning—so we don’t overstate what standard AIF actually supplies.\n\nYes—the retract should not be “design patterns are canonical AIF objects.” It should be: a pattern occupies the implementation role of a policy primitive, and R17‴ addresses expansion of the policy/control-state alphabet when the current generative model cannot express an adequate policy. I’m checking the primary AIF literature for the most exact theoretical home—policy priors, model expansion, and structure learning—so we don’t overstate what standard AIF actually supplies.\n\nYes. The theoretical retract is stronger than “AIF has structure learning somewhere.” R17‴ retracts to expansion of the generative model’s control-state and policy space.\n\nThe crucial distinction is:\n\n- Cascades create new combinations within a fixed action alphabet.\n- Pattern minting expands the alphabet itself.\n\nIn standard discrete AIF, \\(U\\) is the set of actions or “control states,” while each policy \\(\\pi\\) is an allowable sequence of those actions. The \\(E\\) vector assigns a prior to each already-allowable policy. Standard policy inference therefore selects and learns confidence over a supplied repertoire; it does not ordinarily synthesize a new elementary control state. [This AIF tutorial](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8956124/) explicitly distinguishes control states \\(U\\), allowable policy sequences \\(V\\), and the learned prior \\(E\\).\n\nOur correspondence is therefore approximately:\n\n\\[\n\\text{pattern }p \\longleftrightarrow \\text{elementary or temporally extended control }u\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{cascade }(p_1,\\ldots,p_n) \\longleftrightarrow \\text{policy }\\pi\n\\]\n\nR17‴ changes the model from:\n\n\\[\nU' = U \\cup \\{u^\\ast\\}\n\\]\n\nand must also supply beliefs about the new control’s consequences:\n\n\\[\nB' = B \\cup \\{B^{u^\\ast}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThat expands the induced policy space:\n\n\\[\n\\Pi_H(U)\\subset\\Pi_H(U').\n\\]\n\nThis is why a candidate pattern needs more than attractive prose. Until it states a predicted transition or outcome, the forward model cannot imagine its consequences and R5 cannot score policies containing it. The pattern’s THEN clause, expected result, and falsifier are the beginnings of \\(B^{u^\\ast}\\); the fold turns that qualitative transition claim into a concrete construction and forecast.\n\nThe AIF theoretical home\n\nThe closest established home is structure learning by model expansion and subsequent model comparison. Active-inference concept-learning work explicitly expands a model’s state space to accommodate novel causes, after which evidence determines whether the added structure is useful. [Smith et al.’s structure-learning model](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7250191/) is an example, and [Neacsu’s thesis](https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10192997/) distinguishes active inference, parametric learning, and structure learning as separate levels.\n\nOur move from new hidden states to new control states is an implementation-level extension, but the retract is clear:\n\n```text\nImplementation                         AIF theory\n\npolicy-hole / unmatched memories   →   poor evidence under current model\ncandidate pattern p*               →   proposed expanded model m*\npredicted use and outcome          →   new transition hypothesis B^{u*}\nuniform/unearned admission         →   broad prior over expanded alternative\ntrial in a cascade                 →   epistemic policy testing m versus m*\nexternal outcome                   →   evidence under competing structures\npromote / merge / prune            →   model selection / structure learning\n```\n\nThe precise residual—the reason for gray—is candidate generation.\n\nAIF gives us a principled account of:\n\n- representing an expanded candidate model;\n- selecting actions that discriminate between model structures;\n- accumulating evidence;\n- retaining or reducing structure.\n\nIt does not give us a canonical operator that writes a human-legible IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE pattern from memories and policy-holes. That proposal operator is ours: Librarian analysis, LLM authoring, clustering, and pattern-language discipline.\n\nSo the retract-table row could read:\n\n| Mechanism | AIF role | Residual |\n|---|---|---|\n| R17‴ candidate-pattern mint | Generative-model expansion over control states, followed by active model selection | AIF evaluates supplied structural alternatives but does not specify how to synthesize a new human-legible control schema from episodic evidence |\n\nThere is another subtle residual around \\(E\\). In textbook AIF, \\(E\\) has one entry per complete allowable policy, not one entry per elementary action. When we say a new pattern enters “at the uniform prior,” we are really assigning an engineering prior to the pattern and using it to induce priors over newly available cascades. We should not pretend this is literally appending one cell to canonical \\(E\\) unless the cascade set is explicitly enumerated.\n\nR17‴ in the loop\n\nThe complete slow loop would be:\n\n```text\nR6/R13: search policies over the current pattern alphabet\n                       ↓\n          policy-hole or persistent misfit\n                       ↓\nR17‴: propose an expanded control alphabet\n                       ↓\n      candidate pattern with predicted consequences\n                       ↓\nR6/R13: construct discriminating cascades containing it\n                       ↓\nR16: obtain independently witnessed outcomes\n                       ↓\nR3/R7: update beliefs and precision\n                       ↓\nR17: compare, retain, merge, or prune model structure\n```\n\nR15 provides the timescale separation: the fast solver acts within the current vocabulary, while the slow Librarian changes that vocabulary.\n\nAnd the self-evidencing point is lovely but needs the R9 caveat: our inability to express the minting move was genuine evidence that the theoretical vocabulary was incomplete. It warrants proposing R17‴. It does not, by itself, certify that our proposed mechanism is good. R17‴ earns pattern status through the same future enactment-and-review cycle it prescribes.\n\nSo I would classify it as gray but with a very definite retract:\n\n> R17‴ is the War Machine’s engineering realization of active-inference model expansion at the policy/control-state boundary. Its non-canonical residue is the synthesis of the candidate itself.\n\nYes. The theoretical retract is stronger than “AIF has structure learning somewhere.” R17‴ retracts to expansion of the generative model’s control-state and policy space.\n\nThe crucial distinction is:\n\n- Cascades create new combinations within a fixed action alphabet.\n- Pattern minting expands the alphabet itself.\n\nIn standard discrete AIF, \\(U\\) is the set of actions or “control states,” while each policy \\(\\pi\\) is an allowable sequence of those actions. The \\(E\\) vector assigns a prior to each already-allowable policy. Standard policy inference therefore selects and learns confidence over a supplied repertoire; it does not ordinarily synthesize a new elementary control state. [This AIF tutorial](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8956124/) explicitly distinguishes control states \\(U\\), allowable policy sequences \\(V\\), and the learned prior \\(E\\).\n\nOur correspondence is therefore approximately:\n\n\\[\n\\text{pattern }p \\longleftrightarrow \\text{elementary or temporally extended control }u\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{cascade }(p_1,\\ldots,p_n) \\longleftrightarrow \\text{policy }\\pi\n\\]\n\nR17‴ changes the model from:\n\n\\[\nU' = U \\cup \\{u^\\ast\\}\n\\]\n\nand must also supply beliefs about the new control’s consequences:\n\n\\[\nB' = B \\cup \\{B^{u^\\ast}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThat expands the induced policy space:\n\n\\[\n\\Pi_H(U)\\subset\\Pi_H(U').\n\\]\n\nThis is why a candidate pattern needs more than attractive prose. Until it states a predicted transition or outcome, the forward model cannot imagine its consequences and R5 cannot score policies containing it. The pattern’s THEN clause, expected result, and falsifier are the beginnings of \\(B^{u^\\ast}\\); the fold turns that qualitative transition claim into a concrete construction and forecast.\n\nThe AIF theoretical home\n\nThe closest established home is structure learning by model expansion and subsequent model comparison. Active-inference concept-learning work explicitly expands a model’s state space to accommodate novel causes, after which evidence determines whether the added structure is useful. [Smith et al.’s structure-learning model](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7250191/) is an example, and [Neacsu’s thesis](https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10192997/) distinguishes active inference, parametric learning, and structure learning as separate levels.\n\nOur move from new hidden states to new control states is an implementation-level extension, but the retract is clear:\n\n```text\nImplementation                         AIF theory\n\npolicy-hole / unmatched memories   →   poor evidence under current model\ncandidate pattern p*               →   proposed expanded model m*\npredicted use and outcome          →   new transition hypothesis B^{u*}\nuniform/unearned admission         →   broad prior over expanded alternative\ntrial in a cascade                 →   epistemic policy testing m versus m*\nexternal outcome                   →   evidence under competing structures\npromote / merge / prune            →   model selection / structure learning\n```\n\nThe precise residual—the reason for gray—is candidate generation.\n\nAIF gives us a principled account of:\n\n- representing an expanded candidate model;\n- selecting actions that discriminate between model structures;\n- accumulating evidence;\n- retaining or reducing structure.\n\nIt does not give us a canonical operator that writes a human-legible IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE pattern from memories and policy-holes. That proposal operator is ours: Librarian analysis, LLM authoring, clustering, and pattern-language discipline.\n\nSo the retract-table row could read:\n\n| Mechanism | AIF role | Residual |\n|---|---|---|\n| R17‴ candidate-pattern mint | Generative-model expansion over control states, followed by active model selection | AIF evaluates supplied structural alternatives but does not specify how to synthesize a new human-legible control schema from episodic evidence |\n\nThere is another subtle residual around \\(E\\). In textbook AIF, \\(E\\) has one entry per complete allowable policy, not one entry per elementary action. When we say a new pattern enters “at the uniform prior,” we are really assigning an engineering prior to the pattern and using it to induce priors over newly available cascades. We should not pretend this is literally appending one cell to canonical \\(E\\) unless the cascade set is explicitly enumerated.\n\nR17‴ in the loop\n\nThe complete slow loop would be:\n\n```text\nR6/R13: search policies over the current pattern alphabet\n                       ↓\n          policy-hole or persistent misfit\n                       ↓\nR17‴: propose an expanded control alphabet\n                       ↓\n      candidate pattern with predicted consequences\n                       ↓\nR6/R13: construct discriminating cascades containing it\n                       ↓\nR16: obtain independently witnessed outcomes\n                       ↓\nR3/R7: update beliefs and precision\n                       ↓\nR17: compare, retain, merge, or prune model structure\n```\n\nR15 provides the timescale separation: the fast solver acts within the current vocabulary, while the slow Librarian changes that vocabulary.\n\nAnd the self-evidencing point is lovely but needs the R9 caveat: our inability to express the minting move was genuine evidence that the theoretical vocabulary was incomplete. It warrants proposing R17‴. It does not, by itself, certify that our proposed mechanism is good. R17‴ earns pattern status through the same future enactment-and-review cycle it prescribes.\n\nSo I would classify it as gray but with a very definite retract:\n\n> R17‴ is the War Machine’s engineering realization of active-inference model expansion at the policy/control-state boundary. Its non-canonical residue is the synthesis of the candidate itself.\n\nTOOL DIGEST\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_iH9fk3WcCaLsZ7UZ4DSkY3kW\", :args \"const r = await tools.web__run({search_query:[ {q:\\\"site:pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov active inference structure learning novel policies model expansion Bayesian model reduction\\\"}, {q:\\\"active inference policy …\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_iH9fk3WcCaLsZ7UZ4DSkY3kW\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 2.1 seconds Output: Structure Learning and learning structure with Active Inference - UCL Discovery (https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10192997/) citeturn0search0 [wo…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_mgvX1m29otw9LSkJPi6PaHeQ\", :args \"const r = await tools.web__run({open:[ {ref_id:\\\"turn0search1\\\"}, {ref_id:\\\"turn0search5\\\"}, {ref_id:\\\"turn0search8\\\"}, {ref_id:\\\"turn0search0\\\"} ],response_length:\\\"long\\\"}); text(r); \"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_mgvX1m29otw9LSkJPi6PaHeQ\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 1.0 seconds Output: Checking your browser - reCAPTCHA (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7732703/) citeturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Content type: text/html; Source: o…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_xMddanqwiWEK5n47OkTVyT3T\", :args \"const r = await tools.web__run({find:[ {ref_id:\\\"turn1view2\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"E vector\\\"}, {ref_id:\\\"turn1view2\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"habit\\\"}, {ref_id:\\\"turn1