e habit: mission selection needs its own generative vocabulary and evidence model, while `strategy × central × feasible` is better treated as a factorized mission proposal/value model than quietly relabelled \\(E\\). I’ll locate the deferred mission and trace those factors through the live scorer, then extend the compliance mission with an explicit mission-pattern layer before advancing persistence.\n\nYes—this changes the architecture in an important way. The scheduler prior should not simply become a second copy of cascade habit: mission selection needs its own generative vocabulary and evidence model, while `strategy × central × feasible` is better treated as a factorized mission proposal/value model than quietly relabelled \\(E\\). I’ll locate the deferred mission and trace those factors through the live scorer, then extend the compliance mission with an explicit mission-pattern layer before advancing persistence.\n\nThe code settles one historical ambiguity: the live mission model is currently an additive weighted blend, not a product. The handoff explicitly rejected multiplication because strategy-spine missions can have zero measured centrality, so a raw product erases them. But your larger point stands: additive rescue is still an engineering utility, and it does not tell us what a mission is as a strategic policy.\n\nThe principled split I’m developing is: typed p4ng control-pattern relations generate and organize the strategic mission landscape; a mission-specific evidence/feasibility model scores it; \\(E_S\\) records strategic habit only. A dedicated embedding can help propose relations, but typed hypergraph links and retrieved memories must supply the auditable support.\n\nThe code settles one historical ambiguity: the live mission model is currently an additive weighted blend, not a product. The handoff explicitly rejected multiplication because strategy-spine missions can have zero measured centrality, so a raw product erases them. But your larger point stands: additive rescue is still an engineering utility, and it does not tell us what a mission is as a strategic policy.\n\nThe principled split I’m developing is: typed p4ng control-pattern relations generate and organize the strategic mission landscape; a mission-specific evidence/feasibility model scores it; \\(E_S\\) records strategic habit only. A dedicated embedding can help propose relations, but typed hypergraph links and retrieved memories must supply the auditable support.\n\nThere is also a useful correction to preserve in the record: the recent three-factor handoff explicitly chose an additive blend because raw multiplication annihilated strategically important zero-centrality missions. So the next rigorous model should not revive the old product numerically. It should recover the product-of-evidence interpretation only after separating:\n\n- hard executability from predicted success,\n- structural centrality from preference,\n- strategy-pattern fit from habit \\(E_S\\),\n- unknown evidence from negative evidence.\n\nI’m encoding that separation now as a typed, dark mission↔control-pattern↔memory graph contract and a dedicated strategic-selection mission.\n\nThere is also a useful correction to preserve in the record: the recent three-factor handoff explicitly chose an additive blend because raw multiplication annihilated strategically important zero-centrality missions. So the next rigorous model should not revive the old product numerically. It should recover the product-of-evidence interpretation only after separating:\n\n- hard executability from predicted success,\n- structural centrality from preference,\n- strategy-pattern fit from habit \\(E_S\\),\n- unknown evidence from negative evidence.\n\nI’m encoding that separation now as a typed, dark mission↔control-pattern↔memory graph contract and a dedicated strategic-selection mission.\n\nThe new strategic graph test immediately caught a missing closing parenthesis in the test namespace. The production kernel has not run yet; I’m fixing the test form, then I’ll rerun the focused gates before touching the paper narrative.\n\nThe new strategic graph test immediately caught a missing closing parenthesis in the test namespace. The production kernel has not run yet; I’m fixing the test form, then I’ll rerun the focused gates before touching the paper narrative.\n\nThe strategic kernel is now green: only witnessed typed relations can admit missions; proposed embedding edges remain audit-only; attached memories travel as warrants rather than weights; and witnessed blockers remove support instead of multiplying a score by zero. I’m now adding the corresponding distinction to the paper so the reference story says explicitly what the current additive model is and what the pattern-organized strategic model would have to become.\n\nThe strategic kernel is now green: only witnessed typed relations can admit missions; proposed embedding edges remain audit-only; attached memories travel as warrants rather than weights; and witnessed blockers remove support instead of multiplying a score by zero. I’m now adding the corresponding distinction to the paper so the reference story says explicitly what the current additive model is and what the pattern-organized strategic model would have to become.\n\nTOOL DIGEST\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_5KwKEPqgEhMLs9oXyKEZrFHB\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"rg -n -i \\\\\\\"strategy.*central.*feasib|strategic.*central.*doable|multiplicative|mission-value-factor|deferred\\\\\\\" holes src scripts test | head -n 300\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_5KwKEPqgEhMLs9oXyKEZrFHB\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.2 seconds Output: Warning: truncated output (original token count: 11360) Total output lines: 234 src/futon2/aif/a4a_substrate.clj:225: :slush/model-uncertainty-steering :…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_2