 emerging architecture is:\n\n1. A strategic policy selects a mission or short mission cascade.\n2. Typed p4ng control patterns organize the mission landscape.\n3. Hypergraph memories provide warrants, counterexamples, and retrieval handles.\n4. A dedicated mission-plus-control-pattern embedding proposes possible relations.\n5. Witnessed typed edges determine admission; similarity alone does not.\n6. \\(E_S\\) records selection habit separately from strategic merit and feasibility.\n7. Tactical cascade selection then occurs conditionally within the chosen strategic policy.\n\nThis gives us a principled way to exploit the second embedding space you suggested without asking semantic geometry to do evidential work it cannot do.\n\nThe paper builds successfully, and the focused implementation suite passes 48 tests / 177 assertions. Everything remains dark/shadow-only: live mission scheduling has not been changed. The next planned slice is a small gold corpus of mission↔p4ng↔memory relations, followed by comparing lexical, general-embedding, and dedicated-embedding proposal generators against it.\n\nYes—the mission level needs its own policy grain, not a larger version of cascade scoring.\n\nOne important correction from inspecting the live system: its current mission score is additive, not multiplicative:\n\n`0.25 × central + 0.45 × strategic + 0.30 × phase-doable`\n\nThe multiplicative version was deferred because a zero centrality measurement could erase strong strategic evidence. But neither formula is yet a principled generative model or a true \\(E_S\\).\n\nI have now:\n\n- Added the strategic-policy account to [main-2026.tex](/home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex:510), distinguishing proposal support, predicted outcomes/\\(G_S\\), exclusions, and the mission-level habit \\(E_S\\).\n- Written the implementation plan in [M-wm-strategic-mission-selection.md](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/missions/M-wm-strategic-mission-selection.md:10).\n- Added a dark, reason-bearing miss