recorded decision rather than asserting its own input; (b) deleting the :yield-baseline marker comment gave exit 1 with exactly 1 failure, at :62, reporting the missing :yield-baseline adjacency -- the doseq regex really enforces IMMEDIATE adjacency, not mere presence of the marker somewhere. LIVE PIN holds and no SKIP guard is owed: the test reads futon2 holes/labs/zaif-harness/runs/D9-tie-order-count.edn, which git ls-files --error-unmatch confirms is TRACKED; its [:live :live-pin] carries :id e-0f2f9aec-6240-40e9-a25a-e45d9452076f, exactly the record cited in the test comment, and that pin's :decision is the retrieve arm with :g-terms {:retrieve 0.7456643332946383, :act 0.0, :ask -0.35000000000000003, :yield 0.0} -- constants-dependent numbers, which is why mutation (a) bites. The source side is read via io/resource, so it follows the classpath rather than cwd and survives a sibling worktree (it did here). ENUMERATION: the row's -- all seven arm constants -- is exhaustive, and I checked it against the closed form rather than a hand-list. Untruncated command: git show b1391237:src/futon3c/agents/zaif_controller.clj | sed -n '/^(def constants/,/^$/p' | grep -cE '^ +:[a-z-]+ [0-9]' -> 7, and the test's doseq names all 7 tokens. Line pointers resolve at the sha: src :14-27 is the typed map, :98-104 the typed ask payoff, test :47-70 the deftest. COMMITS path-scoped (b1391237 two files under futon3c src+test agents; 2f3004f one file under holes/labs/wm-contract; a70f272 worklist.edn alone). Author date == committer date on all three, so no --amend; b1391237 sits on another lane's d45e0dcf (Validate authenticated submissions as terminal) and is an ancestor of futon3c master; 2f3004f is an ancestor of futon2 main. MECHANISM MATCH to wm U30 729f3fdb: accepted. Both zaif sites take exemplar 2 (wm_regulator_sweep.clj:88-93, a structured marker comment immediately above) rather than exemplar 1's var metadata, and that is the right branch -- the arm constants are map-literal entries, which cannot carry per-entry var metadata, and claude-1's binding assigns the zaif arm constants case to branch 2 by name. One judgment I checked instead of waving through: the ask payoff could have argued for exemplar 1, since ask-value IS a defn and U30 attached var metadata to the defn roi-map-for-missions; the comment is still the better match, because what is typed here is a computed affine combination inside a function body, which is exemplar 2's shape line-for-line, and the checker's ask-value token finds it either way. THREE HONEST LIMITS, none blocking and none introduced by this row. (1) scalar_typing_check.bb enforces substring-anywhere, not adjacency: its :when only asks that :scalar-awaiting-density and the token each appear SOMEWHERE in the file, so the .bb alone would still pass if a marker were moved away from its scalar. Adjacency is enforced by the futon3c test instead, which I mutation-proved above. This is inherited from U30's shape, and tightening it would change the WM half's semantics too, so it is wm-contract's call rather than a unilateral edit from this board. (2) The .bb slurps futon3c's WORKING TREE at a hardcoded (System/getProperty user.home)/code path, so it validates whatever revision futon3c happens to be checked out at, not b1391237; the test likewise reads futon2's working tree via workspace-root and throws rather than SKIPs if futon2 is absent -- the same caveat U18z and U19z recorded, now present in a fourth namespace. Also note nothing runs the .bb automatically: grep over the repo finds no CI or gate invoking scalar_typing_check, so it is a hand-run tally artifact. (3) The tally row moves to :repaired clause in the :unblocked binding is not yet observable anywhere in futon2: there is no futon2-side tally data file carrying a status field (grep -rn for cost-vs-distribution over the repo returns 71 hits, all prose, markers, worklists or the build log -- the tally row itself lives in the p4ng paper), the .bb is the only artifact, and wm-contract U30, the WM half, is itself still :done-unreviewed. This row's own :evidence does not overclaim it: it says the .bb moves the two zaif sites from pending into enforcement, which is exactly what I reproduced. No reviewer fix was needed."
